      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   GIBBS-SAMPLER

      *>> datamunge::bayes::GibbsSampler::GibbsSampler(GibbsOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> GIBBS-SAMPLER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_gibbs_sampler_new" USING DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::bayes::GibbsSampler. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Metropolis-within-Gibbs: updates one coordinate at a time,
      *>>   each via a scalar
      *>> random-walk Metropolis step evaluated against the SAME joint
      *>>   log-density with every
      *>> other coordinate held fixed -- mathematically equivalent to a
      *>>   Metropolis step
      *>> targeting the exact full conditional p(x_i | x_-i), since the
      *>>   joint-density ratio
      *>> with everything else held fixed IS the
      *>>   full-conditional-density ratio. This is the
      *>> standard fallback used whenever a full conditional isn't
      *>>   conjugate/directly
      *>> sampleable (what BUGS/JAGS call "Metropolis-within-Gibbs"),
      *>>   not exact Gibbs sampling
      *>> via closed-form conditional samplers -- those are necessarily
      *>>   model-specific (they
      *>> need to know each conditional's actual distributional family)
      *>>   and aren't something
      *>> a generic engine driven only by a joint log-density can
      *>>   provide. Updating one
      *>> coordinate at a time (rather than proposing the whole vector
      *>>   jointly, as
      *>> RandomWalkMetropolis does) can mix substantially better when
      *>>   parameters live on very
      *>> different scales or are only weakly correlated.
      *>>
      *>> Stores the sampler settings.
       01 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              0.
      *>> options (GibbsOptions = {}): GIBBS-OPTIONS handle. Sampler
      *>>   settings.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: GIBBS-SAMPLER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::bayes::GibbsSampler::sample(optim::ArbitraryFunction
      *>>   & log_posterior, const std::vector<double> & initial_params)
      *>>   const -> GibbsResult
      *>> CALL "dm_gibbs_sampler_sample" USING DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Runs warmup sweeps (adapting each coordinate's step), then
      *>>   num_samples sweeps.
       01 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE.
      *>> Self: the GIBBS-SAMPLER handle.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> log_posterior (optim::ArbitraryFunction &): ARBITRARY-FUNCTION
      *>>   handle. Log density up to a constant; only evaluate() is
      *>>   used.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE-LOG-POSTERIOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> initial_params (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle. Starting point.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE-INITIAL-PARAMS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (GibbsResult): GIBBS-RESULT handle (new; close it).
      *>>   Draws, per-coordinate acceptance rates and step sizes.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::bayes::GibbsSampler::options() const -> const
      *>>   GibbsOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_gibbs_sampler_options" USING DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-OPTIONS
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The options this GibbsSampler was configured with.
       01 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the GIBBS-SAMPLER handle.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const GibbsOptions &): GIBBS-OPTIONS handle (new;
      *>>   refers into its owner, which it keeps alive). The stored
      *>>   options.
          05 DM-GIBBS-SAMPLER-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

