      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   KALMAN-FILTER

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::KalmanFilter(std::vector<std::vector<double>>
      *>>   F, std::vector<std::vector<double>> H,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> Q,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> R, std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> P0) -> KALMAN-FILTER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_new" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> The standard discrete-time linear Kalman filter: x_k = F
      *>>   x_{k-1} + w_k, z_k = H x_k
      *>> + v_k, w_k ~ N(0, Q), v_k ~ N(0, R) -- the
      *>>   minimum-mean-squared-error linear
      *>>   estimator for exactly this model. Stateful: holds the
      *>>     current (x, P) estimate,
      *>>   mutated in place by predict()/update(); F, H, Q, R are fixed
      *>>     for the filter's
      *>>   lifetime (construct a new filter if they change over time).
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW.
      *>> F (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-F USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> H (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-H USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Q (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-Q USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> R (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-R USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> P0 (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-P0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: KALMAN-FILTER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::predict() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_predict" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The time-update (prediction) step: x = F x, P = F P F^T + Q.
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::update(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & z) -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_update" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The measurement-update (correction) step given observation
      *>>   `z`: the standard
      *>> innovation-covariance / Kalman-gain form (S = H P H^T + R, K =
      *>>   P H^T S^-1, x +=
      *>> K(z - Hx), P = (I - KH) P) -- not the numerically-hardened
      *>>   Joseph form, matching
      *>> this codebase's general preference for the textbook formula
      *>>   over a more robust
      *>> but more complex alternative.
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> z (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE-Z USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::state() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_state" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::covariance() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>>
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_covariance" USING
      *>>   DM-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE DM-STATUS
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<std::vector<double>>):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::filter(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & measurements) ->
      *>>   std::vector<KalmanState>
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_filter" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Runs predict() then update(z) for every z in `measurements`,
      *>>   in order, mutating
      *>> this filter's running state -- the ordinary causal (online)
      *>>   filtered estimate at
      *>> every timestep.
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> measurements (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-MEASUREMENTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<KalmanState>): VECTOR-KALMAN-STATE handle
      *>>   (new; close it).
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::KalmanFilter::smooth(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & measurements) ->
      *>>   SmoothResult
      *>> CALL "dm_kalman_filter_smooth" USING DM-KALMAN-FILTER-SMOOTH
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> filter() followed by an RTS (Rauch-Tung-Striebel) backward
      *>>   smoothing pass, which
      *>> uses the ENTIRE sequence (not just data up to each point) to
      *>>   refine every
      *>> estimate -- always at least as accurate as the filtered
      *>>   estimate, at the cost of
      *>> needing the full sequence in advance (not usable online).
      *>>   Leaves this filter's
      *>> running (x, P) at its post-filter state, same as filter()
      *>>   would.
       01 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-SMOOTH.
      *>> Self: the KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-SMOOTH-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> measurements (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-SMOOTH-MEASUREMENTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (SmoothResult): SMOOTH-RESULT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-KALMAN-FILTER-SMOOTH-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   VECTOR-FUNCTION

      *>> datamunge::filter::VectorFunction::VectorFunction() ->
      *>>   VECTOR-FUNCTION handle with COBOL overrides
      *>> CALL "dm_vector_function_extend" USING
      *>>   DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EXTEND DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::VectorFunction. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A COBOL subclass: each PROGRAM-POINTER names the COBOL program
      *>>   that overrides that virtual method. The program receives its
      *>>   -ARGS record (in the callbacks copybook) and the callback
      *>>   status.
      *>>
      *>> Must override: EVALUATE.
      *>>
      *>> A vector-valued function R^n -> R^m: the state-transition or
      *>>   observation model
      *>> supplied to this module's nonlinear filters
      *>>   (ExtendedKalmanFilter,
      *>> UnscentedKalmanFilter, EnsembleKalmanFilter, ParticleFilter).
      *>>   Director-enabled
      *>> (subclass directly in C++, or via SWIG directors in any bound
      *>>   language) so this
      *>> module never needs to know the concrete form of the caller's
      *>>   dynamics.
       01 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EXTEND.
      *>> Override of datamunge::filter::VectorFunction::evaluate(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x) -> std::vector<double>: SET it TO
      *>>   ENTRY "PROGRAM-ID", or leave it NULL (required: pure
      *>>   virtual).
          05 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EXTEND-EVALUATE USAGE PROGRAM-POINTER.
      *>> Result: VECTOR-FUNCTION handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EXTEND-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::VectorFunction::evaluate(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x) -> std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_vector_function_evaluate" USING
      *>>   DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EVALUATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Evaluates the function at `x`.
       01 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EVALUATE.
      *>> Self: the VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EVALUATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EVALUATE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-VECTOR-FUNCTION-EVALUATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::EnsembleKalmanFilter(VectorFunction
      *>>   & f, VectorFunction & h, std::vector<std::vector<double>> Q,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> R, std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> P0, int ensemble_size =
      *>>   100, unsigned int seed = 42) -> ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER
      *>>   handle
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_new" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> The (stochastic, "perturbed observations") Ensemble Kalman
      *>>   Filter: represents the
      *>> state distribution as a finite ensemble of sample state
      *>>   vectors rather than an
      *>> explicit mean/covariance, propagating each member through the
      *>>   true nonlinear f
      *>> (plus sampled process noise) and estimating the covariances
      *>>   the Kalman update needs
      *>> from the ensemble's own sample statistics. Like
      *>>   UnscentedKalmanFilter, needs no
      *>> Jacobian; unlike it, scales to very high-dimensional states
      *>>   (the classical use case
      *>> this filter was designed for -- e.g. geophysical data
      *>>   assimilation with millions of
      *>> state variables) since its cost scales with ensemble size, not
      *>>   state dimension
      *>> squared/cubed. Statistical, not exact: converges toward the
      *>>   optimal (linear-Gaussian
      *>> case: KalmanFilter's) estimate as `ensemble_size` grows, with
      *>>   Monte Carlo sampling
      *>> noise at any finite size.
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 6 to 8 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 6.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 6.
      *>> f (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-F USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> h (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-H USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Q (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-Q USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> R (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-R USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> P0 (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-P0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> ensemble_size (int = 100): int.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-ENSEMBLE-SIZE USAGE
              BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> seed (unsigned int = 42): unsigned int.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-SEED USAGE BINARY-LONG
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::predict() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_predict" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Propagates every ensemble member through the nonlinear f, each
      *>>   perturbed by an
      *>> independently-sampled draw from N(0, Q).
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT.
      *>> Self: the ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-PREDICT-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::update(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & z) -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_update" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The stochastic EnKF update (Evensen/Burgers): estimates the
      *>>   state-observation
      *>> cross-covariance and innovation covariance from the ensemble's
      *>>   own sample
      *>> statistics, then updates each member with an INDEPENDENTLY
      *>>   perturbed copy of `z`
      *>> (adding a fresh N(0, R) draw to each member's correction) --
      *>>   perturbing the
      *>> observation per member, rather than applying one shared
      *>>   correction to all of
      *>> them, is what keeps the ensemble's spread statistically
      *>>   consistent after the
      *>> update instead of collapsing it.
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE.
      *>> Self: the ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> z (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-UPDATE-Z USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::state() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_state" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE DM-STATUS
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE.
      *>> Self: the ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-STATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::covariance() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>>
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_covariance" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE DM-STATUS
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE.
      *>> Self: the ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE-SELF USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<std::vector<double>>):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-COVARIANCE-RESULT USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::EnsembleKalmanFilter::filter(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & measurements) ->
      *>>   std::vector<KalmanState>
      *>> CALL "dm_ensemble_kalman_filter_filter" USING
      *>>   DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER DM-STATUS
       01 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER.
      *>> Self: the ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> measurements (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-MEASUREMENTS USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<KalmanState>): VECTOR-KALMAN-STATE handle
      *>>   (new; close it).
          05 DM-ENSEMBLE-KALMAN-FILTER-FILTER-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER

      *>> datamunge::filter::ExtendedKalmanFilter::ExtendedKalmanFilter(VectorFunction
      *>>   & f, VectorFunction & h, std::vector<std::vector<double>> Q,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> R, std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> P0, double jacobian_step =
      *>>   1.0000000000000001E-5) -> EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_extended_kalman_filter_new" USING
      *>>   DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::ExtendedKalmanFilter. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> The Extended Kalman Filter: the standard first-order
      *>>   generalization of KalmanFilter
      *>> to a nonlinear model x_k = f(x_{k-1}) + w_k, z_k = h(x_k) +
      *>>   v_k -- linearizes f and h
      *>> (via numerical Jacobians, see detail::numerical_jacobian())
      *>>   about the current state
      *>> estimate at every step and otherwise runs the exact same
      *>>   predict/update algebra as
      *>> the linear filter. Exactly reproduces KalmanFilter's result
      *>>   when `f` and `h`
      *>> happen to be linear (their Jacobian is then the same constant
      *>>   matrix everywhere).
       01 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 6 to 7 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 6.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 6.
      *>> f (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-F USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> h (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-H USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Q (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-Q USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> R (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-R USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> P0 (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-P0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> jacobian_step (double = 1.0000000000000001E-5): double.
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-JACOBIAN-STEP USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result: EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-EXTENDED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ExtendedKalmanFilter::predict() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_extended_kalman_filter_predict" USING
