      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   PARTICLE-FILTER

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::ParticleFilter(VectorFunction
      *>>   & f, VectorFunction & h, std::vector<std::vector<double>> Q,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> R, std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> P0, int num_particles =
      *>>   200, unsigned int seed = 42) -> PARTICLE-FILTER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_new" USING DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A bootstrap particle filter (Sequential Importance
      *>>   Resampling): the fully general,
      *>> non-Gaussian generalization of the Kalman family -- represents
      *>>   the state's entire
      *>> posterior distribution as a weighted swarm of sample points
      *>>   rather than assuming any
      *>> particular family of distribution (Gaussian, etc.), so it
      *>>   remains correct even for
      *>> strongly non-Gaussian or multimodal posteriors where every
      *>>   other filter in this
      *>> module (which all assume approximately-Gaussian beliefs) can
      *>>   fail. Costs scale with
      *>> particle count and can need many particles in high dimensions
      *>>   ("the curse of
      *>> dimensionality" for particle filters) -- EnsembleKalmanFilter
      *>>   is usually preferred
      *>> for high-dimensional problems where the Gaussian assumption is
      *>>   acceptable.
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 6 to 8 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 6.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              6.
      *>> f (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-F USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> h (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-H USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Q (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-Q USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> R (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-R USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> P0 (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-P0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> num_particles (int = 200): int.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-NUM-PARTICLES USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
      *>> seed (unsigned int = 42): unsigned int.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-SEED USAGE BINARY-LONG UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: PARTICLE-FILTER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::predict() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_predict" USING
      *>>   DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-PREDICT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Propagates every particle through the nonlinear f, each
      *>>   perturbed by an
      *>> independently-sampled draw from N(0, Q). Weights are untouched
      *>>   (the transition
      *>> density cancels out of the importance weight for this
      *>>   proposal, the standard
      *>> bootstrap-filter simplification).
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-PREDICT.
      *>> Self: the PARTICLE-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-PREDICT-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::update(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & z) -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_update" USING
      *>>   DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-UPDATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Reweights every particle by its Gaussian (N(h(x), R))
      *>>   observation likelihood
      *>> given `z`, normalizes the weights, then resamples the whole
      *>>   swarm from the
      *>> resulting weighted distribution (multinomial resampling, done
      *>>   unconditionally
      *>> every step -- simpler than the adaptive "resample only when
      *>>   the effective sample
      *>> size drops" scheme real-time systems often use, at the cost of
      *>>   extra Monte Carlo
      *>> noise from resampling more often than strictly necessary).
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-UPDATE.
      *>> Self: the PARTICLE-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-UPDATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> z (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-UPDATE-Z USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::state() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_state" USING DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-STATE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-STATE.
      *>> Self: the PARTICLE-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-STATE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-STATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::covariance() const ->
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>>
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_covariance" USING
      *>>   DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-COVARIANCE DM-STATUS
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-COVARIANCE.
      *>> Self: the PARTICLE-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-COVARIANCE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<std::vector<double>>):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-COVARIANCE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::ParticleFilter::filter(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & measurements) ->
      *>>   std::vector<KalmanState>
      *>> CALL "dm_particle_filter_filter" USING
      *>>   DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-FILTER DM-STATUS
       01 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-FILTER.
      *>> Self: the PARTICLE-FILTER handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-FILTER-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> measurements (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-FILTER-MEASUREMENTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<KalmanState>): VECTOR-KALMAN-STATE handle
      *>>   (new; close it).
          05 DM-PARTICLE-FILTER-FILTER-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER

      *>> datamunge::filter::UnscentedKalmanFilter::UnscentedKalmanFilter(VectorFunction
      *>>   & f, VectorFunction & h, std::vector<std::vector<double>> Q,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> R, std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> P0, double alpha = 0.001,
      *>>   double beta = 2.0, double kappa = 0.0) ->
      *>>   UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_unscented_kalman_filter_new" USING
      *>>   DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::filter::UnscentedKalmanFilter. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> The Unscented Kalman Filter: propagates a small deterministic
      *>>   set of "sigma points"
      *>> through the true nonlinear f/h (rather than linearizing f/h
      *>>   itself, as
      *>> ExtendedKalmanFilter does), then reconstructs the predicted
      *>>   mean/covariance from the
      *>> propagated points' weighted statistics. Captures nonlinearity
      *>>   to (at least) second
      *>> order without ever needing a Jacobian -- generally more
      *>>   accurate than EKF for
      *>> strongly nonlinear models, at the cost of 2n+1 function
      *>>   evaluations per step instead
      *>> of one. Exactly reproduces KalmanFilter's result when `f` and
      *>>   `h` are linear.
       01 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 6 to 9 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 6.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 6.
      *>> f (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-F USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> h (VectorFunction &): VECTOR-FUNCTION handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-H USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Q (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-Q USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> R (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-R USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> P0 (std::vector<std::vector<double>>): VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-P0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> alpha (double = 0.001): double.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-ALPHA USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> beta (double = 2.0): double.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-BETA USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> kappa (double = 0.0): double.
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-KAPPA USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result: UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-UNSCENTED-KALMAN-FILTER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::filter::UnscentedKalmanFilter::predict() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_unscented_kalman_filter_predict" USING
