      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   CALLBACK

      *>> datamunge::Callback::Callback() -> CALLBACK handle
      *>> CALL "dm_callback_new" USING DM-CALLBACK-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::Callback. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Base class for user-defined callbacks invoked by datamunge
      *>>   functions.
      *>>
      *>> Derive from this class and override `call()` to supply custom
      *>>   behavior
      *>> wherever a `Callback*` is accepted.
       01 DM-CALLBACK-NEW.
      *>> Result: CALLBACK handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-CALLBACK-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::Callback::Callback() -> CALLBACK handle with COBOL
      *>>   overrides
      *>> CALL "dm_callback_extend" USING DM-CALLBACK-EXTEND DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::Callback. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A COBOL subclass: each PROGRAM-POINTER names the COBOL program
      *>>   that overrides that virtual method. The program receives its
      *>>   -ARGS record (in the callbacks copybook) and the callback
      *>>   status.
      *>>
      *>> May override: CALL.
      *>>
      *>> Base class for user-defined callbacks invoked by datamunge
      *>>   functions.
      *>>
      *>> Derive from this class and override `call()` to supply custom
      *>>   behavior
      *>> wherever a `Callback*` is accepted.
       01 DM-CALLBACK-EXTEND.
      *>> Override of datamunge::Callback::call(double x) -> double: SET
      *>>   it TO ENTRY "PROGRAM-ID", or leave it NULL (keeps the C++
      *>>   method).
          05 DM-CALLBACK-EXTEND-CALL USAGE PROGRAM-POINTER.
      *>> Result: CALLBACK handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-CALLBACK-EXTEND-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::Callback::call(double x) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_callback_call" USING DM-CALLBACK-CALL DM-STATUS
      *>> CALL "dm_callback_call_base" USING DM-CALLBACK-CALL DM-STATUS
      *>>   (runs the C++ method, never a COBOL override)
      *>>
      *>> Applies the callback to a single value.
       01 DM-CALLBACK-CALL.
      *>> Self: the CALLBACK handle.
          05 DM-CALLBACK-CALL-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> x (double): double. Input value.
          05 DM-CALLBACK-CALL-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (double): double. Transformed value; the default
      *>>   implementation returns `x` unchanged.
          05 DM-CALLBACK-CALL-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   RGB

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RGB::RGB() -> RGB handle
      *>> CALL "dm_rgb_new" USING DM-RGB-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::graphics::RGB. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A plotting color: 8-bit red, green, and blue channels plus an
      *>>   alpha opacity,
      *>> the C++ counterpart of R's `rgb(r, g, b, alpha, maxColorValue
      *>>   = 255)`.
      *>>
      *>> Brace-initialize numeric channels: `dm::RGB{255, 0, 0}` is
      *>>   opaque red and
      *>> `dm::RGB{255, 0, 0, 0.5}` is half-transparent red. A
      *>>   default-constructed RGB is the
      *>> library's dark-slate ink `{31, 41, 55}`, fully opaque.
      *>>   Channels are not validated: keep
      *>> them in [0, 255] (both the raster and SVG renderers clamp
      *>>   out-of-range channels), and
      *>> keep alpha in [0, 1] (both renderers clamp it). Every plotting
      *>>   API takes RGBA (the same type as RGB) by value,
      *>> and `dm::c(...)` of several RGBs builds a palette usable with
      *>>   `select()` and set_col().
      *>> Color strings convert implicitly: names are case-insensitive
      *>>   black, white, red, green,
      *>> blue, yellow, cyan, magenta, gray/grey and transparent; hex
      *>>   accepts #RRGGBB and
      *>> #RRGGBBAA, with alpha last and scaled to [0, 1]. Malformed
      *>>   text throws before a
      *>> plot is changed. RGB and RGBA remain the same type, preserving
      *>>   existing palettes
      *>> and the binary layout of numeric colors. Parsing owns no
      *>>   reference to the string.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto gold = dm::RGB{255, 215, 0};
      *>> const auto glass = dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 0.3}; // translucent
      *>>   blue
      *>> const dm::RGB ink; // {31, 41, 55}, opaque
      *>> std::print("{} {} {}\n", gold.r, glass.alpha, ink.r); // 255
      *>>   0.3 31
      *>> const auto palette = dm::c(gold, glass); // a
      *>>   dm::Vector<dm::RGB>
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: dm::RGBA, dm::Plot::set_col(), dm::points()
      *>>
      *>> Construct the default opaque dark-slate ink color.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See the RGB class example.
       01 DM-RGB-NEW.
      *>> Result: RGB handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-RGB-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RGB::RGB(int red, int green, int blue,
