      *>> datamunge::graphics::Plot::view(const std::string & title_hint
      *>>   = "") const -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_plot_view" USING DM-PLOT-VIEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Same as show(); kept as an alias for older code.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::runtime_error: As for show().
      *>>
      *>> See also: show()
       01 DM-PLOT-VIEW.
      *>> Self: the PLOT handle.
          05 DM-PLOT-VIEW-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-PLOT-VIEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> title_hint (const std::string & = ""): text in PIC X(256);
      *>>   -LENGTH -1 trims trailing spaces. Window title; empty uses
      *>>   the plot's title.
          05 DM-PLOT-VIEW-TITLE-HINT PIC X(256).
          05 DM-PLOT-VIEW-TITLE-HINT-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE -1.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::Plot::show(const std::string & title_hint
      *>>   = "") const -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_plot_show" USING DM-PLOT-SHOW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Opens the plot in an interactive X11 window and blocks until
      *>>   it is closed
      *>> (like printing a plot in an interactive R session).
      *>>
      *>> The window has toolbar and keyboard zoom/pan (it re-renders
      *>>   from data bounds) and closes
      *>> on q or Escape; the function returns only then. The window
      *>>   title is `title_hint`, or
      *>> the plot's title (title_text()) when the hint is empty.
      *>>   Requires a build with X11 support and a reachable `DISPLAY`;
      *>>   use save()
      *>> in headless code such as tests and CI.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::runif(0, 1, 1000);
      *>> const auto y = dm::sin(20 / (x + 0.25)) + dm::rnorm(0, 0.2,
      *>>   1000);
      *>> dm::Plot(x,
      *>>   y).set_title("Doppler").set_col("blue").show("Doppler
      *>>   signal");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If X11 support was not compiled in, the
      *>>   display cannot be opened, its visual is unsupported, or the
      *>>   plot has no data series.
      *>>
      *>> See also: save(), to_svg()
       01 DM-PLOT-SHOW.
      *>> Self: the PLOT handle.
          05 DM-PLOT-SHOW-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-PLOT-SHOW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> title_hint (const std::string & = ""): text in PIC X(256);
      *>>   -LENGTH -1 trims trailing spaces. Window title; empty (the
      *>>   default) uses the plot's title.
          05 DM-PLOT-SHOW-TITLE-HINT PIC X(256).
          05 DM-PLOT-SHOW-TITLE-HINT-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE -1.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   R-PLOT

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::RPlot() -> R-PLOT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_new" USING DM-R-PLOT-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::graphics::RPlot (a subclass of PLOT; the
      *>>   inherited methods' records, DM-PLOT-*, take this handle as
      *>>   -SELF). Close the handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A single R-base-graphics-style plot, spelled `dm::Plot` in
      *>>   user code.
      *>>
      *>> Covers R's high-level plotting calls (`plot()`, `hist()`,
      *>>   `barplot()`, `boxplot()`, `pie()`,
      *>> `curve()`, `qqnorm()`, ...) as constructors and static
      *>>   factories, plus the "add to the
      *>> current plot" verbs R exposes as separate functions
      *>>   (`points()`, `lines()`, `abline()`,
      *>> `legend()`, `text()`, ...) as chainable member functions. A
      *>>   plot is an ordinary value: it
      *>> owns copies of its data, copies cheaply enough to pass around,
      *>>   and draws nothing until it
      *>> is rendered with to_svg(), save(), or show().
      *>>
      *>> Two composition styles mix freely. Setters such as set_title()
      *>>   and verbs such as points()
      *>> mutate the plot and return `RPlot&` for chaining; the pipe
      *>>   `plot | dm::points(...)` (see
      *>> compose.hpp) appends a reusable layer to a copy. A fresh plot
      *>>   has the title
      *>> "datamunge plot", axis labels "x" and "y", a 960x640 canvas,
      *>>   and a light grid.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::seql(-2.0, 2.0, 9);
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(x, x * x)
      *>>                .set_title("Parabola")
      *>>                .set_xlab("x")
      *>>                .set_ylab("x squared");
      *>> plt.lines(x, x * x, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}).abline_h(0.0);
      *>> plt.save("parabola.svg");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: dm::points(), dm::legend(), dm::Histogram,
      *>>   dm::Splom, RLayout
      *>>
      *>> Constructs an empty plot with default labels and styling,
      *>>   ready for layers.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot();
      *>> std::print("{} | {} | {}\n", plt.title_text(),
      *>>   plt.x_label_text(), plt.y_label_text());
      *>> // datamunge plot | x | y
      *>> ```
       01 DM-R-PLOT-NEW.
      *>> Result: R-PLOT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::RPlot(std::vector<double> x,
      *>>   std::vector<double> y) -> R-PLOT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_new_vector_double_vector_double" USING
      *>>   DM-R-PLOT-NEW-VECTOR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::graphics::RPlot (a subclass of PLOT; the
      *>>   inherited methods' records, DM-PLOT-*, take this handle as
      *>>   -SELF). Close the handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A single R-base-graphics-style plot, spelled `dm::Plot` in
      *>>   user code.
      *>>
      *>> Covers R's high-level plotting calls (`plot()`, `hist()`,
      *>>   `barplot()`, `boxplot()`, `pie()`,
      *>> `curve()`, `qqnorm()`, ...) as constructors and static
      *>>   factories, plus the "add to the
      *>> current plot" verbs R exposes as separate functions
      *>>   (`points()`, `lines()`, `abline()`,
      *>> `legend()`, `text()`, ...) as chainable member functions. A
      *>>   plot is an ordinary value: it
      *>> owns copies of its data, copies cheaply enough to pass around,
      *>>   and draws nothing until it
      *>> is rendered with to_svg(), save(), or show().
      *>>
      *>> Two composition styles mix freely. Setters such as set_title()
      *>>   and verbs such as points()
      *>> mutate the plot and return `RPlot&` for chaining; the pipe
      *>>   `plot | dm::points(...)` (see
      *>> compose.hpp) appends a reusable layer to a copy. A fresh plot
      *>>   has the title
      *>> "datamunge plot", axis labels "x" and "y", a 960x640 canvas,
      *>>   and a light grid.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::seql(-2.0, 2.0, 9);
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(x, x * x)
      *>>                .set_title("Parabola")
      *>>                .set_xlab("x")
      *>>                .set_ylab("x squared");
      *>> plt.lines(x, x * x, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}).abline_h(0.0);
      *>> plt.save("parabola.svg");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: dm::points(), dm::legend(), dm::Histogram,
      *>>   dm::Splom, RLayout
      *>>
      *>> Constructs a scatter plot of `y` against `x`, like R's
      *>>   `plot(x, y)`.
      *>>
      *>> Equivalent to `RPlot::plot(x, y)`: one unlabeled scatter
      *>>   series drawn in blue
      *>> `{37, 99, 235}` with 4-pixel markers; axis ranges fit the
      *>>   data. The coordinates are
      *>> copied (or moved from rvalues); `dm::Vector<double>` converts
      *>>   implicitly. Restyle the
      *>> points afterwards with set_col().
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(iris.get_column("Petal.Length").values(),
      *>>                     iris.get_column("Petal.Width").values())
      *>>                .set_col("blue");
      *>> plt.save("petals.svg");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the coordinates are empty or have
      *>>   different lengths.
      *>>
      *>> See also: points(), set_col(), plot()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-NEW-VECTOR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE.
      *>> x (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Horizontal
      *>>   coordinates, one per point.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-NEW-VECTOR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE-X USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Vertical
      *>>   coordinates, paired with `x` by position.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-NEW-VECTOR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE-Y USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: R-PLOT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-NEW-VECTOR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE-RESULT USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> static datamunge::graphics::RPlot::create() -> RPlot
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_create" USING DM-R-PLOT-CREATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Returns a fresh empty plot; same as the default constructor
      *>>   `dm::Plot()`.
      *>>
      *>> Kept for language bindings, where a named factory reads better
      *>>   than a constructor.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
