      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::set_col(RGBA color) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_set_col" USING DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Recolors every data series already on the plot (R's `col =`
      *>>   applied after the fact).
      *>>
      *>> Every existing series (points, lines, bars, ...) takes
      *>>   `color`, and per-point palettes
      *>> from dm::points(...).set_col() or points_colored() are
      *>>   cleared, so the whole plot becomes
      *>> one color. Only layers present now are affected: points added
      *>>   later keep their own
      *>> colors. Reference lines (abline) and manual legend entries
      *>>   keep their explicit colors.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(3.0, 1.0,
      *>>   2.0))
      *>>                .set_col(dm::RGB{220, 100, 255});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[0].color.r); // 220
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: set_col(std::string), dm::points()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. New color, including its alpha.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::set_col(std::string color) ->
      *>>   RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_set_col_string" USING DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Same as set_col(RGB) but takes a color name or hex string,
      *>>   like R's `col = "blue"`.
      *>>
      *>> Matching is case-insensitive. Names are black, white, red,
      *>>   green, blue, yellow, cyan,
      *>> magenta, gray/grey, and transparent (a much smaller set than
      *>>   R's `colors()`). Green is
      *>> RGB(0,128,0) as in CSS, and transparent is black with zero
      *>>   alpha. Hex strings are
      *>> `#RRGGBB` or `#RRGGBBAA`, where AA is an alpha byte (`80` is
      *>>   about 0.5).
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::runif(0, 1, 100);
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(x, dm::rnorm(0, 1, 100)).set_col("blue");
      *>> plt.set_col("#FF000080"); // half-transparent red
      *>> const auto& col = plt.series()[0].color;
      *>> std::print("{} {:.2f}\n", col.r, col.alpha); // 255 0.50
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For unknown names or malformed hex;
      *>>   the plot is unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> See also: set_col(RGB)
       01 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (std::string): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1 trims
      *>>   trailing spaces. Color name or hex string.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-COLOR PIC X(256).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-COLOR-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::set_col(const char * color) ->
      *>>   RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_set_col_string_2" USING
      *>>   DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2 DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Parse a literal color without ambiguity between the string and
      *>>   RGBA overloads.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See set_col(std::string), which accepts literals with this
      *>>   overload.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For null, empty or malformed color
      *>>   text; leaves the plot unchanged.
       01 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (const char *): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1 trims
      *>>   trailing spaces. Null-terminated color name or hex text;
      *>>   must be non-null.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2-COLOR PIC X(256).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2-COLOR-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SET-COL-STRING-2-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::background(RGBA color) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_background" USING DM-R-PLOT-BACKGROUND
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Sets the fill color of the whole canvas, like R's `par(bg =)`.
      *>>
      *>> The default is white `{255, 255, 255}`. The color fills the
      *>>   margins as well as the
      *>> plotting area. Its alpha is ignored: the background is always
      *>>   drawn opaque.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0))
      *>>                .background(dm::RGB{17, 24, 39})
      *>>                .axis_color(dm::RGB{229, 231, 235})
      *>>                .grid_color(dm::RGB{55, 65, 81}); // a dark
      *>>                  theme
      *>> std::print("{} {}\n", plt.background_color().r,
      *>>   plt.axes_color().r); // 17 229
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `par()` (`bg`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: axis_color(), grid_color()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-BACKGROUND.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-BACKGROUND-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Canvas fill color.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-BACKGROUND-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-BACKGROUND-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::axis_color(RGBA color) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_axis_color" USING DM-R-PLOT-AXIS-COLOR
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Sets the color of the axis lines and of all plot-level text:
      *>>   tick labels, axis
      *>> labels, title, and subtitle (R's `col.axis`, `col.lab`, and
      *>>   `col.main` in one).
      *>>
      *>> The default is slate gray `{55, 65, 81}`. Legend text follows
      *>>   it too; text() labels and
      *>> data layers keep their own colors.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See background(), which builds a dark theme with all three
      *>>   color setters.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `par()` (`col.axis`, `col.lab`, `col.main`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: background(), grid_color()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-AXIS-COLOR.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-AXIS-COLOR-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Axis and text color.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-AXIS-COLOR-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-AXIS-COLOR-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::grid_color(RGBA color) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_grid_color" USING DM-R-PLOT-GRID-COLOR
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Sets the color of the background grid lines (default light
      *>>   gray `{229, 231, 235}`).
      *>>
      *>> Has no visible effect while the grid is off (show_grid(false))
      *>>   or the axes are hidden.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See background(), which builds a dark theme with all three
      *>>   color setters.
      *>>
      *>> See also: show_grid(), background()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-GRID-COLOR.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-GRID-COLOR-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Grid-line color.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-GRID-COLOR-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-GRID-COLOR-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::show_grid(bool enabled = true) ->
      *>>   RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_show_grid" USING DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Turns the light background grid on or off (on by default),
      *>>   like adding or
      *>> omitting R's `grid()`.
      *>>
      *>> One vertical line per x tick and one horizontal line per y
      *>>   tick, so the grid always lines
      *>> up with the tick labels (round values, evenly spaced ticks, or
      *>>   custom tick positions).
      *>> Hidden axes hide the grid too.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0,
      *>>   2.0)).show_grid(false);
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.grid_visible()); // false
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `grid()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/grid.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: grid_color(), hide_axes()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> enabled (bool = true): 0 or 1. True (the default) draws the
      *>>   grid; false omits it.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID-ENABLED USAGE BINARY-CHAR UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SHOW-GRID-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::x_limits(double min_x, double
      *>>   max_x) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_x_limits" USING DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Fixes the visible x range, like R's `xlim = c(min_x, max_x)`.
      *>>
      *>> Without limits the x range is fitted to every layer (and
      *>>   vertical reference line), plus
      *>> 5% padding on each side. With limits, the plotting area spans
      *>>   exactly [`min_x`, `max_x`]
      *>> with no padding (R pads `xlim` by 4% unless `xaxs = "i"`).
      *>>   Data outside the range is not
      *>> clipped and may be drawn over the margins.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0,
      *>>   9.0))
      *>>                .x_limits(0.0, 4.0)
      *>>                .y_limits(0.0, 10.0);
      *>> std::print("{} {} {}\n", plt.has_x_limits(), plt.x_max(),
      *>>   plt.y_max()); // true 4 10
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `plot.default()` (`xlim`, `ylim`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Unless `max_x > min_x` (NaN fails);
      *>>   the plot is unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> See also: y_limits()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> min_x (double): double. Left edge of the plotting area, in
      *>>   data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS-MIN-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> max_x (double): double. Right edge; must be greater than
      *>>   `min_x`.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS-MAX-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-X-LIMITS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::y_limits(double min_y, double
      *>>   max_y) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_y_limits" USING DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Fixes the visible y range, like R's `ylim = c(min_y, max_y)`.
      *>>
      *>> Without limits the y range is fitted to every layer, plus 10%
      *>>   padding at each end (bar
      *>> layers also include y = 0). With limits, the plotting area
      *>>   spans exactly
      *>> [`min_y`, `max_y`]. Data outside the range is not clipped.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See x_limits(), which sets both ranges.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `plot.default()` (`xlim`, `ylim`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/plot.default.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Unless `max_y > min_y` (NaN fails);
      *>>   the plot is unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> See also: x_limits()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> min_y (double): double. Bottom edge of the plotting area, in
      *>>   data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS-MIN-Y USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> max_y (double): double. Top edge; must be greater than
      *>>   `min_y`.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS-MAX-Y USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-Y-LIMITS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::xlim(double min_x, double max_x)
      *>>   -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_xlim" USING DM-R-PLOT-XLIM DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Short spelling of x_limits(), matching R's xlim plot argument.
      *>> Fixes the horizontal plotting range without automatic padding.
      *>>   Data outside
      *>> the range follows x_limits() rendering behavior; this does not
      *>>   filter layers.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::plot().points(dm::c(0, 1), dm::c(1, 2)).xlim(0,
      *>>   1).ylim(-4, 4);
      *>> std::print("{} {}\n", plt.x_max(), plt.y_min()); // 1 -4
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Unless max_x > min_x (NaN fails); the
      *>>   plot is unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> See also: x_limits(), ylim()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-XLIM.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-XLIM-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> min_x (double): double. Left edge in data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-XLIM-MIN-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> max_x (double): double. Right edge; must be greater than
      *>>   min_x.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-XLIM-MAX-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-XLIM-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::ylim(double min_y, double max_y)
      *>>   -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_ylim" USING DM-R-PLOT-YLIM DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Short spelling of y_limits(), matching R's ylim plot argument.
      *>> Fixes the vertical plotting range without automatic padding;
      *>>   layers are retained.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See xlim(), whose example uses both aliases.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Unless max_y > min_y (NaN fails); the
      *>>   plot is unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> See also: y_limits(), xlim()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-YLIM.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-YLIM-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> min_y (double): double. Bottom edge in data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-YLIM-MIN-Y USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> max_y (double): double. Top edge; must be greater than min_y.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-YLIM-MAX-Y USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
