      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::qqline(std::vector<double> data,
      *>>   RGBA color = ..., double stroke_width = 1.5) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_qqline" USING DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Adds the reference line of a normal Q-Q plot, like R's
      *>>   `qqline(y)`: the line
      *>> through the first and third quartiles of `data` and of the
      *>>   standard normal.
      *>>
      *>> Pass the same data given to qqnorm(). The sample quartiles are
      *>>   R's default "type 7"
      *>> quantiles, as in R's `qqline()` defaults (`probs = c(0.25,
      *>>   0.75)`, `qtype = 7`). The
      *>> slope is the sample IQR divided by the normal IQR (about
      *>>   1.349), so for normal data it
      *>> estimates the standard deviation and the intercept estimates
      *>>   the mean. Added through
      *>> abline().
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const std::vector<double> sample{3.0, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 4.0};
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot::qqnorm(sample).qqline(sample);
      *>> const auto& line = plt.reference_lines()[0];
      *>> std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", line.value, line.slope); //
      *>>   3.0000 1.4826
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `qqline()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/qqnorm.html
      *>> - R. J. Hyndman, Y. Fan (1996). "Sample Quantiles in
      *>>   Statistical Packages". The American
      *>>   Statistician 50(4), 361-365.
      *>>     doi:10.1080/00031305.1996.10473566
      *>>
      *>> Warning: Only to_svg() and `.svg` saves draw reference lines.
      *>>   The raster outputs (`.png`,
      *>> `.pdf`, and show()) currently omit them.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `data` has fewer than two values.
      *>>
      *>> See also: qqnorm(), abline()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> data (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. The sample;
      *>>   at least two values. Taken by value, so the caller's data is
      *>>   unchanged.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-DATA USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Line color (default red `{220, 38,
      *>>   38}`).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> stroke_width (double = 1.5): double. Line width in pixels
      *>>   (default 1.5).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-STROKE-WIDTH USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-QQLINE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::legend(std::vector<std::string>
      *>>   labels, std::vector<RGBA> colors) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_legend" USING DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Adds manual legend rows, like R's `legend(legend = labels, col
      *>>   = colors)`.
      *>>
      *>> Each label is paired with the color at the same position and
      *>>   drawn as a swatch row in
      *>> the legend box. Once any manual row exists, it replaces the
      *>>   automatic legend built from
      *>> labeled series. Calling again appends more rows. Unlike
      *>>   dm::legend(), colors are not
      *>> recycled here: the vectors must match in length. Empty vectors
      *>>   add nothing.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto up = dm::RGB{37, 99, 235};
      *>> const auto down = dm::RGB{220, 38, 38};
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot();
      *>> plt.points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(1.0, 2.0), up)
      *>>    .points(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0), down)
      *>>    .legend({"rising", "falling"}, {up, down});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.legend_entries().size()); // 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `legend()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/legend.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `labels` and `colors` differ in
      *>>   length.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dm::legend() for the recycling, pipe-friendly layer.
       01 DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> labels (std::vector<std::string>): VECTOR-STRING handle. Row
      *>>   texts, in display order.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND-LABELS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> colors (std::vector<RGBA>): VECTOR-RGB handle. Swatch colors,
      *>>   one per label.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND-COLORS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-LEGEND-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::text(double x, double y,
      *>>   std::string label, RGBA color = ..., double font_size =
      *>>   14.0) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_text" USING DM-R-PLOT-TEXT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draws `label` centered on the data point (`x`, `y`), like R's
      *>> `text(x, y, labels = label)`.
      *>>
      *>> The text is centered both horizontally and vertically on the
      *>>   point, like R's default
      *>> `adj`. The point counts toward the automatic axis ranges, but
      *>>   the text's extent does not,
      *>> so long labels near the edge can overflow. Text layers never
      *>>   appear in the legend, and
      *>> set_col() recolors them along with the data.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), dm::c(1.0, 4.0,
      *>>   2.0))
      *>>                .text(2.0, 4.4, "peak", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38},
      *>>                  16.0);
      *>> std::print("{} {}\n", plt.series()[1].label,
      *>>   plt.series()[1].marker_size); // peak 16
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `text()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/text.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: pie(), legend()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 3.
      *>> x (double): double. Horizontal position, in data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> y (double): double. Vertical position, in data units.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-Y USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> label (std::string): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1 trims
      *>>   trailing spaces. The text to draw (XML-escaped in SVG
      *>>   output).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-LABEL PIC X(256).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-LABEL-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              -1.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Text color (default near-black `{17,
      *>>   24, 39}`).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> font_size (double = 14.0): double. Font size in pixels
      *>>   (default 14).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-FONT-SIZE USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-TEXT-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::polygon(std::vector<double> x,
      *>>   std::vector<double> y, RGBA color = ..., bool filled = true)
      *>>   -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_polygon" USING DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draws the closed polygon through the vertices (x[i], y[i]),
      *>>   like R's
      *>> `polygon(x, y, col = color)`.
      *>>
      *>> The last vertex joins the first automatically. Filled polygons
      *>>   (the default) are painted
      *>> with `color` (plus a 1-pixel outline in SVG; the raster fill
      *>>   uses the even-odd rule);
      *>> unfilled ones draw a 2-pixel outline only (R's default is
      *>>   unfilled). Translucent colors work well
      *>> for shaded regions such as confidence bands. Vertices count
      *>>   toward the axis ranges.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot()
      *>>                .polygon({0.0, 1.0, 0.5}, {0.0, 0.0, 1.0},
      *>>                  dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 0.4})
      *>>                .polygon({0.2, 0.8, 0.8, 0.2}, {0.1, 0.1, 0.4,
      *>>                  0.4}, dm::RGB{0, 0, 0}, false);
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].filled); // false
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `polygon()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/polygon.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the coordinates are empty or have
      *>>   different lengths.
      *>>
      *>> See also: pie(), segments()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> x (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Vertex x
      *>>   coordinates, in order around the polygon.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Vertex y
      *>>   coordinates, paired with `x`.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Fill (and outline) color (default
      *>>   blue `{37, 99, 235}`).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> filled (bool = true): 0 or 1. True (the default) fills; false
      *>>   draws the outline only.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-FILLED USAGE BINARY-CHAR UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-POLYGON-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::image(const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   x, const std::vector<double> & y, const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & z, std::vector<RGBA>
      *>>   palette = {}, double z_min = ..., double z_max = ...) ->
      *>>   RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_image" USING DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draws a grid of coloured rectangles, one per value of `z`,
      *>>   like R's `image(x, y, z)`.
      *>>
      *>> Cell (i, j) is centred on (x[i], y[j]) and reaches halfway to
      *>>   its neighbours (a half step
      *>> beyond the outermost centres). Its colour is the palette entry
      *>>   for z[i][j] on a linear
      *>> scale from `z_min` to `z_max`; NaN cells are left empty. Each
      *>>   cell is added as a filled
      *>> polygon, so the grid should have at most tens of thousands of
      *>>   cells.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto plt = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image({0.0, 1.0},
      *>>   {0.0, 1.0, 2.0}, {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size()); // 6
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `image()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/image.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the sizes do not match, x or y is
      *>>   not increasing, or the palette is empty.
      *>>
      *>> See also: terrain_colors(), polygon()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 6 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 3.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Cell
      *>>   centres along x, increasing (at least 2).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Cell
      *>>   centres along y, increasing (at least 2).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> z (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Values, z[i][j] at (x[i],
      *>>   y[j]); z.size() == x.size() and each row has y.size()
      *>>   entries.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-Z USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> palette (std::vector<RGBA> = {}): VECTOR-RGB handle. Colours
      *>>   from low to high (default terrain_colors(12)).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-PALETTE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> z_min (double): double. Value mapped to the first colour; NaN
      *>>   (the default) uses the smallest z.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-Z-MIN USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> z_max (double): double. Value mapped to the last colour; NaN
      *>>   (the default) uses the largest z.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-Z-MAX USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-IMAGE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RPlot::segments(std::vector<double> x0,
      *>>   std::vector<double> y0, std::vector<double> x1,
      *>>   std::vector<double> y1, RGBA color = ..., double
      *>>   stroke_width = 1.0) -> RPlot &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_plot_segments" USING DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draws one straight segment from (x0[i], y0[i]) to (x1[i],
      *>>   y1[i]) for each i, like
      *>> R's `segments(x0, y0, x1, y1)`.
      *>>
      *>> All segments share `color` and `stroke_width` and form one
      *>>   Segment layer; their end
      *>> points count toward the axis ranges. Useful for error bars,
      *>>   residual sticks, and
      *>> dendrograms.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::c(1.0, 2.0, 3.0);
      *>> const auto y = dm::c(2.0, 3.5, 3.0);
      *>> auto plt = dm::Plot(x, y).segments(x, y - 0.5, x, y + 0.5); //
      *>>   +/- 0.5 error bars
      *>> std::print("{}\n", plt.series()[1].x.size()); // 6 (start,
      *>>   end, start, end, ...)
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `segments()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/segments.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the four vectors differ in length
      *>>   or are empty.
      *>>
      *>> See also: abline(), polygon()
       01 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS.
      *>> Self: the R-PLOT handle.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 4 to 6 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 4.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 4.
      *>> x0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Start x
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-X0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y0 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Start y
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-Y0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> x1 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. End x
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-X1 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y1 (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. End y
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-Y1 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> color (RGBA): RGB handle. Segment color (default opaque
      *>>   black).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> stroke_width (double = 1.0): double. Segment width in pixels
      *>>   (default 1).
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-STROKE-WIDTH USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (RPlot &): R-PLOT handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). This plot, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-PLOT-SEGMENTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   R-LAYOUT

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RLayout::RLayout() -> R-LAYOUT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_r_layout_new" USING DM-R-LAYOUT-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::graphics::RLayout. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Tiles independently built plots into one multi-panel figure,
      *>>   like R's
      *>> `par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))` followed by one plot per panel.
      *>>
      *>> Create it with create(rows, cols), then add() up to rows *
      *>>   cols panels; they fill the grid
      *>> row by row. Each panel is copied (and its SVG rendered) at
      *>>   add() time, so later changes to
      *>> the original plot do not show and panels need not outlive the
      *>>   layout. Each cell is
      *>> `width / cols` by `(height - band) / rows` pixels, where band
      *>>   is title_band_px when a
      *>> figure title is set and 0 otherwise; every panel is scaled
      *>>   from its own canvas size into its
      *>> cell. The figure background is white. Unused cells stay empty.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto x = dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 30);
      *>> auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      *>>                .size(1200, 500)
      *>>                .title("Two views")
      *>>                .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::sin(x)).set_title("sin"))
      *>>                .add(dm::Plot(x, dm::cos(x)).set_title("cos"));
      *>> std::print("{} panels in {}x{}\n", fig.panel_count(),
      *>>   fig.rows(), fig.cols());
      *>> // 2 panels in 1x2
      *>> fig.save("two_views.svg");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `par()` (`mfrow`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: RPlot, RPlot::margins(), dm::Splom
       01 DM-R-LAYOUT-NEW.
      *>> Result: R-LAYOUT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> static datamunge::graphics::RLayout::create(std::size_t rows,
      *>>   std::size_t cols) -> RLayout
      *>> CALL "dm_r_layout_create" USING DM-R-LAYOUT-CREATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Creates an empty layout with `rows` by `cols` cells, like R's
      *>> `par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))`.
      *>>
      *>> The figure starts at 960 by 640 pixels with no title.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See RLayout.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `par()` (`mfrow`):
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/par.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `rows` or `cols` is 0.
      *>>
      *>> See also: add()
       01 DM-R-LAYOUT-CREATE.
      *>> rows (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of panel rows; must
      *>>   be positive.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-CREATE-ROWS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> cols (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of panel columns;
      *>>   must be positive.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-CREATE-COLS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RLayout): R-LAYOUT handle (new; close it). The empty
      *>>   layout.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-CREATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RLayout::add(const Plot & panel) ->
      *>>   RLayout &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_layout_add" USING DM-R-LAYOUT-ADD DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Copies `panel` into the next free cell, filling rows left to
      *>>   right, top to bottom.
      *>>
      *>> The panel's SVG is rendered immediately and stored with the
      *>>   copy, so rendering errors
      *>> surface here and later edits to the original plot have no
      *>>   effect on the layout.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,
      *>>   1).add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0, 2.0), dm::c(2.0, 1.0)));
      *>> try {
      *>>   fig.add(dm::Plot(dm::c(1.0), dm::c(1.0)));
      *>> } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
      *>>   std::print("{}\n", e.what()); // RLayout::add: layout is
      *>>     already full
      *>> }
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If all rows * cols cells are already
      *>>   filled.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If `panel` has no data series or its
      *>>   canvas is smaller than its margins.
      *>>
      *>> See also: panel(), panel_count()
       01 DM-R-LAYOUT-ADD.
      *>> Self: the R-LAYOUT handle.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-ADD-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> panel (const Plot &): PLOT handle. The plot to add; any RPlot
      *>>   (or other Plot) converts.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-ADD-PANEL USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RLayout &): R-LAYOUT handle (new; refers into its
      *>>   owner, which it keeps alive). This layout, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-ADD-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RLayout::size(std::size_t width,
      *>>   std::size_t height) -> RLayout &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_layout_size" USING DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Sets the size of the whole figure in pixels (default 960 by
      *>>   640).
      *>>
      *>> Panels keep their own canvas sizes and are scaled into their
      *>>   cells, so text in a panel
      *>> shrinks or grows with it; give panels a size close to their
      *>>   cell for readable labels.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See RLayout.
      *>>
      *>> See also: title()
       01 DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE.
      *>> Self: the R-LAYOUT handle.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> width (std::size_t): unsigned long. Figure width in pixels.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE-WIDTH USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> height (std::size_t): unsigned long. Figure height in pixels,
      *>>   including the title band.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE-HEIGHT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (RLayout &): R-LAYOUT handle (new; refers into its
      *>>   owner, which it keeps alive). This layout, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-SIZE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::RLayout::title(std::string value) ->
      *>>   RLayout &
      *>> CALL "dm_r_layout_title" USING DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Sets an optional figure-level heading drawn in a band above
      *>>   the panels, like R's
      *>> `mtext(main, outer = TRUE)`; an empty string (the default)
      *>>   reserves no band.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See RLayout.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - R `mtext()`:
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/mtext.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: title_text()
       01 DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE.
      *>> Self: the R-LAYOUT handle.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Value (std::string): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1 trims
      *>>   trailing spaces. Heading text; the band is title_band_px
      *>>   pixels tall.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE-VALUE PIC X(256).
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE-VALUE-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE -1.
      *>> Result (RLayout &): R-LAYOUT handle (new; refers into its
      *>>   owner, which it keeps alive). This layout, for chaining.
          05 DM-R-LAYOUT-TITLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

