      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::geometry::VoronoiDiagram. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
       01 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-NEW.
      *>> Result: VORONOI-DIAGRAM handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> std::vector<Point2D>
      *>>   datamunge::geometry::VoronoiDiagram::vertices
      *>> CALL "dm_voronoi_diagram_vertices_get" USING
      *>>   DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-VERTICES DM-STATUS (reads -VALUE)
      *>> CALL "dm_voronoi_diagram_vertices_set" USING
      *>>   DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-VERTICES DM-STATUS (writes -VALUE)
      *>>
      *>> The circumcenter of each Delaunay triangle -- the Voronoi
      *>>   diagram's vertices
      *>> (may repeat a coordinate if it's shared by multiple cells;
      *>>   kept as plain
      *>> resolved points rather than a shared, index-referenced pool
      *>>   for a simpler,
      *>> binding-friendly shape).
       01 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-VERTICES.
      *>> Self: the VORONOI-DIAGRAM handle.
          05 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-VERTICES-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Value (std::vector<Point2D>): VECTOR-POINT2D handle (new;
      *>>   refers into its owner, which it keeps alive).
          05 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-VERTICES-VALUE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> std::vector<std::vector<Point2D>>
      *>>   datamunge::geometry::VoronoiDiagram::cells
      *>> CALL "dm_voronoi_diagram_cells_get" USING
      *>>   DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-CELLS DM-STATUS (reads -VALUE)
      *>> CALL "dm_voronoi_diagram_cells_set" USING
      *>>   DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-CELLS DM-STATUS (writes -VALUE)
      *>>
      *>> cells[i] is points[i]'s Voronoi cell boundary, as
      *>>   circumcenters in angular
      *>> order around points[i]. For an INTERIOR point this is a closed
      *>>   polygon (as with
      *>> any polygon in this module, the closing edge back to
      *>>   cells[i].front() is
      *>> implicit). For a point on the convex hull, the true Voronoi
      *>>   cell is unbounded --
      *>> this only returns its FINITE portion (an open polyline,
      *>>   missing the two rays to
      *>> infinity), since robustly constructing those rays is out of
      *>>   scope here. Empty
      *>> for a point that ended up in no Delaunay triangle (possible
      *>>   for an
      *>> exactly-duplicated input point).
       01 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-CELLS.
      *>> Self: the VORONOI-DIAGRAM handle.
          05 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-CELLS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Value (std::vector<std::vector<Point2D>>):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-POINT2D handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive).
          05 DM-VORONOI-DIAGRAM-CELLS-VALUE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   LINALG-MATRIX

      *>> datamunge::LinalgMatrix::LinalgMatrix(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & values, std::size_t rows, std::size_t
      *>>   cols, bool sparse = false) -> LINALG-MATRIX handle
      *>> CALL "dm_linalg_matrix_new" USING DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::LinalgMatrix. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Binding counterpart of Mat/SpMat with shared, immutable
      *>>   expression operands.
      *>> Transpose, multiplication and inverse actions defer native
      *>>   computation until eval(),
      *>> values() or text(). Handles keep temporary operands alive
      *>>   across garbage collection.
      *>> Sparse products use CSR; solves use the canonical dense LU/QR
      *>>   implementation.
      *>> Concurrent const evaluation is safe; no evaluation cache is
      *>>   mutated.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::LinalgMatrix A({2, 0, 0, 4}, 2, 2), b({6, 8}, 2, 1);
      *>> auto x = A.inverse_action().multiply(b);
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n", x.values()[0]); // 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: Mat, SpMat, solve()
      *>>
      *>> Construct an owned dense or CSR matrix from row-major values.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::LinalgMatrix A({1, 0}, 1, 2, true);
      *>> std::print("{}\n", A.cols()); // 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If dimensions overflow or do not
      *>>   match values.
       01 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              3.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Exactly rows * cols coefficients; sparse zero entries are
      *>>   omitted.
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> rows (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of rows, including
      *>>   zero.
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-ROWS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> cols (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of columns,
      *>>   including zero.
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-COLS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> sparse (bool = false): 0 or 1. Select CSR storage, default
      *>>   false.
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-SPARSE USAGE BINARY-CHAR UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: LINALG-MATRIX handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-LINALG-MATRIX-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::LinalgMatrix::rows() const -> std::size_t
      *>> CALL "dm_linalg_matrix_rows" USING DM-LINALG-MATRIX-ROWS
