      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   ODE-SOLVER

      *>> datamunge::ode::ODESolver::ODESolver(ODEOptions options = {})
      *>>   -> ODE-SOLVER handle
      *>> CALL "dm_ode_solver_new" USING DM-ODE-SOLVER-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::ode::ODESolver. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Numerically integrates dy/dt = f(t, y) forward from t_start to
      *>>   t_end (t_end must be
      *>> greater than t_start; this project's "basic" ODE support does
      *>>   not handle backward
      *>> integration) given an initial state y0, via one of: explicit
      *>>   Euler, the explicit
      *>> midpoint rule (RK2), classical Runge-Kutta (RK4), adaptive
      *>>   Dormand-Prince
      *>> Runge-Kutta 4(5) (RK45), explicit Adams-Bashforth (order 2-4),
      *>>   or
      *>> Adams-Bashforth-Moulton predictor-corrector (order 2-4 -- the
      *>>   "implicit" linear
      *>> multistep option; this project uses a PECE predictor-corrector
      *>>   rather than a full
      *>> Newton-iteration implicit solve, avoiding the need for a
      *>>   Jacobian). Every multistep
      *>> method self-starts using classical RK4 for its first few
      *>>   steps.
       01 DM-ODE-SOLVER-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (ODEOptions = {}): ODE-OPTIONS handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: ODE-SOLVER handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::ode::ODESolver::solve(RHS & rhs, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & y0, double t_start, double t_end)
      *>>   const -> ODESolution
      *>> CALL "dm_ode_solver_solve" USING DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE DM-STATUS
       01 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE.
      *>> Self: the ODE-SOLVER handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> rhs (RHS &): RHS handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-RHS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y0 (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-Y0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> t_start (double): double.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-T-START USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> t_end (double): double.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-T-END USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (ODESolution): ODE-SOLUTION handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::ode::ODESolver::solve_builtin(const std::string &
      *>>   system, const std::vector<double> & params, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & y0, double t_start, double t_end)
      *>>   const -> ODESolution
      *>> CALL "dm_ode_solver_solve_builtin" USING
      *>>   DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Same as solve(), but the RHS is one of a fixed set of named
      *>>   systems instead of a
      *>> live user-supplied one -- the only way every language binding
      *>>   (not just the ones
      *>> with SWIG director support) can use this solver. `params` is
      *>>   interpreted
      *>> positionally per system (missing entries fall back to the
      *>>   defaults below):
      *>> - "exponential_decay": dy/dt = -k y. params = {k=1}, y0 size
      *>>   1.
      *>> - "logistic_growth": dy/dt = r y (1 - y/K). params = {r=1,
      *>>   K=1}, y0 size 1.
      *>> - "harmonic_oscillator": (x,v), dx/dt=v, dv/dt=-omega^2 x.
      *>>   params = {omega=1}, y0 size 2.
      *>> - "van_der_pol": (x,v), dx/dt=v, dv/dt=mu(1-x^2)v-x. params =
      *>>   {mu=1}, y0 size 2.
      *>> - "lorenz": (x,y,z), the classic chaotic system. params =
      *>>   {sigma=10, rho=28, beta=8/3}, y0 size 3.
      *>>   Throws std::invalid_argument for an unknown system name.
       01 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN.
      *>> Self: the ODE-SOLVER handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> system (const std::string &): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1
      *>>   trims trailing spaces.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-SYSTEM PIC X(256).
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-SYSTEM-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> params (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-PARAMS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> y0 (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-Y0 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> t_start (double): double.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-T-START USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> t_end (double): double.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-T-END USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (ODESolution): ODE-SOLUTION handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-SOLVE-BUILTIN-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::ode::ODESolver::options() const -> const ODEOptions
      *>>   &
      *>> CALL "dm_ode_solver_options" USING DM-ODE-SOLVER-OPTIONS
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The options this ODESolver was configured with.
       01 DM-ODE-SOLVER-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the ODE-SOLVER handle.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const ODEOptions &): ODE-OPTIONS handle (new; refers
      *>>   into its owner, which it keeps alive). The stored options.
          05 DM-ODE-SOLVER-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

