      *>   ADA-GRAD

      *>> datamunge::optim::AdaGrad::AdaGrad(AdaGradOptions options =
      *>>   {}) -> ADA-GRAD handle
      *>> CALL "dm_ada_grad_new" USING DM-ADA-GRAD-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::AdaGrad. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> AdaGrad on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
      *>> Accumulates squared full gradients; divides each update by
      *>>   sqrt(sum+epsilon). Tests the pre-update gradient norm after
      *>>   applying the update. Every optimize call resets the
      *>>   accumulator. O(p) local storage.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::AdaGrad optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Duchi, E. Hazan and Y. Singer (2011), Adaptive
      *>>   Subgradient Methods for Online Learning and Stochastic
      *>>   Optimization, https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/duchi11a.html
      *>>
      *>> See also: AdaGradOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::AdaGrad optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-ADA-GRAD-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-ADA-GRAD-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (AdaGradOptions = {}): ADA-GRAD-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by
      *>>   AdaGradOptions.
          05 DM-ADA-GRAD-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: ADA-GRAD handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ADA-GRAD-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::AdaGrad::optimize(DifferentiableFunction &
      *>>   function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_ada_grad_optimize" USING DM-ADA-GRAD-OPTIMIZE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run AdaGrad from the supplied point using the configured
      *>>   finite budget.
      *>> Accumulates squared full gradients; divides each update by
      *>>   sqrt(sum+epsilon). Tests the pre-update gradient norm after
      *>>   applying the update. Every optimize call resets the
      *>>   accumulator. O(p) local storage.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::AdaGradOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::AdaGrad(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: AdaGrad: step_size must be positive;
      *>>   AdaGrad: epsilon must be positive; AdaGrad: tolerance must
      *>>   be non-negative.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), AdaGradOptions
       01 DM-ADA-GRAD-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the ADA-GRAD handle.
          05 DM-ADA-GRAD-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
