      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   AMS-GRAD

      *>> datamunge::optim::AMSGrad::AMSGrad(AMSGradOptions options =
      *>>   {}) -> AMS-GRAD handle
      *>> CALL "dm_ams_grad_new" USING DM-AMS-GRAD-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::AMSGrad. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> AMSGrad on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
      *>> Bias-corrected AMSGrad variant: maximises uncorrected second
      *>>   moments, then divides that maximum by 1-beta2^t. The
      *>>   corrected denominator need not be monotone, so the original
      *>>   uncorrected theorem is not automatically inherited. Tests
      *>>   the gradient used after updating; O(p) reset state.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::AMSGrad optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - S. Reddi, S. Kale and S. Kumar (2018), On the Convergence of
      *>>   Adam and Beyond, https://openreview.net/forum?id=ryQu7f-RZ
      *>>
      *>> See also: AMSGradOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::AMSGrad optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-AMS-GRAD-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (AMSGradOptions = {}): AMS-GRAD-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by
      *>>   AMSGradOptions.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: AMS-GRAD handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::AMSGrad::optimize(DifferentiableFunction &
      *>>   function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_ams_grad_optimize" USING DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run AMSGrad from the supplied point using the configured
      *>>   finite budget.
      *>> Bias-corrected AMSGrad variant: maximises uncorrected second
      *>>   moments, then divides that maximum by 1-beta2^t. The
      *>>   corrected denominator need not be monotone, so the original
      *>>   uncorrected theorem is not automatically inherited. Tests
      *>>   the gradient used after updating; O(p) reset state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::AMSGradOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::AMSGrad(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: AMSGrad: step_size must be positive;
      *>>   AMSGrad: beta1 must be in [0, 1); AMSGrad: beta2 must be in
      *>>   [0, 1); AMSGrad: epsilon must be positive; AMSGrad:
      *>>   tolerance must be non-negative.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), AMSGradOptions
       01 DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the AMS-GRAD handle.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (DifferentiableFunction &): DIFFERENTIABLE-FUNCTION
      *>>   handle. Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite
      *>>   output shapes.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-AMS-GRAD-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::AMSGrad::options() const -> const
