      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   CONJUGATE-GRADIENT

      *>> datamunge::optim::ConjugateGradient::ConjugateGradient(ConjugateGradientOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> CONJUGATE-GRADIENT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_conjugate_gradient_new" USING
      *>>   DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::ConjugateGradient. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> ConjugateGradient on the DifferentiableFunction callback
      *>>   interface.
      *>> Nonlinear Polak-Ribiere-plus directions with negative beta
      *>>   truncated to zero, periodic/descent restarts and an Armijo
      *>>   line search. It is not linear CG with exact line
      *>>   minimisation and does not imply termination in p steps.
      *>>   Returns when gradient/change/search conditions stop
      *>>   progress.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::ConjugateGradient optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: ConjugateGradientOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::ConjugateGradient optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (ConjugateGradientOptions = {}):
      *>>   CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific controls;
      *>>   defaults are listed by ConjugateGradientOptions.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: CONJUGATE-GRADIENT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::ConjugateGradient::optimize(DifferentiableFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_conjugate_gradient_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run ConjugateGradient from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> Nonlinear Polak-Ribiere-plus directions with negative beta
      *>>   truncated to zero, periodic/descent restarts and an Armijo
      *>>   line search. It is not linear CG with exact line
      *>>   minimisation and does not imply termination in p steps.
      *>>   Returns when gradient/change/search conditions stop
      *>>   progress.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::ConjugateGradientOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::ConjugateGradient(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: ConjugateGradient: tolerance must be
      *>>   non-negative; ConjugateGradient: armijo_c1 must be in (0,
      *>>   1); ConjugateGradient: backtracking_factor must be in (0,
      *>>   1).
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), ConjugateGradientOptions
       01 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the CONJUGATE-GRADIENT handle.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (DifferentiableFunction &): DIFFERENTIABLE-FUNCTION
      *>>   handle. Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite
      *>>   output shapes.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::ConjugateGradient::options() const -> const
      *>>   ConjugateGradientOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_conjugate_gradient_options" USING
      *>>   DM-CONJUGATE-GRADIENT-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
