      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   COORDINATE-DESCENT

      *>> datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent::CoordinateDescent(CoordinateDescentOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> COORDINATE-DESCENT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_coordinate_descent_new" USING
      *>>   DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> CoordinateDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback
      *>>   interface.
      *>> Each epoch visits coordinates in index order. Every partial
      *>>   update requests a full gradient and an Armijo-backtracked
      *>>   value sequence; this is not exact one-dimensional
      *>>   minimisation. An exhausted search restores that coordinate.
      *>>   Stops on the full end-of-epoch gradient norm.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::CoordinateDescent optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - S. J. Wright (2015), Coordinate descent algorithms,
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1007/s10107-015-0892-3. This
      *>>   implementation uses the stated full-gradient
      *>>   coordinate/block steps.
      *>>
      *>> See also: CoordinateDescentOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::CoordinateDescent optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 1000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (CoordinateDescentOptions = {}):
      *>>   COORDINATE-DESCENT-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific controls;
      *>>   defaults are listed by CoordinateDescentOptions.
          05 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: COORDINATE-DESCENT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::CoordinateDescent::optimize(DifferentiableFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_coordinate_descent_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run CoordinateDescent from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> Each epoch visits coordinates in index order. Every partial
      *>>   update requests a full gradient and an Armijo-backtracked
      *>>   value sequence; this is not exact one-dimensional
      *>>   minimisation. An exhausted search restores that coordinate.
      *>>   Stops on the full end-of-epoch gradient norm.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::CoordinateDescentOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::CoordinateDescent(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: CoordinateDescent: step_size must be
      *>>   positive; CoordinateDescent: tolerance must be non-negative;
      *>>   CoordinateDescent: armijo_c1 must be in (0, 1);
      *>>   CoordinateDescent: backtracking_factor must be in (0, 1).
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), CoordinateDescentOptions
       01 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the COORDINATE-DESCENT handle.
          05 DM-COORDINATE-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
