      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   GRADIENT-DESCENT

      *>> datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::GradientDescent(GradientDescentOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> GRADIENT-DESCENT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_gradient_descent_new" USING DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::GradientDescent. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> GradientDescent on the DifferentiableFunction callback
      *>>   interface.
      *>> Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates
      *>>   before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective
      *>>   change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away
      *>>   from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line
      *>>   search.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::GradientDescent optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: GradientDescentOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::GradientDescent optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (GradientDescentOptions = {}):
      *>>   GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific controls;
      *>>   defaults are listed by GradientDescentOptions.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: GRADIENT-DESCENT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::optimize(DifferentiableFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_gradient_descent_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run GradientDescent from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> Velocity is momentum*velocity-step_size*gradient. Updates
      *>>   before testing the gradient just used or absolute objective
      *>>   change. Equal objective values can stop an oscillation away
      *>>   from stationarity. O(p) reset velocity state; no line
      *>>   search.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::GradientDescentOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::GradientDescent(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), GradientDescentOptions
       01 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the GRADIENT-DESCENT handle.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (DifferentiableFunction &): DIFFERENTIABLE-FUNCTION
      *>>   handle. Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite
      *>>   output shapes.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::GradientDescent::options() const -> const
      *>>   GradientDescentOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_gradient_descent_options" USING
      *>>   DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
       01 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the GRADIENT-DESCENT handle.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const GradientDescentOptions &):
      *>>   GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS handle (new; refers into its owner,
      *>>   which it keeps alive). Const option reference, valid during
      *>>   this object's lifetime; no mutation.
          05 DM-GRADIENT-DESCENT-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

