      *>   LBFGS

      *>> datamunge::optim::LBFGS::LBFGS(LBFGSOptions options = {}) ->
      *>>   LBFGS handle
      *>> CALL "dm_lbfgs_new" USING DM-LBFGS-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::LBFGS. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> LBFGS on the DifferentiableFunction callback interface.
      *>> Limited-memory BFGS with weak-Wolfe bracket/bisection search,
      *>>   descent fallback and positive-curvature pair retention. If
      *>>   the search budget ends, a previous Armijo step can be used;
      *>>   if none was found, returns. Tests gradient norm or absolute
      *>>   objective change. O(history_size*p) state; history_size must
      *>>   be positive.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::LBFGS optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 500
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: LBFGSOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::LBFGS optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 500
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-LBFGS-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-LBFGS-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (LBFGSOptions = {}): LBFGS-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by
      *>>   LBFGSOptions.
          05 DM-LBFGS-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: LBFGS handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-LBFGS-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::LBFGS::optimize(DifferentiableFunction &
      *>>   function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_lbfgs_optimize" USING DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run LBFGS from the supplied point using the configured finite
      *>>   budget.
      *>> Limited-memory BFGS with weak-Wolfe bracket/bisection search,
      *>>   descent fallback and positive-curvature pair retention. If
      *>>   the search budget ends, a previous Armijo step can be used;
      *>>   if none was found, returns. Tests gradient norm or absolute
      *>>   objective change. O(history_size*p) state; history_size must
      *>>   be positive.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::LBFGSOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::LBFGS(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), LBFGSOptions
       01 DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the LBFGS handle.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (DifferentiableFunction &): DIFFERENTIABLE-FUNCTION
      *>>   handle. Borrowed DifferentiableFunction with matching finite
      *>>   output shapes.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::LBFGS::options() const -> const LBFGSOptions
      *>>   &
      *>> CALL "dm_lbfgs_options" USING DM-LBFGS-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
       01 DM-LBFGS-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the LBFGS handle.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const LBFGSOptions &): LBFGS-OPTIONS handle (new;
      *>>   refers into its owner, which it keeps alive). Const option
      *>>   reference, valid during this object's lifetime; no mutation.
          05 DM-LBFGS-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

