      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT

      *>> datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::LevenbergMarquardt(LevenbergMarquardtOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_levenberg_marquardt_new" USING
      *>>   DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> LevenbergMarquardt on the ResidualFunction callback interface.
      *>> Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by
      *>>   unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of
      *>>   half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance
      *>>   and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after
      *>>   rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient
      *>>   certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - K. Levenberg (1944), A Method for the Solution of Certain
      *>>   Non-Linear Problems in Least Squares,
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1090/qam/10666; D. W. Marquardt (1963),
      *>>   An Algorithm for Least-Squares Estimation of Nonlinear
      *>>   Parameters, https://doi.org/10.1137/0111030
      *>>
      *>> See also: LevenbergMarquardtOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (LevenbergMarquardtOptions = {}):
      *>>   LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific
      *>>   controls; defaults are listed by LevenbergMarquardtOptions.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::optimize(ResidualFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_levenberg_marquardt_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run LevenbergMarquardt from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by
      *>>   unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of
      *>>   half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance
      *>>   and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after
      *>>   rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient
      *>>   certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   data=dm::optim::optimization_curve_data();dm::optim::CurveResidual
      *>>   f(data);
      *>> dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardtOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{2,.5};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: LevenbergMarquardt: initial_damping
      *>>   must be positive.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), LevenbergMarquardtOptions
       01 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT handle.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (ResidualFunction &): RESIDUAL-FUNCTION handle.
      *>>   Borrowed ResidualFunction with matching finite output
      *>>   shapes.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates (half residual SSE); not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-LEVENBERG-MARQUARDT-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::LevenbergMarquardt::options() const -> const
