      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   NEWTON

      *>> datamunge::optim::Newton::Newton(NewtonOptions options = {})
      *>>   -> NEWTON handle
      *>> CALL "dm_newton_new" USING DM-NEWTON-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::Newton. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Newton on the HessianFunction callback interface.
      *>> Adds fixed damping to the supplied Hessian diagonal, solves
      *>>   with partial-pivot Gaussian elimination, and takes a full
      *>>   step. This is not a globalised line-search method;
      *>>   indefinite or nearly singular Hessians can increase the loss
      *>>   or produce huge steps. Stops on gradient norm. Dense O(p^3)
      *>>   solve/O(p^2) storage.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::Newton optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 100
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: NewtonOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::Newton optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 100
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-NEWTON-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-NEWTON-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (NewtonOptions = {}): NEWTON-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by
      *>>   NewtonOptions.
          05 DM-NEWTON-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: NEWTON handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-NEWTON-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::Newton::optimize(HessianFunction & function,
      *>>   std::vector<double> & coordinates) const -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_newton_optimize" USING DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run Newton from the supplied point using the configured finite
      *>>   budget.
      *>> Adds fixed damping to the supplied Hessian diagonal, solves
      *>>   with partial-pivot Gaussian elimination, and takes a full
      *>>   step. This is not a globalised line-search method;
      *>>   indefinite or nearly singular Hessians can increase the loss
      *>>   or produce huge steps. Stops on gradient norm. Dense O(p^3)
      *>>   solve/O(p^2) storage.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::NewtonOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::Newton(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Newton: Hessian has wrong dimension;
      *>>   Newton: singular Hessian.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), NewtonOptions
       01 DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the NEWTON handle.
          05 DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (HessianFunction &): HESSIAN-FUNCTION handle.
      *>>   Borrowed HessianFunction with matching finite output shapes.
          05 DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

