      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   PROXIMAL-GRADIENT

      *>> datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::ProximalGradient(ProximalGradientOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> PROXIMAL-GRADIENT handle
      *>> CALL "dm_proximal_gradient_new" USING DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient. Close the handle
      *>>   with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> ProximalGradient on the ProximalFunction callback interface.
      *>> ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with
      *>>   gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate
      *>>   displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or
      *>>   backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned
      *>>   objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::ProximalGradient optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), A Fast Iterative
      *>>   Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse
      *>>   Problems, https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542
      *>>
      *>> See also: ProximalGradientOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::ProximalGradient optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (ProximalGradientOptions = {}):
      *>>   PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific controls;
      *>>   defaults are listed by ProximalGradientOptions.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: PROXIMAL-GRADIENT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::optimize(ProximalFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_proximal_gradient_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run ProximalGradient from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> ISTA update prox(x-step_size*gradient(x),step_size), with
      *>>   gradient of the smooth part only. Stops on coordinate
      *>>   displacement norm. There is no Lipschitz estimation or
      *>>   backtracking; caller chooses a stable rate. The returned
      *>>   objective includes both smooth and nonsmooth parts.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
      *>> dm::optim::ProximalGradientOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::ProximalGradient(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: ProximalGradient: step_size must be
      *>>   positive; ProximalGradient: proximal returned wrong
      *>>   dimension.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), ProximalGradientOptions
       01 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the PROXIMAL-GRADIENT handle.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (ProximalFunction &): PROXIMAL-FUNCTION handle.
      *>>   Borrowed ProximalFunction with matching finite output
      *>>   shapes.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::ProximalGradient::options() const -> const
      *>>   ProximalGradientOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_proximal_gradient_options" USING
      *>>   DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
       01 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the PROXIMAL-GRADIENT handle.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const ProximalGradientOptions &):
      *>>   PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS handle (new; refers into its
      *>>   owner, which it keeps alive). Const option reference, valid
      *>>   during this object's lifetime; no mutation.
          05 DM-PROXIMAL-GRADIENT-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
