      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   RBF-GAUSSIAN-PROCESS-SURROGATE

      *>> datamunge::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate(double
      *>>   length_scale = 1.0, double noise = 9.9999999999999995E-7) ->
      *>>   RBF-GAUSSIAN-PROCESS-SURROGATE handle
      *>> CALL "dm_rbf_gaussian_process_surrogate_new_double_double"
      *>>   USING DM-RBF-GAUSSIAN-PROCESS-SURROGATE-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate (a
      *>>   subclass of BAYESIAN-SURROGATE; the inherited methods'
      *>>   records, DM-BAYESIAN-SURROGATE-*, take this handle as
      *>>   -SELF). Close the handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> Exact Gaussian-process regression surrogate with the
      *>>   squared-exponential kernel
      *>>
      *>> `s\exp(-\lVert x-x'\rVert^2/2\ell^2)`.
      *>>
      *>> fit() Cholesky-factors `K+\sigma_n^2I=LL^\top` once and solves
      *>>   for
      *>> `\alpha=(K+\sigma_n^2I)^{-1}(y-m)`; predict() then needs one
      *>>   forward substitution per point:
      *>> mean `m+k_*^\top\alpha` and variance `s-\lVert
      *>>   L^{-1}k_*\rVert^2`.
      *>>
      *>> The two-argument constructor keeps the original fixed setup:
      *>>   zero prior mean, unit signal
      *>> variance, no normalisation or fitting, expected improvement.
      *>>   The options constructor adds
      *>> value normalisation, marginal-likelihood fitting of the
      *>>   hyperparameters and other acquisitions.
      *>> The kernel is isotropic, so the inputs should be on comparable
      *>>   scales.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(1.0, 0.0);
      *>> gp.fit({{0.0}, {2.0}}, {1.0, -1.0});
      *>> const auto p = gp.predict({1.0});
      *>> std::print("{:.4f} {:.4f}\n", p.mean, p.sd); // 0.0000 0.5933
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C. E. Rasmussen and C. K. I. Williams (2006). *Gaussian
      *>>   Processes for Machine Learning*,
      *>>   chapters 2 and 5. MIT Press.
      *>>
      *>> See also: BayesianOptimization, expected_improvement()
      *>>
      *>> Fixed-hyperparameter surrogate with expected improvement.
      *>>
      *>> `\ell>0`.
      *>>
      *>> `\ge0`).
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `length_scale` is not positive or
      *>>   `noise` is negative.
       01 DM-RBF-GAUSSIAN-PROCESS-SURROGATE-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-RBF-GSSN-PROCESS-SURROGATE-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE-GIVEN USAGE
              BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> length_scale (double = 1.0): double. Kernel length scale
          05 DM-RBF-GSSN-PRCSS-SRRGT-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE-LENGTH-SCALE
              USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> noise (double = 9.9999999999999995E-7): double. Noise variance
      *>>   added to the diagonal (
          05 DM-RBF-GSSN-PROCESS-SURROGATE-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE-NOISE USAGE
              COMP-2.
      *>> Result: RBF-GAUSSIAN-PROCESS-SURROGATE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-RBF-GSSN-PROCESS-SURROGATE-NEW-DOUBLE-DOUBLE-RESULT
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

