      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   SAGA

      *>> datamunge::optim::SAGA::SAGA(SAGAOptions options = {}) -> SAGA
      *>>   handle
      *>> CALL "dm_saga_new" USING DM-SAGA-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::SAGA. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> SAGA on the DifferentiableSeparableFunction callback
      *>>   interface.
      *>> Uses current_i-table_i+mean(table), then refreshes one stored
      *>>   component gradient and its running mean. The estimator
      *>>   targets mean component loss while evaluate() returns their
      *>>   sum. Stops on epoch-boundary objective change or the last
      *>>   estimator norm; the latter is not the full gradient norm.
      *>>   O(n*p) gradient table.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::SAGA optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - A. Defazio, F. Bach and S. Lacoste-Julien (2014), SAGA: A
      *>>   Fast Incremental Gradient Method With Support for
      *>>   Non-Strongly Convex Composite Objectives,
      *>>   https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.0202
      *>>
      *>> See also: SAGAOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::SAGA optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); //
      *>>   10000
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-SAGA-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-SAGA-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (SAGAOptions = {}): SAGA-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Method-specific controls; defaults are listed by
      *>>   SAGAOptions.
          05 DM-SAGA-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: SAGA handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-SAGA-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::SAGA::optimize(DifferentiableSeparableFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_saga_optimize" USING DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run SAGA from the supplied point using the configured finite
      *>>   budget.
      *>> Uses current_i-table_i+mean(table), then refreshes one stored
      *>>   component gradient and its running mean. The estimator
      *>>   targets mean component loss while evaluate() returns their
      *>>   sum. Stops on epoch-boundary objective change or the last
      *>>   estimator norm; the latter is not the full gradient norm.
      *>>   O(n*p) gradient table.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1},{1}},{1,3});
      *>> dm::optim::SAGAOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::SAGA(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: SAGA: step_size must be positive;
      *>>   SAGA: tolerance must be non-negative; SAGA: function has
      *>>   zero terms.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), SAGAOptions
       01 DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the SAGA handle.
          05 DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (DifferentiableSeparableFunction &):
      *>>   DIFFERENTIABLE-SEPARABLE-FUNCTION handle. Borrowed
      *>>   DifferentiableSeparableFunction with matching finite output
      *>>   shapes.
          05 DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-SAGA-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::SAGA::options() const -> const SAGAOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_saga_options" USING DM-SAGA-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
       01 DM-SAGA-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the SAGA handle.
          05 DM-SAGA-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
