      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   SQP

      *>> datamunge::optim::SQP::SQP(SQPOptions options = {}) -> SQP
      *>>   handle
      *>> CALL "dm_sqp_new" USING DM-SQP-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::SQP. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> SQP on the EqualityConstrainedFunction callback interface.
      *>> Restricted projection-like implementation, not a general SQP
      *>>   QP solver: takes an objective-gradient step, then sequential
      *>>   linearised row corrections using squared Jacobian-row norms
      *>>   plus regularization. Its stopping condition compares
      *>>   constraint norm and the constant absolute step_size to
      *>>   tolerance, not the actual displacement or Lagrangian
      *>>   gradient.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::SQP optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: SQPOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::SQP optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-SQP-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-SQP-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.
      *>> options (SQPOptions = {}): SQP-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific
      *>>   controls; defaults are listed by SQPOptions.
          05 DM-SQP-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: SQP handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-SQP-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::SQP::optimize(EqualityConstrainedFunction &
      *>>   function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_sqp_optimize" USING DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run SQP from the supplied point using the configured finite
      *>>   budget.
      *>> Restricted projection-like implementation, not a general SQP
      *>>   QP solver: takes an objective-gradient step, then sequential
      *>>   linearised row corrections using squared Jacobian-row norms
      *>>   plus regularization. Its stopping condition compares
      *>>   constraint norm and the constant absolute step_size to
      *>>   tolerance, not the actual displacement or Lagrangian
      *>>   gradient.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::EqualityQuadratic f({2,0},1);
      *>> dm::optim::SQPOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto value=dm::optim::SQP(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), SQPOptions
       01 DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the SQP handle.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (EqualityConstrainedFunction &):
      *>>   EQUALITY-CONSTRAINED-FUNCTION handle. Borrowed
      *>>   EqualityConstrainedFunction with matching finite output
      *>>   shapes.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::SQP::options() const -> const SQPOptions &
      *>> CALL "dm_sqp_options" USING DM-SQP-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Borrow this optimizer's stored configuration.
       01 DM-SQP-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the SQP handle.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const SQPOptions &): SQP-OPTIONS handle (new; refers
      *>>   into its owner, which it keeps alive). Const option
      *>>   reference, valid during this object's lifetime; no mutation.
          05 DM-SQP-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

