      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   TRUST-REGION-NEWTON

      *>> datamunge::optim::TrustRegionNewton::TrustRegionNewton(TrustRegionNewtonOptions
      *>>   options = {}) -> TRUST-REGION-NEWTON handle
      *>> CALL "dm_trust_region_newton_new" USING
      *>>   DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::optim::TrustRegionNewton. Close the
      *>>   handle with CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> TrustRegionNewton on the HessianFunction callback interface.
      *>> Cauchy-point variant: clips a steepest-descent ray using gHg
      *>>   and the current radius. Accepts any strict objective
      *>>   decrease and doubles the radius, otherwise halves it. It
      *>>   does not compute a model-agreement ratio, dogleg step or
      *>>   exact trust-region subproblem. Stops on gradient norm or
      *>>   tiny radius; O(p^2) Hessian work.
      *>> The caller supplies finite, dimensionally consistent
      *>>   callbacks. Inputs are borrowed;
      *>> coordinates mutate in place and can retain a partial state if
      *>>   a callback throws.
      *>> Calls use local optimizer state and propagate callback
      *>>   exceptions; sharing a mutable
      *>> objective across threads requires external synchronisation.
      *>>   Normal return alone
      *>> is not a uniform convergence certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::TrustRegionNewton optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization,
      *>>   second edition, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5.
      *>>   The implemented variant and stopping rules are specified
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> See also: TrustRegionNewtonOptions, trace_optimize()
      *>>
      *>> Store a copy of the controls without running the objective.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::optim::TrustRegionNewton optimizer;
      *>> std::print("{}\n",optimizer.options().max_iterations); // 200
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: optimize()
       01 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 1 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> options (TrustRegionNewtonOptions = {}):
      *>>   TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIONS handle. Method-specific
      *>>   controls; defaults are listed by TrustRegionNewtonOptions.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: TRUST-REGION-NEWTON handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::optim::TrustRegionNewton::optimize(HessianFunction
      *>>   & function, std::vector<double> & coordinates) const ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_trust_region_newton_optimize" USING
      *>>   DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Run TrustRegionNewton from the supplied point using the
      *>>   configured finite budget.
      *>> Cauchy-point variant: clips a steepest-descent ray using gHg
      *>>   and the current radius. Accepts any strict objective
      *>>   decrease and doubles the radius, otherwise halves it. It
      *>>   does not compute a model-agreement ratio, dogleg step or
      *>>   exact trust-region subproblem. Stops on gradient norm or
      *>>   tiny radius; O(p^2) Hessian work.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
      *>> dm::optim::TrustRegionNewtonOptions
      *>>   controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
      *>> std::vector<double> x{0,0};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   value=dm::optim::TrustRegionNewton(controls).optimize(f,x);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",std::isfinite(value)); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: TrustRegionNewton: initial_radius
      *>>   must be positive.
      *>> - std::exception: Exceptions from supplied callbacks
      *>>   propagate. Raw solvers do not uniformly reject nonfinite
      *>>   inputs.
      *>>
      *>> See also: trace_optimize(), TrustRegionNewtonOptions
       01 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE.
      *>> Self: the TRUST-REGION-NEWTON handle.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> function (HessianFunction &): HESSIAN-FUNCTION handle.
      *>>   Borrowed HessianFunction with matching finite output shapes.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-FUNCTION USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coordinates (std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Owned-by-caller finite start; overwritten with the native
      *>>   returned point.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-COORDINATES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Objective at the native returned
      *>>   coordinates; not a stop-status code.
          05 DM-TRUST-REGION-NEWTON-OPTIMIZE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

