      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   ARIMA

      *>> datamunge::stats::ARIMA::ARIMA(const std::vector<double> & y,
      *>>   ARIMAOptions options = {}) -> ARIMA handle
      *>> CALL "dm_arima_new" USING DM-ARIMA-NEW DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::stats::ARIMA. Close the handle with CALL
      *>>   "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A Box-Jenkins seasonal ARIMA(p, d, q)(P, D, Q)_s model, fit to
      *>>   a single numeric
      *>> series by conditional sum of squares (CSS): the series is
      *>>   differenced (regular
      *>> d times, then seasonal D times with period s) to stationarity,
      *>>   the combined
      *>> AR/MA polynomials (regular composed with seasonal, via
      *>>   polynomial multiplication)
      *>> are estimated by minimizing the conditional residual sum of
      *>>   squares with
      *>> DifferentialEvolution, and forecasts are produced by recursing
      *>>   the fitted
      *>> difference equation forward and re-integrating through the
      *>>   differencing steps.
      *>>
      *>> Setting seasonal_period == 0 (the default) reduces this to a
      *>>   plain, non-seasonal
      *>> ARIMA(p, d, q) model -- i.e. SARIMA is the general case, ARIMA
      *>>   the special case,
      *>> exactly as in R's arima()/statsmodels' SARIMAX().
      *>>
      *>> Note: CSS estimation approximates the exact Gaussian
      *>>   likelihood by conditioning
      *>> on the first max(p + P*s, q + Q*s) observations rather than
      *>>   filtering the full
      *>> series (e.g. via a Kalman filter); it is a standard, textbook
      *>>   ARIMA fitting
      *>> method (R's arima(method = "CSS")) but will differ slightly
      *>>   from exact-ML fits.
      *>>
      *>> Fit the model to a series.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If a seasonal order is set while
      *>>   seasonal_period is zero, or if the series is too short for
      *>>   the requested differencing or AR order.
       01 DM-ARIMA-NEW.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-ARIMA-NEW-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. The
      *>>   observed series, in time order.
          05 DM-ARIMA-NEW-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> options (ARIMAOptions = {}): ARIMA-OPTIONS handle. Model
      *>>   orders and optimizer settings.
          05 DM-ARIMA-NEW-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: ARIMA handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ARIMA-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::ARIMA::options() const -> const ARIMAOptions
      *>>   &
      *>> CALL "dm_arima_options" USING DM-ARIMA-OPTIONS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The options the model was fit with.
       01 DM-ARIMA-OPTIONS.
      *>> Self: the ARIMA handle.
          05 DM-ARIMA-OPTIONS-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (const ARIMAOptions &): ARIMA-OPTIONS handle (new;
      *>>   refers into its owner, which it keeps alive). The stored
      *>>   ARIMAOptions.
          05 DM-ARIMA-OPTIONS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

