      *>>
      *>> Minimizes ||y - Bβ||² + λ ||D_d β||², where D_d is the
      *>>   order-d difference matrix on the
      *>> coefficients (d = 2 by default). Uniform knots continue past
      *>>   both ends of the data
      *>> without repeated boundary knots. When d <= degree+1,
      *>>   polynomials of degree < d
      *>> go unpenalized: λ → ∞ gives the least-squares line for d
      *>>   = 2. A rich basis can
      *>> include functions with no data under them if the data identify
      *>>   the penalty nullspace.
      *>> Without an explicit λ, it is chosen
      *>> by minimizing generalized cross-validation, GCV(λ) = n·RSS /
      *>>   (n − edf)².
      *>>
      *>> The polynomial-nullspace claim requires uniform spacing; it
      *>>   need not hold for irregular
      *>> supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only
      *>>   when the design identifies
      *>> the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns
      *>>   its fit and permits concurrent
      *>> const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost
      *>>   O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
      *>> this work. Very large lambda can make the equations
      *>>   numerically singular.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}
      *>>   {:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); //
      *>>   0.428571 2.142857
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing
      *>>   with B-splines and penalties.
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
      *>>
      *>> See also: fda::fit_pspline(), smoothing_path(), summary()
      *>>
      *>> `n_basis` B-splines of the given `degree` with equally spaced
