      *>> supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only
      *>>   when the design identifies
      *>> the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns
      *>>   its fit and permits concurrent
      *>> const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost
      *>>   O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
      *>> this work. Very large lambda can make the equations
      *>>   numerically singular.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}
      *>>   {:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); //
      *>>   0.428571 2.142857
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing
      *>>   with B-splines and penalties.
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
      *>>
      *>> See also: fda::fit_pspline(), smoothing_path(), summary()
      *>>
      *>> Cubic basis on the given knot locations; boundary knots are
      *>>   the smallest/largest of
      *>> `knots` and `x`. The difference penalty assumes roughly even
      *>>   spacing. λ chosen by GCV.
      *>> Uses the same bounded GCV search as the basis-count
      *>>   constructor.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::Vector<> x=dm::seql(0,1,30);
      *>> const dm::PSR fit(x,dm::sin(6*x),dm::seql(0,1,8));
      *>> std::print("{}\n",fit.basis_size()); // 10
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For invalid data/knots or zero-width
      *>>   combined domain.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If every candidate system fails
      *>>   numerically.
      *>>
      *>> See also: PSplineRegression, gcv(), knots()
       01 DM-P-SPLN-RGRSSN-NEW-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DOUBLE-VECTOR-DOUBLE.
      *>> x (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Nonempty finite
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-P-SPLN-RGRSSN-NEW-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-VECTOR-DOUBLE-X
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Finite matching
      *>>   responses.
          05 DM-P-SPLN-RGRSSN-NEW-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-VECTOR-DOUBLE-Y
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> knots (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Nonempty
      *>>   finite breakpoints, sorted/deduplicated and enlarged to
      *>>   contain x.
          05 DM-P-SPLN-RGRSSN-NEW-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-KNOTS USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: P-SPLINE-REGRESSION handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-P-SPLN-RGRSSN-NEW-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-VCTR-DBL-RESULT
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::PSplineRegression::PSplineRegression(const Vector<>
      *>>   & x, const Vector<> & y, const Vector<> & knots, double
      *>>   lambda, std::size_t degree = 3, std::size_t penalty_order =
      *>>   2) -> P-SPLINE-REGRESSION handle
      *>> CALL "dm_p_spl_rgr_new_vct_dbl_vct_dbl_vctr_dbl_dbl_ulng_ulng"
      *>>   USING
      *>>   DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULNG-ULNG
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::PSplineRegression. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A P-spline regression curve y ≈ Σ_j β_j B_j(x) (Eilers &
      *>>   Marx). `dm::PSR` for short.
      *>>
      *>> Minimizes ||y - Bβ||² + λ ||D_d β||², where D_d is the
      *>>   order-d difference matrix on the
      *>> coefficients (d = 2 by default). Uniform knots continue past
      *>>   both ends of the data
      *>> without repeated boundary knots. When d <= degree+1,
      *>>   polynomials of degree < d
      *>> go unpenalized: λ → ∞ gives the least-squares line for d
      *>>   = 2. A rich basis can
      *>> include functions with no data under them if the data identify
      *>>   the penalty nullspace.
      *>> Without an explicit λ, it is chosen
      *>> by minimizing generalized cross-validation, GCV(λ) = n·RSS /
      *>>   (n − edf)².
      *>>
      *>> The polynomial-nullspace claim requires uniform spacing; it
      *>>   need not hold for irregular
      *>> supplied knots. A positive lambda identifies a rich basis only
      *>>   when the design identifies
      *>> the penalty nullspace. No values are dropped. The class owns
      *>>   its fit and permits concurrent
      *>> const reads. Sparse assembly and dense Cholesky cost
      *>>   O(n(p+1)^2+K^3); GCV repeats
      *>> this work. Very large lambda can make the equations
      *>>   numerically singular.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}
      *>>   {:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0],fit.effective_df()); //
      *>>   0.428571 2.142857
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing
      *>>   with B-splines and penalties.
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
      *>>
      *>> See also: fda::fit_pspline(), smoothing_path(), summary()
      *>>
      *>> Basis on the given knot locations with a fixed smoothing
      *>>   parameter `lambda`.
      *>> Outer spacings are continued past the domain, without repeated
      *>>   boundary knots.
      *>> The penalty acts on coefficient index and does not correct for
      *>>   unequal knot spacing.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::PSR
      *>>   fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},dm::Vector<>{0,1,2},1.0,1,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}\n",fit.predict({1})[0]); // 0.428571
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For invalid data, options, knots or
      *>>   zero-width combined domain.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If the system is singular or non-finite.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If difference weights or the penalty
      *>>   overflow.
      *>>
      *>> See also: PSplineRegression, fda::pspline_difference_matrix()
       01 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULNG-ULNG.
      *>> Arguments to send: 4 to 6 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 4.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-6JR1M
              USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 4.
      *>> x (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Nonempty finite
      *>>   coordinates.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-DBL-ULN-ULNG-X
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Finite matching
      *>>   responses.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-DBL-ULN-ULNG-Y
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> knots (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Nonempty
      *>>   finite breakpoints, sorted/deduplicated and enlarged to
      *>>   contain x.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-SR9I8
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> lambda (double): double. Finite nonnegative smoothing
      *>>   multiplier.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-QTV81
              USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> degree (std::size_t = 3): unsigned long. Polynomial degree,
      *>>   default three.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-PSTLG
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> penalty_order (std::size_t = 2): unsigned long. Positive
      *>>   difference order smaller than the resulting basis, default
      *>>   two.
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-3IJDE
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: P-SPLINE-REGRESSION handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-P-SPL-RGR-NEW-VCT-DBL-VCT-DBL-VCTR-DBL-DBL-ULN-KTOGJ
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::PSplineRegression::predict(const Vector<> & x)
      *>>   const -> Vector<>
      *>> CALL "dm_p_spline_regression_predict" USING
      *>>   DM-P-SPLINE-REGRESSION-PREDICT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Evaluates the fitted curve at `x`. Points outside the fitted
      *>>   domain take the value
      *>> at the nearest boundary, i.e. constant extrapolation.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const dm::PSR fit({0,1,2},{0,1,0},3,1.0,1,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}\n",fit.predict({-1})[0]); // 0.285714
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For non-finite coordinates.
      *>>
      *>> See also: basis_matrix(), domain()
       01 DM-P-SPLINE-REGRESSION-PREDICT.
      *>> Self: the P-SPLINE-REGRESSION handle.
          05 DM-P-SPLINE-REGRESSION-PREDICT-SELF USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> x (const Vector<> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Finite evaluation
      *>>   coordinates; empty is accepted.
          05 DM-P-SPLINE-REGRESSION-PREDICT-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Vector<>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close it). Owned
      *>>   predictions in input order, after clamping x to domain().
          05 DM-P-SPLINE-REGRESSION-PREDICT-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

