      *> ----------------------------------------------------------------
      *>   TENSOR-B-SPLINE

      *>> datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline::TensorBSpline(BSplineBasis
      *>>   basis, std::vector<double> coefficients) -> TENSOR-B-SPLINE
      *>>   handle
      *>> CALL "dm_tensor_b_spline_new" USING DM-TENSOR-B-SPLINE-NEW
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A scalar tensor-product B-spline f(x) = sum_j c_j B_j(x) with
      *>>   owned coefficients.
      *>>
      *>> With one basis this is an ordinary spline curve; with d bases
      *>>   a surface or volume. The
      *>> coefficient net is row-major over the per-axis basis sizes
      *>>   (last axis fastest), the same
      *>> column order as fda::BSpline::evaluate(). Evaluation clamps
      *>>   each coordinate into the
      *>> basis domain, so points outside the domain take the boundary
      *>>   value. Const methods are
      *>> safe to call concurrently while the object is not mutated.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2); // linear
      *>>   on [0, 1]
      *>> dm::fda::TensorBSpline f({b, b}, {0, 1, 1, 2}); // f(x, y) = x
      *>>   + y
      *>> std::print("{:.2f}\n", f.evaluate({0.25, 0.5})); // 0.75
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C. de Boor (1972). On calculating with B-splines. Journal of
      *>>   Approximation Theory
      *>>   6(1), 50-62. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9
      *>>
      *>> See also: fit_tensor_bspline(), fit_tensor_pspline(), BSpline
      *>>
      *>> One-dimensional spline curve on `basis`.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::fda::TensorBSpline
      *>>   f(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2), {1.0, 3.0});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the coefficient count differs from
      *>>   basis.basis_size().
       01 DM-TENSOR-B-SPLINE-NEW.
      *>> basis (BSplineBasis): B-SPLINE-BASIS handle. Basis for the
      *>>   single axis.
          05 DM-TENSOR-B-SPLINE-NEW-BASIS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coefficients (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. One
      *>>   coefficient per basis function.
          05 DM-TENSOR-B-SPLINE-NEW-COEFFICIENTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: TENSOR-B-SPLINE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-TENSOR-B-SPLINE-NEW-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline::TensorBSpline(std::vector<BSplineBasis>
      *>>   bases, std::vector<double> coefficients) -> TENSOR-B-SPLINE
      *>>   handle
      *>> CALL "dm_tnsr_b_spln_new_vector_b_spline_basis_vector_double"
      *>>   USING DM-TNSR-B-SPLN-NEW-VECTOR-B-SPLINE-BASIS-VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> C++ class datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline. Close the handle with
      *>>   CALL "dm_close".
      *>>
      *>> A scalar tensor-product B-spline f(x) = sum_j c_j B_j(x) with
      *>>   owned coefficients.
      *>>
      *>> With one basis this is an ordinary spline curve; with d bases
      *>>   a surface or volume. The
      *>> coefficient net is row-major over the per-axis basis sizes
      *>>   (last axis fastest), the same
      *>> column order as fda::BSpline::evaluate(). Evaluation clamps
      *>>   each coordinate into the
      *>> basis domain, so points outside the domain take the boundary
      *>>   value. Const methods are
      *>> safe to call concurrently while the object is not mutated.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2); // linear
      *>>   on [0, 1]
      *>> dm::fda::TensorBSpline f({b, b}, {0, 1, 1, 2}); // f(x, y) = x
      *>>   + y
      *>> std::print("{:.2f}\n", f.evaluate({0.25, 0.5})); // 0.75
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C. de Boor (1972). On calculating with B-splines. Journal of
      *>>   Approximation Theory
      *>>   6(1), 50-62. https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9
      *>>
      *>> See also: fit_tensor_bspline(), fit_tensor_pspline(), BSpline
      *>>
      *>> Tensor-product spline over one basis per axis.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(0, 2); //
      *>>   piecewise constant
      *>> dm::fda::TensorBSpline f({b, b}, {1, 2, 3, 4});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", f.evaluate({0.75, 0.25})); // 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `bases` is empty or the net size
      *>>   does not match.
       01 DM-TNSR-B-SPLN-NEW-VECTOR-B-SPLINE-BASIS-VECTOR-DOUBLE.
      *>> bases (std::vector<BSplineBasis>): VECTOR-B-SPLINE-BASIS
      *>>   handle. One basis per dimension (at least one).
          05 DM-TNSR-B-SPLN-NEW-VCTR-B-SPLN-BSS-VECTOR-DOUBLE-BASES
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> coefficients (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Row-major net whose size is the product of the basis sizes.
          05 DM-TNSR-B-SPLN-NEW-VCTR-B-SPLN-BSS-VCTR-DBL-CFFCNTS USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result: TENSOR-B-SPLINE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-TNSR-B-SPLN-NEW-VCTR-B-SPLN-BSS-VECTOR-DOUBLE-RESULT
              USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

