      *>> datamunge::algebra::complex_roots(const Polynomial & p,
      *>>   std::size_t max_iterations = 200, double tolerance =
      *>>   9.9999999999999998E-13) -> std::vector<Complex>
      *>> CALL "dm_complex_roots" USING DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> All n = p.degree() complex roots of p (real roots included,
      *>>   with a negligible
      *>> imaginary part), via the Durand-Kerner (Weierstrass) method:
      *>>   simultaneous
      *>> fixed-point iteration z_k <- z_k - p(z_k) / prod_{j != k}(z_k
      *>>   - z_j), starting from n
      *>> points evenly spaced (with a fixed angular offset to avoid
      *>>   real-axis symmetry, which
      *>> would otherwise stall convergence to any non-real root of a
      *>>   real-coefficient
      *>> polynomial) around a circle whose radius is a Cauchy bound on
      *>>   root magnitude.
      *>> Converges quadratically once guesses are close, but -- unlike
      *>>   Sturm's theorem-based
      *>> real_roots(), which isolates each real root to a provably
      *>>   correct interval before
      *>> refining it -- this is a general iterative method with no
      *>>   convergence guarantee.
      *>> For best results p should be square-free (see
      *>>   square_free_factorization()); repeated
      *>> roots make two of the simultaneous iterates chase the same
      *>>   point, which both slows
      *>> convergence and risks a near-zero denominator late in the
      *>>   iteration.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: if p has degree 0 (a nonzero constant
      *>>   has no roots; the zero polynomial is degenerate).
       01 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> p (const Polynomial &): POLYNOMIAL handle.
          05 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_iterations (std::size_t = 200): unsigned long.
          05 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS-MAX-ITERATIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> tolerance (double = 9.9999999999999998E-13): double.
          05 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS-TOLERANCE USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<Complex>): VECTOR-COMPLEX handle (new;
      *>>   close it).
          05 DM-COMPLEX-ROOTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::algebra::rational_roots(const Polynomial & p,
      *>>   double tolerance = 1.0000000000000001E-9) ->
      *>>   std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_rational_roots" USING DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> All rational roots of p, via the Rational Root Theorem: every
      *>>   rational root a/b in
      *>> lowest terms has a dividing the constant term and b dividing
      *>>   the leading coefficient,
      *>> so trying every such ratio (after first dividing out any
      *>>   factors of x, i.e. any
      *>> roots at exactly 0) finds every rational root exactly -- a
      *>>   finite, exact search, in
      *>> contrast to real_roots()'s numeric bisection or
      *>>   complex_roots()'s general iteration.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: if p's coefficients are not within
      *>>   `tolerance` of integers.
       01 DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> p (const Polynomial &): POLYNOMIAL handle.
          05 DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> tolerance (double = 1.0000000000000001E-9): double. Used both
      *>>   to verify p's coefficients are within `tolerance` of
      *>>   integers (required for the theorem to apply) and to test
      *>>   each candidate ratio's residual.
          05 DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS-TOLERANCE USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-RATIONAL-ROOTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

