      *>> datamunge::image::rotate(const Image & src, double degrees,
      *>>   const Pixel & fill_color = {}) -> Image
      *>> CALL "dm_rotate" USING DM-ROTATE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> A new image rotated clockwise by an arbitrary angle (in
      *>>   degrees) about its center,
      *>> via inverse-mapping + bilinear resampling. The output canvas
      *>>   is exactly large enough
      *>> to contain the fully-rotated source (like Pillow's
      *>>   expand=True), and pixels sourced
      *>> from outside the original image are filled with `fill_color`.
       01 DM-ROTATE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-ROTATE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> src (const Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-ROTATE-SRC USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> degrees (double): double.
          05 DM-ROTATE-DEGREES USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> fill_color (const Pixel & = {}): PIXEL handle.
          05 DM-ROTATE-FILL-COLOR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Image): IMAGE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-ROTATE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::gaussian_pyramid(const image::Image & img, int
      *>>   levels, double sigma = 1.0) -> std::vector<image::Image>
      *>> CALL "dm_gaussian_pyramid" USING DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The classical Gaussian pyramid: level 0 is `img` itself; each
      *>>   subsequent level is
      *>> the previous level Gaussian-blurred (sigma = `sigma`) then
      *>>   downsampled by 2x
      *>> (bilinear resize) -- a coarse-to-fine multi-resolution
      *>>   representation used
      *>> throughout this module (e.g. as the natural foundation for a
      *>>   pyramidal optical-flow
      *>> extension, though lucas_kanade_optical_flow() itself stays
      *>>   single-level). Stops
      *>> early (producing fewer than `levels` images) if a level's
      *>>   dimensions would shrink
      *>> below 1x1. Throws std::invalid_argument for levels < 1.
       01 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> img (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID-IMG USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> levels (int): int.
          05 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID-LEVELS USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> sigma (double = 1.0): double.
          05 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID-SIGMA USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<image::Image>): VECTOR-IMAGE handle (new;
      *>>   close it).
          05 DM-GAUSSIAN-PYRAMID-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::detail::signed_difference_image(const
      *>>   image::Image & cur, const image::Image & upsampled) ->
      *>>   image::Image
      *>> CALL "dm_signed_difference_image" USING
      *>>   DM-SIGNED-DIFFERENCE-IMAGE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Per-channel signed difference (cur - upsampled), re-centered
      *>>   at 128 and clamped to
      *>> [0, 255] so it stores in an ordinary Image -- the standard way
      *>>   to make a Laplacian
      *>> pyramid level (which is naturally signed)
      *>>   visualizable/storable without introducing
      *>> a separate signed-pixel image type just for this.
       01 DM-SIGNED-DIFFERENCE-IMAGE.
      *>> cur (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-SIGNED-DIFFERENCE-IMAGE-CUR USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> upsampled (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-SIGNED-DIFFERENCE-IMAGE-UPSAMPLED USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (image::Image): IMAGE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-SIGNED-DIFFERENCE-IMAGE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::laplacian_pyramid(const image::Image & img, int
      *>>   levels, double sigma = 1.0) -> std::vector<image::Image>
      *>> CALL "dm_laplacian_pyramid" USING DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The Laplacian pyramid: for every level except the last,
      *>>   level_i = (Gaussian level i)
      *>> minus (Gaussian level i+1 upsampled back to level i's size) --
      *>>   the detail lost by
      *>> blurring+downsampling at that scale, re-centered at 128 (see
      *>> detail::signed_difference_image()) so it stores as an ordinary
      *>>   image; the final
      *>> level is the smallest Gaussian level kept as-is (the residual
      *>>   low-frequency image
      *>> the whole pyramid was built from). Reconstructing the original
      *>>   from a Laplacian
      *>> pyramid is the classical use (progressively add each
      *>>   un-recentered level back onto
      *>> the upsampled reconstruction-so-far) but isn't implemented
      *>>   here -- this module only
      *>> needed the decomposition direction.
       01 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              2.
      *>> img (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID-IMG USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> levels (int): int.
          05 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID-LEVELS USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> sigma (double = 1.0): double.
          05 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID-SIGMA USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<image::Image>): VECTOR-IMAGE handle (new;
      *>>   close it).
          05 DM-LAPLACIAN-PYRAMID-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::connected_components(const image::Image & img,
      *>>   bool eight_connected = true) -> LabelMap
      *>> CALL "dm_connected_components" USING DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Labels every foreground (luma >= 128) connected region of
      *>>   `img` with a distinct
      *>> positive integer via flood fill (simple breadth-first flood
      *>>   fill per unvisited
      *>> foreground pixel, rather than the classical two-pass
      *>>   union-find labeling algorithm
      *>> -- same asymptotic complexity, much easier to verify correct,
      *>>   matching this module's
      *>> established simplicity-over-cleverness bias).
      *>>   `eight_connected` selects 8-
      *>> neighborhood (diagonals count) vs. 4-neighborhood (orthogonal
      *>>   only) connectivity.
       01 DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 1.
      *>> img (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS-IMG USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> eight_connected (bool = true): 0 or 1.
          05 DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS-EIGHT-CONNECTED USAGE BINARY-CHAR
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (LabelMap): LABEL-MAP handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-CONNECTED-COMPONENTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::otsu_threshold(const image::Image & img) -> int
      *>> CALL "dm_otsu_threshold" USING DM-OTSU-THRESHOLD DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Otsu's method: the grayscale threshold (0-255) that maximizes
      *>>   the between-class
      *>> variance of the pixels it would split into "below" and
      *>>   "at-or-above" groups --
      *>> the standard automatic threshold selection when a fixed
      *>>   threshold isn't known in
      *>> advance. Pairs directly with image::threshold(img,
      *>>   otsu_threshold(img)).
       01 DM-OTSU-THRESHOLD.
      *>> img (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-OTSU-THRESHOLD-IMG USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (int): int.
          05 DM-OTSU-THRESHOLD-RESULT USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::cv::kmeans_segment(const image::Image & img, int k,
      *>>   int max_iterations = 20, unsigned int seed = 42) ->
      *>>   image::Image
      *>> CALL "dm_kmeans_segment" USING DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> K-means color segmentation: clusters every pixel of `img` by
      *>>   its RGB value into `k`
      *>> clusters (Lloyd's algorithm, deterministic fixed-seed
      *>>   initialization by sampling k
      *>> distinct pixels) and returns a new RGB image where every pixel
      *>>   is replaced by its
      *>> assigned cluster's mean color. Throws std::invalid_argument if
      *>>   `k` exceeds the
      *>> number of distinct pixels available to seed from.
       01 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> img (const image::Image &): IMAGE handle.
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-IMG USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> k (int): int.
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-K USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> max_iterations (int = 20): int.
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-MAX-ITERATIONS USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> seed (unsigned int = 42): unsigned int.
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-SEED USAGE BINARY-LONG UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (image::Image): IMAGE handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-KMEANS-SEGMENT-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::datasets::data_home() -> std::string
      *>> CALL "dm_data_home" USING DM-DATA-HOME DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The cache root where downloaded datasets are stored.
      *>>
      *>> The first of: `$DATAMUNGE_DATA_DIR`;
      *>>   `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/datamunge/datasets`;
      *>> `$HOME/.cache/datamunge/datasets`; on Windows
      *>>   `%LOCALAPPDATA%/datamunge/datasets`;
      *>> otherwise `build/datasets` relative to the working directory.
      *>>   Nothing is created.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{}\n", dm::datasets::data_home()); // e.g.
      *>>   /home/me/.cache/datamunge/datasets
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: fetch()
       01 DM-DATA-HOME.
      *>> Result (std::string): text in PIC X(16384); -LENGTH is its
      *>>   length in bytes. The directory path (it may not exist yet).
          05 DM-DATA-HOME-RESULT PIC X(16384).
          05 DM-DATA-HOME-RESULT-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              0.

      *>> datamunge::datasets::downloadable() ->
      *>>   std::vector<std::string>
      *>> CALL "dm_downloadable" USING DM-DOWNLOADABLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The names fetch() accepts, sorted: "california_housing",
      *>>   "cifar10", "cifar100",
      *>> "diamonds", "fashion_mnist", "karate_club", "mnist",
      *>>   "rebot_devarm", "sudoku".
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> for (const auto& name : dm::datasets::downloadable())
      *>>   std::print("{}\n", name);
      *>> ```
       01 DM-DOWNLOADABLE.
      *>> Result (std::vector<std::string>): VECTOR-STRING handle (new;
      *>>   close it).
          05 DM-DOWNLOADABLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::datasets::fetch(const std::string & name, const
      *>>   std::string & directory = "") -> std::string
      *>> CALL "dm_fetch" USING DM-FETCH DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Makes sure a downloadable dataset is on disk and returns where
      *>>   it is.
      *>>
      *>> For the tabular datasets ("diamonds", "california_housing")
      *>>   this is the CSV file. For the
      *>> image datasets it is a directory: "mnist" and "fashion_mnist"
      *>>   hold the four uncompressed
      *>> IDX files (`train-images-idx3-ubyte`, ...); "cifar10" holds
      *>>   `data_batch_1..5.bin` and
      *>> `test_batch.bin`; "cifar100" holds `train.bin` and `test.bin`
      *>>   (the binary versions);
      *>> "sudoku" holds Norvig's `easy50.txt`, `top95.txt` and
      *>>   `hardest.txt`.
      *>> "karate_club" holds the970-byte original ZIP, GML topology and
      *>>   source README;
      *>> every cached archive/member is checksum-verified.
      *>>   karate_club() reads34nodes/78edges.
      *>> "rebot_devarm" holds 38 pinned Seeed DevArm CAD/URDF/STL files
      *>>   and the
      *>> CERN-OHL-W-2.0 source license in their upstream relative
      *>>   directory layout.
      *>> DevArm verifies the size and SHA-256 of every cached file on
      *>>   every call;
      *>> changed files throw without replacement. Missing files are
      *>>   staged and verified
      *>> before publication. It uses the same offline/mirror/cache
      *>>   controls, and works
      *>> without the optional native data-preparation dependencies.
      *>>   Hashing uses memory
      *>> proportional to the largest source file (about 35 MB).
      *>> Approximate download sizes: diamonds 2.7 MB,
      *>>   california_housing 1.4 MB, MNIST 11 MB,
      *>> Fashion-MNIST 30 MB, CIFAR-10 163 MB, CIFAR-100 161 MB, sudoku
      *>>   14 KB, DevArm 56 MB.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto housing =
      *>>   dm::datasets::read_csv(dm::datasets::fetch("california_housing"));
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> For a complete CAD asset tree, use
      *>>   `dm::datasets::fetch("rebot_devarm")`.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - Seeed reBot-DevArm source:
      *>>   https://github.com/Seeed-Projects/reBot-DevArm
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: for an unknown name.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: if files are missing and downloading is
      *>>   disabled or fails, or a download does not match its
      *>>   checksum.
      *>>
      *>> See also: data_home(), downloadable(), download_file(),
      *>>   karate_club()
       01 DM-FETCH.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-FETCH-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> name (const std::string &): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1
      *>>   trims trailing spaces. One of downloadable().
          05 DM-FETCH-NAME PIC X(256).
          05 DM-FETCH-NAME-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> directory (const std::string & = ""): text in PIC X(256);
      *>>   -LENGTH -1 trims trailing spaces. Where the files live;
      *>>   empty means `data_home() + "/" + name`. Files already
      *>>   present there are used as they are, except DevArm and
      *>>   karate_club's verified files.
          05 DM-FETCH-DIRECTORY PIC X(256).
          05 DM-FETCH-DIRECTORY-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              -1.
      *>> Result (std::string): text in PIC X(16384); -LENGTH is its
      *>>   length in bytes. The local path of the CSV file or the
      *>>   dataset directory.
          05 DM-FETCH-RESULT PIC X(16384).
          05 DM-FETCH-RESULT-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 0.

      *>> datamunge::datasets::download_file(const std::string & url,
      *>>   const std::string & destination, const std::string & md5 =
      *>>   "") -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_download_file" USING DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Downloads `url` to `destination` with curl, unless the
      *>>   destination already exists.
      *>>
      *>> The file is downloaded into a temporary directory next to
      *>>   `destination`, checked, and only
      *>> then renamed into place. Parent directories are created as
      *>>   needed. `DATAMUNGE_DATA_OFFLINE`
      *>> is honored; `DATAMUNGE_DATA_MIRROR` is not (fetch() applies
      *>>   it).
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::datasets::download_file("https://example.org/data.csv",
      *>>   "/tmp/data.csv");
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::runtime_error: if downloading is disabled or fails, or
      *>>   the checksum differs.
       01 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> url (const std::string &): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1
      *>>   trims trailing spaces. An `https://`, `http://` or `file://`
      *>>   URL.
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-URL PIC X(256).
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-URL-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              -1.
      *>> destination (const std::string &): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH
      *>>   -1 trims trailing spaces. The final file path.
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-DESTINATION PIC X(256).
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-DESTINATION-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> md5 (const std::string & = ""): text in PIC X(256); -LENGTH -1
      *>>   trims trailing spaces. Expected lowercase hex MD5 of the
      *>>   downloaded bytes; empty skips the check.
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-MD5 PIC X(256).
          05 DM-DOWNLOAD-FILE-MD5-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              -1.

      *>> datamunge::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(double a, double b,
      *>>   std::size_t num_elements) -> Mesh1D
      *>> CALL "dm_make_uniform_mesh1d" USING DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Builds a uniform mesh of `num_elements` equal-length elements
      *>>   on [a, b].
      *>> Throws std::invalid_argument if num_elements is 0 or b <= a.
       01 DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D.
      *>> a (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D-A USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> b (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D-B USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> num_elements (std::size_t): unsigned long.
          05 DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D-NUM-ELEMENTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Mesh1D): MESH1D handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-MAKE-UNIFORM-MESH1D-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::squared_distance(const Point2D & a, const
      *>>   Point2D & b) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_squared_distance" USING DM-SQUARED-DISTANCE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Evaluate squared Euclidean distance using the original double
      *>>   arithmetic.
      *>> No finite/range validation. Differences, products and sum can
      *>>   overflow,
      *>> underflow or lose low bits; NaNs propagate according to
      *>>   floating arithmetic.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::cout << dm::geometry::squared_distance({0,0},{3,4}); //
      *>>   25
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: distance()
       01 DM-SQUARED-DISTANCE.
      *>> a (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. First point.
          05 DM-SQUARED-DISTANCE-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. Second point.
          05 DM-SQUARED-DISTANCE-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Rounded squared distance, possibly
      *>>   infinity/NaN or underflowed zero.
          05 DM-SQUARED-DISTANCE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::distance(const Point2D & a, const Point2D
      *>>   & b) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_distance" USING DM-DISTANCE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Return sqrt(squared_distance(a,b)) with its original range
      *>>   limitations.
      *>> A finite mathematical distance may overflow through the
      *>>   squared intermediate;
      *>> this convenience primitive performs no checked hypot or input
      *>>   validation.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::cout << dm::geometry::distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 5
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: squared_distance()
       01 DM-DISTANCE.
      *>> a (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. First point.
          05 DM-DISTANCE-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. Second point.
          05 DM-DISTANCE-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Rounded square root of the
      *>>   double-valued squared distance.
          05 DM-DISTANCE-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::cross(const Point2D & a, const Point2D &
      *>>   b, const Point2D & c) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_cross" USING DM-CROSS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Evaluate the original double determinant (b-a) cross (c-a).
      *>> A positive exact determinant means counterclockwise, but this
      *>>   approximation
      *>> can cancel, overflow or underflow and its returned sign is not
      *>>   certified.
      *>> No tolerance or finite validation is applied.
      *>>   orientation_sign() supplies an
      *>> exact sign for finite stored binary64 points; this primitive
      *>>   remains unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::cout << dm::geometry::cross({0,0},{1,0},{0,1}); // 1
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: orientation_sign()
       01 DM-CROSS.
      *>> a (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. First point.
          05 DM-CROSS-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. Second point.
          05 DM-CROSS-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> c (const Point2D &): POINT2D handle. Third point.
          05 DM-CROSS-C USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Rounded double determinant, possibly
      *>>   infinity/NaN or a misleading zero.
          05 DM-CROSS-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(const
      *>>   std::vector<Point2D> & points) -> std::vector<Triangle>
      *>> CALL "dm_delaunay_triangulation" USING
      *>>   DM-DELAUNAY-TRIANGULATION DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with
      *>>   exact signs.
      *>> Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout.
      *>>   Finite coordinate
      *>> validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce
      *>>   to earliest ids;
      *>> collinear/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces.
      *>>   Uses Bowyer-Watson
      *>> cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any
      *>>   finite super-triangle
      *>> whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All
      *>>   orientation/incircle signs
      *>> are exact for finite stored binary64, including
      *>>   subnormals/extreme scales.
      *>> Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in
      *>>   DelaunayTrace.
      *>> Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference
      *>>   original ids. Finite
      *>> output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No
      *>>   boundary constraints,
      *>> holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is
      *>>   supplied. Input unchanged;
      *>> O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records/u
      *>>   distinct sites with
      *>> ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working/output
      *>>   storage, no shared
      *>> state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is
      *>>   bounded per binary64 query.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation({{0,0},{1,0},{.5,.000001}}).size());
      *>>   // 1
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - A. Bowyer (1981), Computing Dirichlet tessellations,
      *>>   Computer Journal 24(2),162–166,
      *>>   doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162.
      *>> - D. F. Watson (1981), Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay
      *>>   tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes, Computer
      *>>   Journal 24(2),167–172, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167. This
      *>>   variant uses symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic
      *>>   signs, earliest duplicate identities and ordered cavity
      *>>   edges.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Any nonfinite coordinate, even on
      *>>   short input.
      *>> - std::length_error: Counter arithmetic or container capacity
      *>>   exceeded.
      *>> - std::logic_error: Internal cavity topology invariant fails;
      *>>   no partial mesh returned.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: Allocation fails.
      *>>
      *>> See also: delaunay_trace(), incircle_sign()
       01 DM-DELAUNAY-TRIANGULATION.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Borrowed finite planar sites, possibly
      *>>   empty/duplicate/collinear.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRIANGULATION-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<Triangle>): VECTOR-TRIANGLE handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned complete finite mesh of the distinct sites'
      *>>   convex hull, or no faces for collinearity.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRIANGULATION-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::delaunay_trace(const std::vector<Point2D>
      *>>   & points, std::size_t max_points = 1000000, std::size_t
      *>>   max_tests = 10000000, std::size_t max_entries = 1000000) ->
      *>>   DelaunayTrace
      *>> CALL "dm_delaunay_trace_2" USING DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2 DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the
      *>>   same solver.
      *>> No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record
      *>>   cap and all
      *>> allocation. max_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus
      *>>   hull-side calls, checked
      *>> before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one
      *>>   per insertion
      *>> plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including
      *>>   ghosts, checked
      *>> before retaining the event. Points/maps/final output and
      *>>   temporary cavities are
      *>> excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or
      *>>   wall time. Zero
      *>> disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity/event sizes)
      *>>   additional history,
      *>> at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns
      *>>   no partial result.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace({{0,0},{4,0},{4,4},{0,4},{2,2}},5,10);
      *>> std::print("{}
      *>>   {}\n",t.triangles.size(),t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests);
      *>>   // 4 10
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - Bowyer (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162; Watson (1981),
      *>>   doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167; records the symbolic-boundary
      *>>   variant rather than a geometric replay.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Any nonfinite coordinate.
      *>> - std::length_error: Optional cap, count arithmetic or
      *>>   container capacity exceeded.
      *>> - std::logic_error: Internal cavity invariant fails.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: Allocation fails.
      *>>
      *>> See also: DelaunayTrace, delaunay_triangulation()
       01 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Finite original coordinates, unchanged.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_points (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Complete
      *>>   original-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-MAX-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_tests (std::size_t = 10000000): unsigned long. Cumulative
      *>>   complete-scan conflict cap, default 10000000; zero disables.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-MAX-TESTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_entries (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Cumulative
      *>>   event-entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-MAX-ENTRIES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DelaunayTrace): DELAUNAY-TRACE handle (new; close it).
      *>>   Owned points, original-id maps, finite mesh, actual events
      *>>   and work.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-TRACE-2-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::delaunay_analyze(const
      *>>   std::vector<Point2D> & points, const std::vector<Triangle> &
      *>>   triangles, std::size_t max_tests = 1000000) ->
      *>>   DelaunayAnalysis
      *>> CALL "dm_delaunay_analyze" USING DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Measure a supplied mesh and test every nonvertex site against
      *>>   every circle.
      *>> Performs the structural checks on DelaunayAnalysis first. The
      *>>   complete prospective
      *>> circle count faces*(unique_sites-3) is checked before circle
      *>>   queries; zero cap
      *>> disables only that limit. O(n log(n+1)+f log(f+1)+fu) work and
      *>>   O(n+f) storage;
      *>> minimum angles are floating diagnostics and are not certified
      *>>   bounds. This is a
      *>> local property analysis, not a global embedding/coverage
      *>>   certificate.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D>
      *>>   p{{0,0},{1,0},{1,1},{0,1}};
      *>> const auto
      *>>   a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p));
      *>> std::print("{} {}
      *>>   {}\n",a.edges,a.boundary_edges,a.circle_tests); // 5 4 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Nonfinite coordinates,
      *>>   bad/degenerate/non-CCW/duplicate faces or nonmanifold edges.
      *>> - std::length_error: Circle-count arithmetic or optional cap
      *>>   exceeded.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: Allocation fails.
      *>>
      *>> See also: DelaunayAnalysis, delaunay_triangulation()
       01 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Finite original sites, unchanged.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> triangles (const std::vector<Triangle> &): VECTOR-TRIANGLE
      *>>   handle. Finite CCW faces with valid original indices,
      *>>   unchanged.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE-TRIANGLES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_tests (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Complete
      *>>   exact-circle query cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE-MAX-TESTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DelaunayAnalysis): DELAUNAY-ANALYSIS handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned scalar topology, actual query/violation
      *>>   counts and approximate angle.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-ANALYZE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(std::size_t count,
      *>>   std::uint32_t seed = 20250728, double extent = 10.0,
      *>>   std::size_t max_points = 1000000) -> std::vector<Point2D>
      *>> CALL "dm_delaunay_uniform_cloud" USING
      *>>   DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Generate the report's seeded uniform square-cloud control in
      *>>   the core.
      *>> Uses mt19937(seed) and
      *>>   uniform_real_distribution<double>(0,extent), x then y
      *>> for each point. Same-library runs reproduce the original
      *>>   report's seed20250728
      *>> and 30-site square when defaults are used. Standard
      *>>   distribution algorithms
      *>> need not match across C++ libraries, so the seed is not a
      *>>   universal byte fixture.
      *>> Coincident draws are allowed and later triangulation
      *>>   deduplicates them.
      *>> O(count) work/storage, owned output, no global RNG or shared
      *>>   mutation.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(30).size());
      *>>   // 30
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Nonpositive or nonfinite extent.
      *>> - std::length_error: Optional count cap or container capacity
      *>>   exceeded before allocation.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: Allocation fails.
      *>>
      *>> See also: delaunay_trace()
       01 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 1.
      *>> count (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of original
      *>>   generated records, zero allowed.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-COUNT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> seed (std::uint32_t = 20250728): unsigned int. mt19937 seed,
      *>>   default 20250728.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-SEED USAGE BINARY-LONG UNSIGNED.
      *>> extent (double = 10.0): double. Positive finite square extent,
      *>>   default 10; checked even for count zero.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-EXTENT USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> max_points (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Optional
      *>>   complete output-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-MAX-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<Point2D>): VECTOR-POINT2D handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned generated points in [0,extent) on each
      *>>   axis.
          05 DM-DELAUNAY-UNIFORM-CLOUD-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fem::make_rectangular_mesh2d(double x0, double y0,
      *>>   double x1, double y1, std::size_t nx, std::size_t ny) ->
      *>>   Mesh2D
      *>> CALL "dm_make_rectangular_mesh2d" USING
      *>>   DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Builds a structured rectangular mesh on [x0, x1] x [y0, y1]:
      *>>   nx * ny grid cells,
      *>> each split into two counterclockwise triangles, for a total of
      *>>   nx*ny*2 triangles and
      *>> (nx+1)*(ny+1) nodes, ordered row-major (x fastest, then y).
      *>> Throws std::invalid_argument if nx or ny is 0, or if x1 <= x0
      *>>   or y1 <= y0.
       01 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D.
      *>> x0 (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-X0 USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> y0 (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-Y0 USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> x1 (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-X1 USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> y1 (double): double.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-Y1 USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> nx (std::size_t): unsigned long.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-NX USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> ny (std::size_t): unsigned long.
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-NY USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Mesh2D): MESH2D handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-MAKE-RECTANGULAR-MESH2D-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fem::make_mesh2d_from_points(const
      *>>   std::vector<datamunge::geometry::Point2D> & points) ->
      *>>   Mesh2D
      *>> CALL "dm_make_mesh2d_from_points" USING
      *>>   DM-MAKE-MESH2D-FROM-POINTS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Builds a mesh over the convex hull of an arbitrary point cloud
      *>>   via Delaunay
      *>> triangulation (datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation) --
      *>>   useful for
      *>> unstructured domains a structured grid does not fit.
       01 DM-MAKE-MESH2D-FROM-POINTS.
      *>> points (const std::vector<datamunge::geometry::Point2D> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
          05 DM-MAKE-MESH2D-FROM-POINTS-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Mesh2D): MESH2D handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-MAKE-MESH2D-FROM-POINTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fem::boundary_nodes(const Mesh2D & mesh) ->
      *>>   std::vector<int>
      *>> CALL "dm_boundary_nodes" USING DM-BOUNDARY-NODES DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Node indices lying on the mesh's outer boundary: every node
      *>>   incident to a boundary
      *>> edge (an edge belonging to exactly one triangle). Works
      *>>   generically for both a
      *>> structured rectangular mesh (the domain's four sides) and an
      *>>   unstructured Delaunay
      *>> mesh (the convex hull), returned in ascending order with no
      *>>   duplicates.
       01 DM-BOUNDARY-NODES.
      *>> mesh (const Mesh2D &): MESH2D handle.
          05 DM-BOUNDARY-NODES-MESH USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<int>): VECTOR-INT handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-BOUNDARY-NODES-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::bounding_box(const std::vector<Point2D> &
      *>>   points) -> BoundingBox
      *>> CALL "dm_bounding_box" USING DM-BOUNDING-BOX DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The axis-aligned bounding box of `points`. Throws
      *>>   std::invalid_argument for an
      *>> empty point set.
       01 DM-BOUNDING-BOX.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
          05 DM-BOUNDING-BOX-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (BoundingBox): BOUNDING-BOX handle (new; close it).
          05 DM-BOUNDING-BOX-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::closest_pair(const std::vector<Point2D> &
      *>>   points) -> ClosestPairResult
      *>> CALL "dm_closest_pair" USING DM-CLOSEST-PAIR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Return the closest point copies and finite-double distance
      *>>   using the checked all-pairs kernel.
      *>> Validates at least two finite points, visits every original
      *>>   i<j pair and compares
      *>> widened hypot distances. Equal rounded distances keep the
      *>>   lexicographically
      *>> first index pair. Duplicates are valid distinct records and
      *>>   can yield zero.
      *>> No early stop, sorting or spatial pruning; O(n^2) work and
      *>>   O(1) auxiliary storage.
      *>> No input mutation/shared state. Widened comparison can change
      *>>   former false
      *>> zero/infinite results and last-bit ties; it is not an
      *>>   exact-real predicate.
      *>> The old call/result layout remains; indexed/traced variants
      *>>   retain input ids.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto r=dm::geometry::closest_pair({{0,0},{3,4},{0,1}});
      *>> std::print("{}\n",r.distance); // 1
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If point count is too small or any
      *>>   coordinate is nonfinite.
      *>> - std::length_error: If complete pair count cannot fit size_t.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If arithmetic/counters or the selected
      *>>   legacy distance cannot fit.
      *>>
      *>> See also: closest_pair_indexed(), closest_pair_trace()
       01 DM-CLOSEST-PAIR.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Borrowed original finite planar coordinates, length at least
      *>>   two.
          05 DM-CLOSEST-PAIR-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (ClosestPairResult): CLOSEST-PAIR-RESULT handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned selected point copies and checked
      *>>   finite-double measured distance.
          05 DM-CLOSEST-PAIR-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::convex_hull(std::vector<Point2D> points)
      *>>   -> std::vector<Point2D>
      *>> CALL "dm_convex_hull" USING DM-CONVEX-HULL DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Compute Andrew's strict monotone-chain hull using exact
      *>>   binary64 orientation signs.
      *>> Validates all coordinates before internal indexed copies or
      *>>   sorting; the
      *>> original by-value argument copy occurs at the call boundary.
      *>>   Exact coordinate
      *>> equality removes duplicates (including signed zero); the
      *>>   earliest matching
      *>> original record supplies its representation. Returns
      *>>   lexicographic-minimum
      *>> first, counterclockwise, no repeated endpoint and no collinear
      *>>   edge interiors.
      *>> Empty/singleton/two-point/collinear outputs follow
      *>>   ConvexHullAnalysis. O(n log n)
      *>> comparisons plus O(n) bounded exact-sign tests, O(n)
      *>>   scratch/output, no global
      *>> state. The legacy by-value signature is retained and has no
      *>>   optional caps.
      *>> Other geometry routines that use cross() retain their own
      *>>   floating contracts.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   h=dm::geometry::convex_hull({{0,0},{2,0},{2,2},{0,2},{1,1}});
      *>> std::cout << h.size(); // 4
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - A. M. Andrew (1979), Another efficient algorithm for convex
      *>>   hulls in two
      *>>   dimensions, https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Any nonfinite coordinate.
      *>> - std::length_error: Work/container size exhaustion.
      *>>
      *>> See also: convex_hull_analyze(), convex_hull_trace(),
      *>>   orientation_sign()
       01 DM-CONVEX-HULL.
      *>> points (std::vector<Point2D>): VECTOR-POINT2D handle. Finite
      *>>   planar records, copied by value as in the original API.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<Point2D>): VECTOR-POINT2D handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned strict hull of the represented coordinates.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::convex_hull_analyze(const
      *>>   std::vector<Point2D> & points, ConvexHullOptions options =
      *>>   {}) -> ConvexHullAnalysis
      *>> CALL "dm_convex_hull_analyze" USING DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Execute the same exact-sign hull kernel with identities and
      *>>   actual work.
      *>> Same ordering, degeneracy and complexity contract as
      *>>   convex_hull(). Full finite
      *>> validation precedes the complete max_points cap and scratch
      *>>   allocation. No
      *>> events or completed-chain snapshots are retained; event cap is
      *>>   ignored.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   a=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze({{0,0},{1,0},{2,0}});
      *>> std::cout << a.hull.size() << ' ' << a.work.distinct_points;
      *>>   // 2 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3
      *>>   .
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Nonfinite coordinate.
      *>> - std::length_error: Complete point cap or work/container
      *>>   exhaustion.
      *>>
      *>> See also: convex_hull(), ConvexHullOptions
       01 DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              1.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Finite original records; not mutated.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> options (ConvexHullOptions = {}): CONVEX-HULL-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Complete original-record cap, zero unlimited; defaults one
      *>>   million.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (ConvexHullAnalysis): CONVEX-HULL-ANALYSIS handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned hull, earliest original indices and actual
      *>>   counters.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-ANALYZE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::convex_hull_trace(const
      *>>   std::vector<Point2D> & points, ConvexHullOptions options =
      *>>   {}) -> ConvexHullTrace
      *>> CALL "dm_convex_hull_trace" USING DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Capture the hull kernel's actual stack events without replay
      *>>   or quadratic snapshots.
      *>> Same computation/work as analyze, plus O(n) owned sorted
      *>>   records, compact
      *>> test/pop/push edits and completed lower/upper chains. Positive
      *>>   max_events is
      *>> checked online on actual records, including pushes; zero
      *>>   disables the cap.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto t=dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,0}});
      *>> std::cout << t.events.size(); // 4
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3
      *>>   .
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Nonfinite coordinate.
      *>> - std::length_error: Point/event cap or work/container
      *>>   exhaustion.
      *>>
      *>> See also: convex_hull_analyze(), HullEvent
       01 DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              1.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
      *>>   Finite original records; not mutated.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> options (ConvexHullOptions = {}): CONVEX-HULL-OPTIONS handle.
      *>>   Complete point and actual event caps.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (ConvexHullTrace): CONVEX-HULL-TRACE handle (new; close
      *>>   it). Owned actual sorted coordinates, identities, work and
      *>>   scan edits.
          05 DM-CONVEX-HULL-TRACE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::sample_gaussian_points(std::size_t count
      *>>   = 40, std::uint32_t seed = 20240728, std::size_t max_points
      *>>   = 1000000) -> std::vector<Point2D>
      *>> CALL "dm_sample_gaussian_points" USING
      *>>   DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draw unsnapped independent standard-normal x,y pairs with a
      *>>   local 32-bit MT stream.
      *>> Reproduces the original hull report's
      *>>   mt19937/normal_distribution call order,
      *>> including its cached normal variate. Distribution mappings can
      *>>   differ between
      *>> standard libraries; a seed alone does not promise portable
      *>>   coordinate bits.
      *>> Complete count cap precedes allocation. Empty count is valid.
      *>>   O(count) expected
      *>> work/storage; no global RNG or mutation. Finite draws are
      *>>   checked before return.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::cout <<
      *>>   dm::geometry::sample_gaussian_points(40,20240728).size(); //
      *>>   40
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C++ normal_distribution specification:
      *>>   https://eel.is/c++draft/rand.dist.norm.normal .
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::length_error: Complete draw cap/container exhaustion.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: A generated coordinate is nonfinite.
      *>>
      *>> See also: convex_hull(), random_point_cloud()
       01 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 0 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 0.
          05 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 0.
      *>> count (std::size_t = 40): unsigned long. Number of points,
      *>>   default forty.
          05 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS-COUNT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> seed (std::uint32_t = 20240728): unsigned int. Local mt19937
      *>>   seed, default 20240728.
          05 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS-SEED USAGE BINARY-LONG UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_points (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Complete
      *>>   draw cap, default one million; zero unlimited.
          05 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS-MAX-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<Point2D>): VECTOR-POINT2D handle (new;
      *>>   close it). Owned unsnapped normal point cloud.
          05 DM-SAMPLE-GAUSSIAN-POINTS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::terrain_colors(std::size_t n) ->
      *>>   std::vector<RGBA>
      *>> CALL "dm_terrain_colors" USING DM-TERRAIN-COLORS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> `n` colours from green through yellow and brown to near-white,
      *>>   as R's `terrain.colors(n)`.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `n` is 0.
       01 DM-TERRAIN-COLORS.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of colours (at least
      *>>   1).
          05 DM-TERRAIN-COLORS-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<RGBA>): VECTOR-RGB handle (new; close it).
      *>>   The palette, low to high.
          05 DM-TERRAIN-COLORS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::diverging_colors(std::size_t n) ->
      *>>   std::vector<RGBA>
      *>> CALL "dm_diverging_colors" USING DM-DIVERGING-COLORS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> `n` colours from dark blue through white to dark red, a
      *>>   diverging palette for signed
      *>> values such as correlations (ColorBrewer's RdBu, reversed so
      *>>   that low is blue).
      *>>
      *>> Interpolates linearly between the seven RdBu anchor colours;
      *>>   an odd `n` puts pure
      *>> near-white (247, 247, 247) in the middle, so with `z_min =
      *>>   -z_max` in RPlot::image() zero
      *>> maps to white.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto pal = dm::graphics::diverging_colors(3);
      *>> std::print("{} {} {}\n", pal[0].r, pal[1].g, pal[2].r); // 33
      *>>   247 178
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C. Brewer, ColorBrewer 2.0 (RdBu scheme):
      *>>   https://colorbrewer2.org
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `n` is 0.
      *>>
      *>> See also: terrain_colors(), RPlot::image()
       01 DM-DIVERGING-COLORS.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of colours (at least 1;
      *>>   1 gives the white midpoint).
          05 DM-DIVERGING-COLORS-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<RGBA>): VECTOR-RGB handle (new; close it).
      *>>   The palette, low (blue) to high (red).
          05 DM-DIVERGING-COLORS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::viridis_colors(std::size_t n) ->
      *>>   std::vector<RGBA>
      *>> CALL "dm_viridis_colors" USING DM-VIRIDIS-COLORS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> `n` colours of the viridis scale, from dark purple through
      *>>   blue and green to yellow,
      *>> matching R's `viridisLite::viridis(n)` at n = 10.
      *>>
      *>> Viridis is perceptually uniform, so equal steps in value look
      *>>   like equal steps in colour,
      *>> and it stays readable in greyscale and for the common forms of
      *>>   colour blindness. Colours are
      *>> interpolated linearly between viridis(10)'s ten anchors.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto pal = dm::graphics::viridis_colors(10);
      *>> std::print("{} {} {}\n", pal[0].r, pal[5].g, pal[9].b); // 68
      *>>   158 37
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - S. van der Walt and N. Smith (2015). "mpl colourmaps"
      *>>   (viridis): https://bids.github.io/colormap/
      *>> - viridisLite: https://sjmgarnier.github.io/viridisLite/
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If `n` is 0.
      *>>
      *>> See also: value_colors(), terrain_colors()
       01 DM-VIRIDIS-COLORS.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Number of colours (at least
      *>>   1).
          05 DM-VIRIDIS-COLORS-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<RGBA>): VECTOR-RGB handle (new; close it).
      *>>   The palette, low (purple) to high (yellow).
          05 DM-VIRIDIS-COLORS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::graphics::value_colors(const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   values, std::vector<RGBA> palette = {}, double z_min = ...,
      *>>   double z_max = ...) -> std::vector<RGBA>
      *>> CALL "dm_value_colors" USING DM-VALUE-COLORS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> One colour per value on a continuous scale, for colouring
      *>>   scatter points by a
      *>> numeric variable (ggplot2's `scale_colour_viridis_c()`).
      *>>
      *>> Each value is mapped linearly from [`z_min`, `z_max`] to the
      *>>   palette and rounded to the
      *>> nearest entry; values outside are clamped, NaN values get grey
      *>>   (190, 190, 190), and a zero
      *>> range maps everything to the middle colour. Pass the result to
      *>>   RPlot::points_colored().
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto cols = dm::graphics::value_colors({0.0, 0.5, 1.0},
      *>>   dm::graphics::viridis_colors(3));
      *>> std::print("{} {} {}\n", cols[0].r, cols[1].r, cols[2].r); //
      *>>   68 35 253
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> See also: viridis_colors(), RPlot::points_colored()
       01 DM-VALUE-COLORS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Values to colour.
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> palette (std::vector<RGBA> = {}): VECTOR-RGB handle. Colours
      *>>   from low to high (default viridis_colors(256)).
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-PALETTE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> z_min (double): double. Value of the first colour; NaN (the
      *>>   default) uses the smallest value.
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-Z-MIN USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> z_max (double): double. Value of the last colour; NaN (the
      *>>   default) uses the largest value.
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-Z-MAX USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<RGBA>): VECTOR-RGB handle (new; close it).
      *>>   One colour per value.
          05 DM-VALUE-COLORS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::dtw_trace_entries(std::size_t n,
      *>>   std::size_t m) -> std::size_t
      *>> CALL "dm_dtw_trace_entries" USING DM-DTW-TRACE-ENTRIES
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Compute the complete logical capture size without allocating a
      *>>   DTW matrix.
      *>> Checks positive lengths, int path-index representability and
      *>>   size_t geometry.
      *>> A representable count does not promise that the corresponding
      *>>   buffers fit memory.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::dtw_trace_entries(3,4)); // 31
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For either zero length.
      *>> - std::length_error: For index, product or capture-count
      *>>   overflow.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dynamic_time_warping_trace()
       01 DM-DTW-TRACE-ENTRIES.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive first length, at most
      *>>   INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DTW-TRACE-ENTRIES-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> m (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive second length, at
      *>>   most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DTW-TRACE-ENTRIES-M USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::size_t): unsigned long. n+m+2*n*m logical
      *>>   entries, excluding result paths and scratch rows.
          05 DM-DTW-TRACE-ENTRIES-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_work(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b) ->
      *>>   DTWComputation
      *>> CALL "dm_dynamic_time_warping_work" USING
      *>>   DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-WORK DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work
      *>>   without cost history.
      *>> Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No
      *>>   band, slope bound,
      *>> step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters.
      *>>   Finite ties choose
      *>> diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. O(n*m) recurrence
      *>>   time, O(n*m+n+m)
      *>> scratch/output, with only two double cost rows and n*m
      *>>   predecessor bytes.
      *>> Complete input/geometry validation precedes allocation.
      *>>   Positive overflowing
      *>> off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum
      *>>   is rejected.
      *>> Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to
      *>>   the returned
      *>> distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share
      *>>   no mutable state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See DTWWork and DTWComputation for executed work examples.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), Dynamic programming algorithm
      *>>   optimization for
      *>>   spoken word recognition, IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49.
      *>>     doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
      *>>   This is the unrestricted scalar absolute-loss variant,
      *>>     without the paper's window/slope rules.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For empty or nonfinite input.
      *>> - std::length_error: For geometry/index/container bounds.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If the final minimum is not finite
      *>>   double.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: If scratch or result storage cannot be
      *>>   allocated.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dynamic_time_warping(), dynamic_time_warping_trace()
       01 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-WORK.
      *>> a (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-WORK-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-WORK-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DTWComputation): DTW-COMPUTATION handle (new; close
      *>>   it). Independent optimal result and counts, with no retained
      *>>   input/cost history.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-WORK-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b) ->
      *>>   DTWResult
      *>> CALL "dm_dynamic_time_warping" USING DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Preserve the original two-argument unrestricted absolute-loss
      *>>   DTW entry point.
      *>> Same validation, ownership, deterministic ties, numerical
      *>>   limits and complexity
      *>> as dynamic_time_warping_work(); discards only its work
      *>>   counters. This distance
      *>> is not a metric: unequal stretched sequences can have zero
      *>>   cost and the triangle
      *>> inequality can fail. Equal-length diagonal alignment bounds it
      *>>   by rigid L1 loss.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See DTWResult for the executed original stretched-sequence
      *>>   example.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49,
      *>>   doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For empty or nonfinite input.
      *>> - std::length_error: For geometry/index/container bounds.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If the final optimum is not finite
      *>>   double.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: If scratch or result storage cannot be
      *>>   allocated.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dynamic_time_warping_work(), dtw_path_cost()
       01 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING.
      *>> a (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite second sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DTWResult): DTW-RESULT handle (new; close it). Owned
      *>>   optimal distance and inclusive zero-based parallel int
      *>>   paths.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_trace(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b,
      *>>   std::size_t max_entries = 1000000) -> DTWTrace
      *>> CALL "dm_dynamic_time_warping_trace" USING
      *>>   DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Capture actual positive-cell costs and predecessor choices
      *>>   from the shared DTW kernel.
      *>> Validates complete finite inputs/geometry before checking the
      *>>   optional complete
      *>> capture budget, then copies inputs and runs once. Owns every
      *>>   snapshot. No partial
      *>> trace/result escapes on failure. Same variant/ties/numerical
      *>>   rules as ordinary DTW.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> See DTWTrace and DTWPredecessor for executed capture examples.
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49,
      *>>   doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For empty or nonfinite input, before
      *>>   budget checks.
      *>> - std::length_error: For geometry/index/container/capture
      *>>   limits.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If the final optimum is not finite
      *>>   double.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: If owned or working storage cannot be
      *>>   allocated.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dtw_trace_entries(), dynamic_time_warping_work()
       01 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> a (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first input, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite second input, length at most INT_MAX.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> max_entries (std::size_t = 1000000): unsigned long. Complete
      *>>   logical entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables only cap.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE-MAX-ENTRIES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DTWTrace): DTW-TRACE handle (new; close it).
      *>>   Independent input, costs, directions, optimal result, counts
      *>>   and capture cost.
          05 DM-DYNAMIC-TIME-WARPING-TRACE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::dtw_path_cost(const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   a, const std::vector<double> & b, const std::vector<int> &
      *>>   path_a, const std::vector<int> & path_b) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_dtw_path_cost" USING DM-DTW-PATH-COST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Validate a complete monotone witness and sum its unshifted
      *>>   absolute losses.
      *>> Checks all finite input and path geometry before numerical
      *>>   accumulation: equal
      *>> nonempty path lengths, in-bounds indices, exact endpoints and
      *>>   unit advances
      *>> (1,0), (0,1), (1,1). This certifies feasibility/cost, not
      *>>   optimality. O(n+m+L)
      *>> validation/sum, no allocations, input unchanged; no shared
      *>>   mutable state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto r=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping({1,2},{1,2});
      *>> std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::dtw_path_cost({1,2},{1,2},r.path_a,r.path_b));
      *>>   // 0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For invalid finite inputs or path
      *>>   shape/indices/steps/endpoints.
      *>> - std::length_error: For input index/geometry bounds.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: If the supplied path's accumulated cost
      *>>   is not finite double.
      *>>
      *>> See also: DTWResult, plot_dtw_alignment()
       01 DM-DTW-PATH-COST.
      *>> a (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first sequence.
          05 DM-DTW-PATH-COST-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite second sequence.
          05 DM-DTW-PATH-COST-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> path_a (const std::vector<int> &): VECTOR-INT handle. Borrowed
      *>>   signed first indices, zero through a.size()-1.
          05 DM-DTW-PATH-COST-PATH-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> path_b (const std::vector<int> &): VECTOR-INT handle. Borrowed
      *>>   parallel signed second indices, zero through b.size()-1.
          05 DM-DTW-PATH-COST-PATH-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Finite forward-order sum of absolute
      *>>   differences on the supplied witness.
          05 DM-DTW-PATH-COST-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & a, const std::vector<double> & b,
      *>>   const DTWResult & result, double offset =
      *>>   1.6000000000000001, std::size_t max_pairs = 4096) ->
      *>>   graphics::RPlot
      *>> CALL "dm_plot_dtw_alignment" USING DM-PLOT-DTW-ALIGNMENT
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Draw two scalar series and their validated DTW correspondence
      *>>   segments natively.
      *>> Costs use unshifted inputs; only drawing adds offset to a and
      *>>   subtracts it from b.
      *>> Requires result.distance to equal the validated forward
      *>>   witness sum exactly;
      *>> it does not independently prove optimality. Validates complete
      *>>   inputs/witness,
      *>> finite plotted coordinates and optional path-pair cap before
      *>>   drawing allocation.
      *>> Owns all series; no file I/O or model mutation. Concurrent
      *>>   calls share no state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto r=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping({1,2},{1,2});
      *>> const auto p=dm::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment({1,2},{1,2},r);
      *>> std::print("{}\n",p.series().size()); // 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For invalid
      *>>   inputs/witness/distance/offset.
      *>> - std::length_error: For input geometry/index limits or
      *>>   exceeded nonzero pair cap.
      *>> - std::overflow_error: For witness cost or shifted
      *>>   drawing-coordinate overflow.
      *>> - std::bad_alloc: If owned plot storage cannot be allocated.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dtw_path_cost(), graphics::RPlot
       01 DM-PLOT-DTW-ALIGNMENT.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-PLOT-DTW-ALIGNMENT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              3.
      *>> a (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first sequence.
          05 DM-PLOT-DTW-ALIGNMENT-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite second sequence.
