      *>>
      *>> The diameter of a point set: the farthest pair of points, by
      *>>   Euclidean distance.
      *>> The farthest pair is always a pair of convex hull vertices, so
      *>>   this computes the
      *>> hull first (convex_hull()) and then searches only among its
      *>>   (typically far fewer)
      *>> vertices -- brute-force O(h^2) over the hull rather than the
      *>>   classical O(h)
      *>> two-pointer rotating-calipers walk, the same "correct and
      *>>   simple over asymptotically
      *>> optimal" tradeoff this module already makes for
      *>>   closest_pair(). Throws
      *>> std::invalid_argument for fewer than 2 distinct points.
       01 DM-POLYGON-DIAMETER.
      *>> points (const std::vector<Point2D> &): VECTOR-POINT2D handle.
          05 DM-POLYGON-DIAMETER-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (DiameterResult): DIAMETER-RESULT handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-POLYGON-DIAMETER-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::geometry::minimum_bounding_rectangle(const
      *>>   std::vector<Point2D> & points) -> MinimumBoundingRectangle
      *>> CALL "dm_minimum_bounding_rectangle" USING
      *>>   DM-MINIMUM-BOUNDING-RECTANGLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> The minimum-AREA bounding rectangle of a point set (not
      *>>   necessarily axis-aligned).
      *>> By a classical theorem, the optimal rectangle always has one
      *>>   side collinear with a
      *>> convex hull edge -- so this computes the hull, then for each
      *>>   hull edge, measures the
      *>> bounding rectangle aligned with that edge's direction
      *>>   (projecting every hull point
      *>> onto the edge direction and its perpendicular), keeping the
      *>>   smallest-area result.
      *>> O(h^2) (h = hull size): the classical rotating-calipers
      *>>   formulation does this in
      *>> O(h) via incrementally-advanced support points, but
      *>>   re-projecting from scratch for
      *>> every edge is far simpler to get right and, again, this
