      *>> datamunge::optim::expected_improvement(double mean, double sd,
      *>>   double incumbent, double exploration = 0.0) -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_expected_improvement" USING DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Expected improvement of a Gaussian prediction below an
      *>>   incumbent (minimisation).
      *>>
      *>> For `Y\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)` and target
      *>>   `t=\text{incumbent}-\xi`,
      *>> `\operatorname{E}\max(t-Y,0)=(t-\mu)\Phi(u)+\sigma\varphi(u)`
      *>>   with `u=(t-\mu)/\sigma`.
      *>>
      *>> `\mu`.
      *>>
      *>> `\sigma\ge0`; 0 gives `\max(t-\mu,0)`.
      *>>
      *>> `\xi\ge0` an improvement must exceed.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{:.4f}\n", dm::optim::expected_improvement(0.0,
      *>>   1.0, 0.0)); // 0.3989 = phi(0)
      *>> ```
       01 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 3.
      *>> mean (double): double. Predicted mean
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-MEAN USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> sd (double): double. Predicted standard deviation
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-SD USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> incumbent (double): double. Smallest value observed so far.
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-INCUMBENT USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> exploration (double = 0.0): double. Margin
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-EXPLORATION USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (double): double. The expected improvement (never
      *>>   negative).
          05 DM-EXPECTED-IMPROVEMENT-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::optim::probability_of_improvement(double mean,
      *>>   double sd, double incumbent, double exploration = 0.0) ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_probability_of_improvement" USING
      *>>   DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Probability that a Gaussian prediction falls below incumbent
      *>>   − exploration.
      *>>
      *>> `\mu`.
      *>>
      *>> `\xi\ge0`.
      *>>
      *>> `\Phi\big((\text{incumbent}-\xi-\mu)/\sigma\big)`.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> std::print("{:.4f}\n",
      *>>   dm::optim::probability_of_improvement(1.0, 1.0, 0.0)); //
      *>>   0.1587
      *>> ```
       01 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 3.
      *>> mean (double): double. Predicted mean
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-MEAN USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> sd (double): double. Predicted standard deviation; 0 gives 0
      *>>   or 1.
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-SD USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> incumbent (double): double. Smallest value observed so far.
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-INCUMBENT USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> exploration (double = 0.0): double. Margin
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-EXPLORATION USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (double): double.
          05 DM-PROBABILITY-OF-IMPROVEMENT-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::stats::one_way_anova(const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   values, const std::vector<std::size_t> & group_sizes) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_one_way_anova" USING DM-ONE-WAY-ANOVA DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Classical equal-variance one-way ANOVA, like R
      *>>   oneway.test(var.equal=TRUE).
      *>>
      *>> Tests equality of group means with between/within mean-square
      *>>   ratio. Independent normal
      *>> observations and a common within-group population variance
      *>>   give the exact F reference. This is
      *>> not Welch ANOVA. O(N) work, O(1) auxiliary storage; input
      *>>   unchanged, no omission or shared
      *>> mutable state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   a=dm::stats::one_way_anova({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",a.statistic); // 27
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/oneway.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid grouping/nonfinite input, no
      *>>   residual df, zero within- group variation or arithmetic
      *>>   overflow.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-ONE-WAY-ANOVA.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Finite observations concatenated group by group.
          05 DM-ONE-WAY-ANOVA-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> group_sizes (const std::vector<std::size_t> &): VECTOR-ULONG
      *>>   handle. At least two positive sizes summing exactly to
      *>>   values.size(); N must exceed the group count.
          05 DM-ONE-WAY-ANOVA-GROUP-SIZES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=F, parameter1=k-1, parameter2=N-k
      *>>   and upper-tail p_value; no CI or group estimates.
          05 DM-ONE-WAY-ANOVA-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::kruskal_wallis_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & values, const std::vector<std::size_t>
      *>>   & group_sizes) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_kruskal_wallis_test" USING DM-KRUSKAL-WALLIS-TEST
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Tie-corrected Kruskal-Wallis rank test, like R kruskal.test().
      *>>
      *>> Ranks the pooled observations with average ties and uses the
      *>>   chi-square(k-1) approximation.
      *>> The usual exchangeability null is identical independent group
      *>>   distributions; a common-shape
      *>> shift model permits a location interpretation. It is not an
      *>>   omnibus test for every possible
      *>> distributional difference. Small groups can invalidate the
      *>>   approximation. O(N log N) work/O(N)
      *>> space, no mutation, omission or shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   h=dm::stats::kruskal_wallis_test({1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9},{3,3,3});
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",h.statistic); // 7.2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/kruskal.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid groups, nonfinite input or
      *>>   all observations tied.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-KRUSKAL-WALLIS-TEST.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Finite observations concatenated by group.
          05 DM-KRUSKAL-WALLIS-TEST-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> group_sizes (const std::vector<std::size_t> &): VECTOR-ULONG
      *>>   handle. At least two positive group sizes summing to
      *>>   values.size().
          05 DM-KRUSKAL-WALLIS-TEST-GROUP-SIZES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=H, parameter1=k-1 and upper-tail
      *>>   approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-KRUSKAL-WALLIS-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & observed, const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   expected_probabilities = {}) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_chi_squared_goodness_of_fit" USING
      *>>   DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Pearson chi-square test of specified category probabilities,
      *>>   like R chisq.test().
      *>>
      *>> Uses sum((observed-expected)^2/expected) and chi-square(k-1);
      *>>   the probabilities are fixed, not
      *>> estimated here. Expected counts must be sufficiently large for
      *>>   the approximation. Nonnegative
      *>> fractional counts are accepted algebraically but do not
      *>>   automatically define a sampling model.
      *>> O(k) work/storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state or
      *>>   omission.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto q=dm::stats::chi_squared_goodness_of_fit({10,10});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid counts/probabilities, sizes
      *>>   or zero/nonfinite total.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> observed (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   At least two finite nonnegative counts with positive finite
      *>>   total.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT-OBSERVED USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> expected_probabilities (const std::vector<double> & = {}):
      *>>   VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Empty (default) means uniform;
      *>>   otherwise positive finite probabilities matching counts and
      *>>   summing to one within 1e-10.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT-EXPECTED-PROBABILITIES USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=X-squared, parameter1=k-1 and
      *>>   approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-GOODNESS-OF-FIT-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::chi_squared_test_independence(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & table, std::size_t nrows, std::size_t
      *>>   ncols, bool correct = true) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_chi_squared_test_independence" USING
      *>>   DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Pearson chi-square test for a contingency table, like R
      *>>   chisq.test().
      *>>
      *>> Expected counts are row_sum*column_sum/total;
      *>>   df=(rows-1)(columns-1). The asymptotic reference
      *>> assumes independent multinomial/product-multinomial
      *>>   observations and adequately populated
      *>> expected cells. O(rows*columns) work and O(rows+columns)
      *>>   space; inputs unchanged, no shared
      *>> state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   q=dm::stats::chi_squared_test_independence({10,10,10,10},2,2);
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",q.statistic); // 0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/chisq.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid shape, counts/margins or
      *>>   nonfinite total.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 3.
      *>> table (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Finite nonnegative row-major counts with strictly positive
      *>>   margins; integer counts are the usual sampling model.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-TABLE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> nrows (std::size_t): unsigned long. At least two rows.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-NROWS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> ncols (std::size_t): unsigned long. At least two columns;
      *>>   table length must equal nrows*ncols.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-NCOLS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> correct (bool = true): 0 or 1. True (default) subtracts
      *>>   min(.5,abs(O-E)) from each absolute residual only for 2x2
      *>>   tables.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-CORRECT USAGE BINARY-CHAR
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=X-squared, parameter1=df,
      *>>   approximate upper-tail p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-CHI-SQUARED-TEST-INDEPENDENCE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::pearson_correlation_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y,
      *>>   Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level =
      *>>   0.94999999999999996) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_pearson_correlation_test" USING
      *>>   DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Pearson product-moment correlation test with Fisher-z
      *>>   confidence bounds.
      *>>
      *>> Uses t=r*sqrt((n-2)/(1-r^2)). The exact zero-correlation null
      *>>   reference requires independent
      *>> bivariate-normal pairs; Fisher-z intervals are approximate. At
      *>>   n=3 no confidence interval is
      *>> reported; n>=4 gets one. Perfect correlations use
      *>>   infinite-statistic limits. O(n) work, no
      *>> input mutation/omission or shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   r=dm::stats::pearson_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{2,4,6,8});
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant
      *>>   samples or invalid controls.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least three finite nonconstant first measurements.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Same-length finite nonconstant second measurements, paired
      *>>   by index.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   population correlation.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE
                 0.
             88 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=t, parameter1=n-2, estimate1=r
      *>>   and p_value; has_conf_int only for n>=4.
          05 DM-PEARSON-CORRELATION-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::spearman_correlation_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y,
      *>>   Alternative alternative = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_spearman_correlation_test" USING
      *>>   DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Spearman correlation with average ranks and a t-approximation
      *>>   p-value.
      *>>
      *>> Computes Pearson correlation on ranks. The reference is always
      *>>   t(n-2), not a small-sample
      *>> exact permutation distribution; ties receive average ranks.
      *>>   Tests zero rank correlation under
      *>> the approximation, not arbitrary independence. O(n log n)
      *>>   work/O(n) storage, no
      *>> mutation/omission or shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   r=dm::stats::spearman_correlation_test({1,2,3,4},{10,20,30,40});
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",r.estimate1); // 1.0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid lengths, nonfinite/constant
      *>>   samples or alternative.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least three finite nonconstant first measurements.
          05 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Same-length finite nonconstant paired second measurements.
          05 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   population rank correlation.
          05 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE
                 0.
             88 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=rank-correlation t,
      *>>   parameter1=n-2, estimate1=rho, approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-SPEARMAN-CORRELATION-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::kendall_tau(const std::vector<double> & x,
      *>>   const std::vector<double> & y) -> KendallTau
      *>> CALL "dm_kendall_tau" USING DM-KENDALL-TAU DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Kendall rank coefficients by Knight inversion counting.
      *>>
      *>> Sorts paired values and counts concordant, discordant and tied
      *>>   pairs in O(n log n) work/O(n)
      *>> storage. Inputs are unchanged and calls share no mutable
      *>>   state. This is a coefficient
      *>> calculation, not a hypothesis test; tau_b is zero when its
      *>>   denominator vanishes.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto k=dm::stats::kendall_tau({1,2,3,4},{1,2,3,4});
      *>> std::print("{}\n",k.concordant); // 6
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/cor.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Lengths differ, fewer than two
      *>>   values, or nonfinite observations.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-KENDALL-TAU.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite first coordinates.
          05 DM-KENDALL-TAU-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Equal-length finite second coordinates, paired by index.
          05 DM-KENDALL-TAU-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (KendallTau): KENDALL-TAU handle (new; close it). Owned
      *>>   pair counts, tau_a and tie-adjusted tau_b.
          05 DM-KENDALL-TAU-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(std::size_t a,
      *>>   std::size_t b, std::size_t c, std::size_t d, Alternative
      *>>   alternative = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_fisher_exact_test_2x2" USING DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Conditional Fisher exact test of a 2x2 table with fixed
      *>>   margins.
      *>>
      *>> Conditions on row/column totals and tests odds ratio one.
      *>>   Two-sided p-values sum probabilities
      *>> no larger than the observed table (relative ordering tolerance
      *>>   1e-7); they are not generally
      *>> twice the smaller tail. Direct tail summation avoids 1-CDF
      *>>   cancellation. Empty margins give
      *>> p=1. O(s) work/O(1) auxiliary storage for feasible support s;
      *>>   inputs unchanged, no shared
      *>> state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto f=dm::stats::fisher_exact_test_2x2(0,3,3,0);
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",f.p_value); // 0.1
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/fisher.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Count-total overflow or invalid
      *>>   alternative.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2.
      *>> Arguments to send: 4 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 4.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 4.
      *>> a (std::size_t): unsigned long. Top-left nonnegative integer
      *>>   count.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-A USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> b (std::size_t): unsigned long. Top-right count.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-B USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> c (std::size_t): unsigned long. Bottom-left count.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-C USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> d (std::size_t): unsigned long. Bottom-right count; total must
      *>>   fit size_t.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-D USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the
      *>>   odds ratio relative to one.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=a, p_value and estimate1=sample
      *>>   odds ratio a*d/(b*c), not R conditional MLE; denominator
      *>>   zero gives infinity for positive numerator or NaN for 0/0.
      *>>   No confidence interval.
          05 DM-FISHER-EXACT-TEST-2X2-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, double mean = 0.0, double sd = 1.0,
      *>>   Alternative alternative = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_ks_test_one_sample_normal" USING
      *>>   DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> One-sample KS test against a fully specified continuous normal
      *>>   CDF.
      *>>
      *>> Two-sided p-values use the Stephens corrected asymptotic
      *>>   Kolmogorov reference; one-sided
      *>> values use exp(-2*n*D^2). Mean and sd must be fixed
      *>>   independently of this sample: fitting them
      *>> here invalidates the stated calibration. This is not a
      *>>   Lilliefors test. Ties are accepted, but
      *>> continuous-null calibration is affected by rounding. O(n log
      *>>   n) work/O(n) storage, input
      *>> unchanged, no omission/shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal({-1,0,1});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.parameter1); // 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid
      *>>   mean/sd or alternative.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 1.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite observations.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> mean (double = 0.0): double. Finite prespecified normal mean,
      *>>   default zero.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-MEAN USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> sd (double = 1.0): double. Positive finite prespecified normal
      *>>   SD, default one.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-SD USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default); Greater tests empirical
      *>>   CDF above reference, Less below (opposite to a location
      *>>   alternative).
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE
                 0.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n and
      *>>   approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-NORMAL-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, double lower = 0.0, double upper =
      *>>   1.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_ks_test_one_sample_uniform" USING
      *>>   DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> One-sample KS test against a specified continuous uniform CDF.
      *>>
      *>> Same asymptotic p-value and tie caveats as
      *>>   ks_test_one_sample_normal(); limits must be
      *>> prespecified rather than estimated from x. O(n log n)
      *>>   work/O(n) storage; no mutation, omission
      *>> or shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   k=dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_uniform({.2,.4,.6,.8});
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",k.statistic); // 0.2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid sample, limits or
      *>>   alternative.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite observations.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> lower (double = 0.0): double. Finite prespecified lower bound,
      *>>   default zero.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-LOWER USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> upper (double = 1.0): double. Finite upper bound greater than
      *>>   lower, default one.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-UPPER USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Greater for empirical
      *>>   CDF above reference or Less below.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED
                 VALUE 0.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n,
      *>>   approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-UNIFORM-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::ks_test_two_sample(const std::vector<double>
      *>>   & x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative
      *>>   = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_ks_test_two_sample" USING DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Two-sample KS comparison of empirical CDFs with asymptotic
      *>>   p-values.
      *>>
      *>> Uses effective n=n_x*n_y/(n_x+n_y) in the asymptotic
      *>>   reference. This is not an exact finite-
      *>> sample calculation; ties/rounding and very small groups affect
      *>>   calibration. O(N log N)
      *>> work/O(N) storage; inputs unchanged, no omission/shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto k=dm::stats::ks_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{1,2,3});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",k.statistic); // 0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ks.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Empty/nonfinite samples or invalid
      *>>   alternative.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first sample.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite independent second sample.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default); Greater tests F_x above
      *>>   F_y, Less below, so Greater corresponds to stochastically
      *>>   smaller x.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=D or D+/D-, parameter1=n_x,
      *>>   parameter2=n_y and approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-KS-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::shapiro_francia_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_shapiro_francia_test" USING DM-SHAPIRO-FRANCIA-TEST
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Shapiro-Francia composite normality test, like R
      *>>   nortest::sf.test().
      *>>
      *>> Squared correlation of sorted observations with Blom normal
      *>>   scores, using Royston (1993) log-
      *>> normal p-value approximation. Tests some normal distribution;
      *>>   a large p-value does not prove
      *>> normality. Rounding/ties affect calibration. O(n log n)
      *>>   work/O(n) storage; input unchanged, no
      *>> omission/shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto s=dm::stats::shapiro_francia_test({1,2,3,4,5});
      *>> std::print("{}\n",s.has_conf_int); // false
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://search.r-project.org/CRAN/refmans/nortest/html/sf.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid sample size, nonfinite input
      *>>   or constant sample.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-SHAPIRO-FRANCIA-TEST.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Between
      *>>   5 and 5000 finite, nonconstant observations.
          05 DM-SHAPIRO-FRANCIA-TEST-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=estimate1=W-prime and approximate
      *>>   p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-SHAPIRO-FRANCIA-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::p_adjust(const std::vector<double> & p,
      *>>   PAdjustMethod method = BH) -> std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_p_adjust_2" USING DM-P-ADJUST-2 DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Adjust a vector of p-values for multiple comparisons, matching
      *>>   R's p.adjust().
      *>>
      *>> - Bonferroni: p[i] * n, capped at 1 -- simplest and most
      *>>   conservative FWER control.
      *>> - Holm: step-down Bonferroni (Holm-Bonferroni) -- uniformly
      *>>   more powerful than
      *>>   plain Bonferroni while still controlling FWER, no extra
      *>>     assumptions.
      *>> - Hochberg: step-up FWER control -- more powerful than Holm,
      *>>   but assumes the test
      *>>   statistics are independent (or positively dependent).
      *>> - Hommel: step-up FWER control, uniformly more powerful than
      *>>   Hochberg under the
      *>>   same independence assumption; the most expensive to compute
      *>>     (O(n^2)). For n == 2
      *>>   this degenerates to (and is computed as) Hochberg, matching
      *>>     R.
      *>> - BH (Benjamini-Hochberg, aka "fdr" in R): controls the false
      *>>   discovery rate under
      *>>   independence or positive regression dependence -- the
      *>>     standard choice for large-
      *>>   scale testing (e.g. genomics, many simultaneous hypothesis
      *>>     tests).
      *>> - BY (Benjamini-Yekutieli): controls the FDR under arbitrary
      *>>   dependence; more
      *>>   conservative than BH.
      *>> - None: returns the input p-values unchanged.
      *>>
      *>> As in R, a single p-value (n <= 1) is always returned
      *>>   unchanged regardless of
      *>> `method`.
       01 DM-P-ADJUST-2.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 2 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-P-ADJUST-2-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 1.
      *>> p (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-P-ADJUST-2-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> method (PAdjustMethod = BH): P-ADJUST-METHOD value (see the 88
      *>>   levels).
          05 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-BONFERRONI VALUE 0.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-HOLM VALUE 1.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-HOCHBERG VALUE 2.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-HOMMEL VALUE 3.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-BH VALUE 4.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-BY VALUE 5.
             88 DM-P-ADJUST-2-METHOD-NONE VALUE 6.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-P-ADJUST-2-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::westfall_young_adjust(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & sorted_p) -> std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_westfall_young_adjust" USING DM-WESTFALL-YOUNG-ADJUST
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Westfall-Young single-step minP adjustment (Westfall & Young,
      *>>   1993) -- a resampling-
      *>> based FWER correction that accounts for the actual dependence
      *>>   structure between
      *>> tests (unlike the analytic methods above, which either assume
      *>>   independence or make
      *>> no assumption at all).
      *>>
      *>> `resampled_p` is a B x m matrix: row b holds the m p-values
      *>>   recomputed on the
      *>> b-th permutation or bootstrap resample (generated by the
      *>>   caller -- this function is
      *>> purely the combinatorial engine, not the resampling scheme
      *>>   itself). `sorted_p` is
      *>> the m original p-values sorted ascending, and column k of
      *>>   every row in `resampled_p`
      *>> must correspond to the same hypothesis as sorted_p[k] (i.e.
      *>>   the caller sorts once and
      *>> reorders both consistently before calling).
      *>>
      *>> Single-step: every hypothesis is compared against the same
      *>>   reference distribution --
      *>> the per-resample minimum p-value taken over *all* m hypotheses
      *>>   -- so adjusted p[k] =
      *>> (fraction of resamples whose overall minimum p-value <=
      *>>   sorted_p[k]). This is
      *>> automatically monotonic in sorted_p and valid under subset
      *>>   pivotality, but less
      *>> powerful than the step-down refinement below since it never
      *>>   narrows the comparison
      *>> set as hypotheses are resolved.
      *>>
      *>> Returned in the same (ascending sorted_p) order as the input;
      *>>   the caller is
      *>> responsible for mapping back to original hypothesis order if
      *>>   needed.
       01 DM-WESTFALL-YOUNG-ADJUST.
      *>> resampled_p (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-WESTFALL-YOUNG-ADJUST-RESAMPLED-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> sorted_p (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-WESTFALL-YOUNG-ADJUST-SORTED-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-WESTFALL-YOUNG-ADJUST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::romano_wolf_adjust(const
      *>>   std::vector<std::vector<double>> & resampled_p, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & sorted_p) -> std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_romano_wolf_adjust" USING DM-ROMANO-WOLF-ADJUST
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Romano-Wolf step-down resampling adjustment (Romano & Wolf,
      *>>   2005) -- like
      *>> westfall_young_adjust, but more powerful: at each step k (in
      *>>   ascending sorted_p
      *>> order), the reference distribution is the per-resample minimum
      *>>   p-value taken only
      *>> over the *remaining* hypotheses k..m-1, narrowing by one
      *>>   hypothesis at each step
      *>> rather than always using all m. This is the step-down
      *>>   generalization that controls
      *>> FWER without requiring subset pivotality. Monotonicity
      *>>   (adjusted p[k] >= adjusted
      *>> p[k-1]) is enforced explicitly, since narrowing the comparison
      *>>   set at each step means
      *>> it isn't automatic the way it is for the single-step procedure
      *>>   above.
      *>>
      *>> Same input/output conventions as westfall_young_adjust:
      *>>   `resampled_p` is a B x m
      *>> matrix with column k aligned to sorted_p[k], and the result is
      *>>   returned in the same
      *>> sorted order.
       01 DM-ROMANO-WOLF-ADJUST.
      *>> resampled_p (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ROMANO-WOLF-ADJUST-RESAMPLED-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> sorted_p (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
          05 DM-ROMANO-WOLF-ADJUST-SORTED-P USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it).
          05 DM-ROMANO-WOLF-ADJUST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(std::size_t
      *>>   successes, std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative
      *>>   alternative = TwoSided, bool correct = true, double
      *>>   conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_proportion_test_one_sample" USING
      *>>   DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> One-proportion score test with an uncorrected Wilson
      *>>   confidence interval.
      *>>
      *>> Tests a fixed Bernoulli success probability using the signed
      *>>   null-standard-error z statistic;
      *>> statistic stores z^2 but the chosen tail uses its sign. The
      *>>   optional continuity correction
      *>> affects the test only: its Wilson interval is uncorrected and
      *>>   need not invert that test.
      *>> Approximation requires sufficiently populated success/failure
      *>>   counts. O(1) work/storage, no
      *>> shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_one_sample(45,100);
      *>> std::print("{:.2f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.45
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid counts, null probability,
      *>>   alternative or confidence level.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 6 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> successes (std::size_t): unsigned long. Integer successes in
      *>>   [0,n].
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-SUCCESSES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive independent-trial
      *>>   count.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> p (double = 0.5): double. Finite null probability strictly in
      *>>   (0,1), default .5.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-P USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true
      *>>   probability relative to p.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED
                 VALUE 0.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> correct (bool = true): 0 or 1. True (default) applies the
      *>>   capped half-count correction to z.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-CORRECT USAGE BINARY-CHAR
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=z^2, parameter1=1,
      *>>   estimate1=successes/n, p_value and uncorrected Wilson
      *>>   confidence bounds.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(std::size_t
      *>>   successes1, std::size_t n1, std::size_t successes2,
      *>>   std::size_t n2, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, bool
      *>>   correct = true, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_proportion_test_two_sample" USING
      *>>   DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Pooled score test for two proportions, with an unpooled
      *>>   difference interval.
      *>>
      *>> Tests equality using the pooled null SE and optionally a
      *>>   capped continuity correction. The
      *>> difference CI uses an unpooled Wald SE and an uncapped
      *>>   half-count width correction when
      *>> requested; it is approximate and is not a Wilson/Newcombe
      *>>   interval. All-zero/all-one pooled
      *>> samples return z=0,p=1. O(1) work/storage, no shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto p=dm::stats::proportion_test_two_sample(2,10,3,10);
      *>> std::print("{:.1f}\n",p.estimate1); // 0.2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/prop.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid counts, alternative or
      *>>   confidence level.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 4 to 7 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 4.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED VALUE 4.
      *>> successes1 (std::size_t): unsigned long. First success count
      *>>   in [0,n1].
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-SUCCESSES1 USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> n1 (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive first
      *>>   independent-trial count.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-N1 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> successes2 (std::size_t): unsigned long. Second success count
      *>>   in [0,n2].
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-SUCCESSES2 USAGE
              BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> n2 (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive independent
      *>>   second-trial count.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-N2 USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   p1-p2.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED
                 VALUE 0.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> correct (bool = true): 0 or 1. True (default) corrects the
      *>>   score test and adds a half-count CI width.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-CORRECT USAGE BINARY-CHAR
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=z^2, parameter1=1,
      *>>   estimates=sample proportions, p_value and confidence bounds
      *>>   for p1-p2, clamped to [-1,1].
          05 DM-PROPORTION-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::binomial_test(std::size_t successes,
      *>>   std::size_t n, double p = 0.5, Alternative alternative =
      *>>   TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_binomial_test" USING DM-BINOMIAL-TEST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Exact binomial test with Clopper-Pearson confidence bounds,
      *>>   like R binom.test().
      *>>
      *>> One-sided p-values are inclusive tails. Two-sided p-values sum
      *>>   PMFs no larger than the
      *>> observed PMF, using relative comparison tolerance 1e-7. The
      *>>   equal-tailed Clopper-Pearson
      *>> interval has at-least-nominal coverage but need not invert the
      *>>   probability-ordered two-sided
      *>> test. Null endpoints zero/one are supported. Two-sided
      *>>   enumeration costs O(n), intervals use
      *>> bounded beta-CDF bisection; O(1) auxiliary storage, no shared
      *>>   state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto b=dm::stats::binomial_test(9,10);
      *>> std::print("{:.6f}\n",b.p_value); // 0.021484
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/binom.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid counts, probability,
      *>>   alternative or confidence level.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> successes (std::size_t): unsigned long. Integer observed
      *>>   successes in [0,n].
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-SUCCESSES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> n (std::size_t): unsigned long. Positive independent Bernoulli
      *>>   trial count.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-N USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> p (double = 0.5): double. Finite null probability in [0,1],
      *>>   default .5.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-P USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for true
      *>>   success probability.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=successes, parameter1=n,
      *>>   estimate1=successes/n, p_value and Clopper-Pearson interval;
      *>>   one-sided intervals end at zero/one.
          05 DM-BINOMIAL-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::t_test_one_sample(const std::vector<double>
      *>>   & x, double mu = 0.0, Alternative alternative = TwoSided,
      *>>   double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_t_test_one_sample" USING DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> One-sample Student t-test of a population mean, like R
      *>>   t.test().
      *>>
      *>> Independent normal observations give t=(mean-mu)/SE with n-1
      *>>   df. The CI estimates the
      *>> population mean, not mean-mu. Inputs are copied only for
      *>>   paired differences; all routines
      *>> leave inputs unchanged and use no shared mutable state. O(n)
      *>>   work; no null/nonfinite omission.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 3
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid
      *>>   mu/alternative/confidence or zero/nonfinite standard error.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              1.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite observations with positive representable
      *>>   sample variance.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> mu (double = 0.0): double. Finite null mean, default zero, in
      *>>   observation units.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-MU USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater,
      *>>   relative to mu.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level strictly in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=t, parameter1=df,
      *>>   estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence bounds;
      *>>   remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have
      *>>   one infinite endpoint.
          05 DM-T-TEST-ONE-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::t_test_two_sample(const std::vector<double>
      *>>   & x, const std::vector<double> & y, bool equal_variance =
      *>>   false, Alternative alternative = TwoSided, double conf_level
      *>>   = 0.94999999999999996) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_t_test_two_sample" USING DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Independent two-sample Welch or pooled Student mean test, like
      *>>   R t.test().
      *>>
      *>> Welch uses unequal sample variances and Satterthwaite
      *>>   approximate df; normality alone does not
      *>> make its reference exact. Pooled Student requires equal
      *>>   population variances and independent
      *>> normal observations for its exact reference. CI estimates
      *>>   mean(x)-mean(y). O(n_x+n_y) work,
      *>> inputs unchanged, no shared mutable state or omission.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_two_sample({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.estimate1); // 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Invalid sample sizes/values,
      *>>   alternative/confidence, unrepresentable mean difference or
      *>>   zero/nonfinite combined standard error.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite observations in the first group.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite independent observations in the second
      *>>   group.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> equal_variance (bool = false): 0 or 1. False (default) uses
      *>>   Welch; true uses pooled variance.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-EQUAL-VARIANCE USAGE BINARY-CHAR
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   mean(x)-mean(y).
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=t, parameter1=df,
      *>>   estimate1/2=group means, p_value and confidence bounds for
      *>>   their difference. One-sided bounds can be infinite.
          05 DM-T-TEST-TWO-SAMPLE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::t_test_paired(const std::vector<double> & x,
      *>>   const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative =
      *>>   TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_t_test_paired" USING DM-T-TEST-PAIRED DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Paired Student t-test on aligned differences, like R
      *>>   t.test(paired=TRUE).
      *>>
      *>> Runs the one-sample test on x[i]-y[i]. Differences, rather
      *>>   than the two marginal samples, need
      *>> independent normal sampling for the exact t reference. Row
      *>>   order defines pairing; no values
      *>> are omitted. O(n) work/storage; inputs unchanged, no shared
      *>>   mutable state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_paired({2,4,7},{1,2,4});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 2
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Length mismatch, nonfinite
      *>>   data/differences, invalid controls or zero/nonfinite
      *>>   difference standard error.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite first measurements.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Equal-length finite second measurements paired by index.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the
      *>>   population mean difference.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). One-sample difference result with method
      *>>   Paired t-test; estimate1 is mean difference, CI targets the
      *>>   same difference.
          05 DM-T-TEST-PAIRED-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::f_test_variance(const std::vector<double> &
      *>>   x, const std::vector<double> & y, Alternative alternative =
      *>>   TwoSided, double conf_level = 0.94999999999999996) ->
      *>>   HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_f_test_variance" USING DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R
      *>>   var.test().
      *>>
      *>> Uses sample variance(x)/sample variance(y). Independent normal
      *>>   populations give the
      *>> F(df_x,df_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is
      *>>   not a gatekeeper for choosing
      *>> Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n_x+n_y) work/O(1) auxiliary
      *>>   storage; inputs unchanged, no
      *>> omission/shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
      *>> std::print("{:.2f}\n",f.estimate1); // 0.25
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Too few/nonfinite observations,
      *>>   nonpositive/nonfinite variances or invalid controls.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite first-sample values with positive finite
      *>>   sample variance.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. At
      *>>   least two finite independent second-sample values with
      *>>   positive finite sample variance.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   population variance ratio relative to one.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
             88 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> conf_level (double = 0.94999999999999996): double. Finite
      *>>   confidence level in (0,1), default .95.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-CONF-LEVEL USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=estimate1=variance ratio,
      *>>   parameter1/2=sample sizes minus one, p_value and ratio
      *>>   confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or
      *>>   infinity.
          05 DM-F-TEST-VARIANCE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, double mu = 0.0, Alternative
      *>>   alternative = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_wilcoxon_signed_rank_test" USING
      *>>   DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Normal-approximation Wilcoxon signed-rank test with
      *>>   tie/continuity corrections.
      *>>
      *>> Tests symmetry of x-mu about zero under independent
      *>>   observations, not an arbitrary-population
      *>> mean or median null. For paired data pass aligned differences.
      *>>   Exact-zero differences are
      *>> removed; absolute differences receive average ranks. Always
      *>>   uses a normal approximation, even
      *>> for small tie-free samples; no exact option or CI. O(n log n)
      *>>   work/O(n) storage; input
      *>> unchanged, no shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_signed_rank_test({1,2,3,4});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 10
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Nonfinite input/mu, invalid
      *>>   alternative, no nonzero differences or zero rank variance.
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST.
      *>> Arguments to send: 1 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 1.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 1.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Finite
      *>>   values; at least one nonzero difference from mu must remain.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> mu (double = 0.0): double. Finite symmetry centre, default
      *>>   zero.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-MU USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for the
      *>>   signed location shift.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE
                 0.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE
                 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=positive rank sum V, estimate1=mu
      *>>   (not an estimated pseudomedian), approximate p_value; no CI.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-SIGNED-RANK-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test(const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & x, const std::vector<double> & y,
      *>>   Alternative alternative = TwoSided) -> HypothesisTestResult
      *>> CALL "dm_wilcoxon_rank_sum_test" USING
      *>>   DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Tie/continuity-corrected Mann-Whitney U test using a normal
      *>>   approximation.
      *>>
      *>> Pools ranks, giving average ranks to ties, and reports U for
      *>>   the first sample. Its identical-
      *>> distribution null requires independent exchangeable samples. A
      *>>   common-shape shift model
      *>> supports a location interpretation; equal medians alone is
      *>>   insufficient. U/(n_x*n_y) estimates
      *>> P(X>Y)+P(X=Y)/2, not a mean difference. Approximation is used
      *>>   at every size. O(N log N)
      *>> work/O(N) storage, inputs unchanged, no shared state.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto
      *>>   w=dm::stats::wilcoxon_rank_sum_test({1,2,3},{4,5,6});
      *>> std::print("{:.0f}\n",w.statistic); // 0
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> -
      *>>   https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: Empty/nonfinite samples, invalid
      *>>   alternative or zero rank variance (all pooled observations
      *>>   tied).
      *>>
      *>> See also: HypothesisTestResult
       01 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST.
      *>> Arguments to send: 2 to 3 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 2.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED
              VALUE 2.
      *>> x (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite first sample.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-X USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> y (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonempty finite independent second sample.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-Y USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> alternative (Alternative = TwoSided): ALTERNATIVE value (see
      *>>   the 88 levels). TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for
      *>>   first-versus-second stochastic location.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-ALTERNATIVE USAGE BINARY-LONG
              SIGNED.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-TWO-SIDED VALUE 0.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-LESS VALUE 1.
             88 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-ALTERNATIVE-GREATER VALUE 2.
      *>> Result (HypothesisTestResult): HYPOTHESIS-TEST-RESULT handle
      *>>   (new; close it). statistic=U=rank_sum(x)-n_x(n_x+1)/2 and
      *>>   approximate p_value; no CI or location estimate.
          05 DM-WILCOXON-RANK-SUM-TEST-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::bs(double lower, double upper, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & knots, std::size_t degree = 3, bool
      *>>   intercept = false) -> Bspline
      *>> CALL "dm_bs" USING DM-BS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Construct an owned B-spline basis for repeated evaluation with
      *>>   eval().
      *>> Free-function spelling of Bspline: construction fixes the
      *>>   knots and boundaries
      *>> without evaluating a design matrix or fitting a model. The
      *>>   returned value owns
      *>> its basis; the input knot vector may be modified or destroyed
      *>>   after this call.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> const auto basis = dm::bs(0, 1, dm::seql(0, 1, 3));
      *>> const auto design = basis.eval(dm::c(0, 0.5, 1));
      *>> std::print("{} {}\n", design.rows(), design.cols()); // 3 4
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: For invalid boundaries, knots or
      *>>   overflowing degree.
      *>>
      *>> See also: Bspline, Bspline::eval()
       01 DM-BS.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 5 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-BS-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE 3.
      *>> lower (double): double. Finite left boundary, strictly below
      *>>   upper.
          05 DM-BS-LOWER USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> upper (double): double. Finite right boundary.
          05 DM-BS-UPPER USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> knots (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Finite, strictly increasing breakpoints in [lower, upper].
      *>>   Boundary entries are optional; an empty vector creates a
      *>>   basis without interior knots.
          05 DM-BS-KNOTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> degree (std::size_t = 3): unsigned long. Polynomial degree,
      *>>   default 3 (cubic).
          05 DM-BS-DEGREE USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> intercept (bool = false): 0 or 1. Keep the first basis column,
      *>>   default false. Leave false with an intercept formula such as
      *>>   `y ~ .`; use a no-intercept formula when true.
          05 DM-BS-INTERCEPT USAGE BINARY-CHAR UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (Bspline): BSPLINE handle (new; close it). Owned
      *>>   Bspline with the same degree, intercept policy and
      *>>   validation as its constructor. Call eval() on training and
      *>>   prediction coordinates.
          05 DM-BS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::mkdir(const std::filesystem::path & path) -> bool
      *>> CALL "dm_mkdir" USING DM-MKDIR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Create a directory and any missing parent directories.
      *>> Accepts native filesystem paths, strings and string literals.
      *>>   An existing
      *>> directory is a successful no-op; an existing file at the
      *>>   target or along its
      *>> parents is an error. Relative paths use the current working
      *>>   directory, which
      *>> this function does not change. Permissions follow the native
      *>>   filesystem and
      *>> process defaults. A failed recursive operation may leave
      *>>   created parents.
      *>> This C++ convenience forwards to
      *>>   std::filesystem::create_directories().
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> dm::mkdir("build/figs");
      *>> std::print("{}\n",
      *>>   std::filesystem::is_directory("build/figs")); // true
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::filesystem::filesystem_error: For an empty path, a
      *>>   conflicting file, insufficient permissions or other
      *>>   operating-system filesystem errors.
      *>>
      *>> See also: std::filesystem::create_directories()
       01 DM-MKDIR.
      *>> path (const std::filesystem::path &): text in PIC X(256);
      *>>   -LENGTH -1 trims trailing spaces. Directory to create,
      *>>   including any missing parents; must not be empty.
          05 DM-MKDIR-PATH PIC X(256).
          05 DM-MKDIR-PATH-LENGTH USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE -1.
      *>> Result (bool): 0 or 1. True if the target directory was
      *>>   created, false if it already existed.
          05 DM-MKDIR-RESULT USAGE BINARY-CHAR UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::hello() -> void
      *>> CALL "dm_hello" USING DM-HELLO DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Prints a hello message to standard output.
       01 DM-HELLO.
          05 FILLER PIC X.

      *>> datamunge::call_with_callback(double x, Callback * cb) ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_call_with_callback" USING DM-CALL-WITH-CALLBACK
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Invokes a callback with the given value.
       01 DM-CALL-WITH-CALLBACK.
      *>> x (double): double. Input value passed to the callback.
          05 DM-CALL-WITH-CALLBACK-X USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> cb (Callback *): CALLBACK handle, 0 for null. Pointer to a
      *>>   `Callback` instance; must not be null.
          05 DM-CALL-WITH-CALLBACK-CB USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Result of `cb->call(x)`.
          05 DM-CALL-WITH-CALLBACK-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::map_dvector_with_callback(const std::vector<double>
      *>>   & values, Callback * cb) -> std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_map_dvector_with_callback" USING
      *>>   DM-MAP-DVECTOR-WITH-CALLBACK DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Applies a callback to every element of a vector.
       01 DM-MAP-DVECTOR-WITH-CALLBACK.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Source vector of doubles.
          05 DM-MAP-DVECTOR-WITH-CALLBACK-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> cb (Callback *): CALLBACK handle, 0 for null. Pointer to a
      *>>   `Callback` instance; must not be null.
          05 DM-MAP-DVECTOR-WITH-CALLBACK-CB USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it). New vector where each element is the result of
      *>>   `cb->call(v)` for the corresponding element `v` in `values`.
          05 DM-MAP-DVECTOR-WITH-CALLBACK-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::make_dvector(double a, double b, double c) ->
      *>>   std::vector<double>
      *>> CALL "dm_make_dvector" USING DM-MAKE-DVECTOR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Constructs a three-element vector from individual values.
       01 DM-MAKE-DVECTOR.
      *>> a (double): double. First element.
          05 DM-MAKE-DVECTOR-A USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> b (double): double. Second element.
          05 DM-MAKE-DVECTOR-B USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> c (double): double. Third element.
          05 DM-MAKE-DVECTOR-C USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::vector<double>): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle (new; close
      *>>   it). `std::vector<double>{a`, b, c}.
          05 DM-MAKE-DVECTOR-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::sum_dvector(const std::vector<double> & values) ->
      *>>   double
      *>> CALL "dm_sum_dvector" USING DM-SUM-DVECTOR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Computes the sum of all elements in a vector.
       01 DM-SUM-DVECTOR.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Vector of doubles to sum.
          05 DM-SUM-DVECTOR-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (double): double. Sum of all elements, or 0.0 if the
      *>>   vector is empty.
