      *>> datamunge::make_dpair(double a, double b) -> std::pair<double,
      *>>   double>
      *>> CALL "dm_make_dpair" USING DM-MAKE-DPAIR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Constructs a pair of doubles.
       01 DM-MAKE-DPAIR.
      *>> a (double): double. First element.
          05 DM-MAKE-DPAIR-A USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> b (double): double. Second element.
          05 DM-MAKE-DPAIR-B USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (std::pair<double, double>): -FIRST: double; -SECOND:
      *>>   double. `std::pair<double`, double>{a, b}.
          05 DM-MAKE-DPAIR-RESULT-FIRST USAGE COMP-2.
          05 DM-MAKE-DPAIR-RESULT-SECOND USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::sum_dpair(const std::pair<double, double> & values)
      *>>   -> double
      *>> CALL "dm_sum_dpair" USING DM-SUM-DPAIR DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Computes the sum of both elements in a pair.
       01 DM-SUM-DPAIR.
      *>> values (const std::pair<double, double> &): -FIRST: double;
      *>>   -SECOND: double. Pair of doubles.
          05 DM-SUM-DPAIR-VALUES-FIRST USAGE COMP-2.
          05 DM-SUM-DPAIR-VALUES-SECOND USAGE COMP-2.
      *>> Result (double): double. `values.first` + values.second.
          05 DM-SUM-DPAIR-RESULT USAGE COMP-2.

      *>> datamunge::fda::refine_basis(const BSplineBasis & basis, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & inserted_knots) -> BSplineRefinement
      *>> CALL "dm_refine_basis" USING DM-REFINE-BASIS DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Inserts knots into a clamped B-spline basis (Oslo algorithm).
      *>>
      *>> Builds the refined knot vector (old knots merged with
      *>>   `inserted_knots`) and the sparse
      *>> discrete-B-spline operator that re-expresses every coarse
      *>>   spline on it exactly. Each
      *>> row has at most degree + 1 nonzeros and the work is O(m p^2)
      *>>   for m refined functions.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto coarse = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2); //
      *>>   knots 0 0 1 1
      *>> auto r = dm::fda::refine_basis(coarse, {0.25});
      *>> std::print("{}\n", r.apply({0.0, 4.0})); // [0, 1, 4]
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete
      *>>   B-splines and subdivision
      *>>   techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer
      *>>     graphics. Computer Graphics
      *>>   and Image Processing 14(2), 87-111.
      *>>     https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4
      *>> - W. Boehm (1980). Inserting new knots into B-spline curves.
      *>>   Computer-Aided Design
      *>>   12(4), 199-201. https://doi.org/10.1016/0010-4485(80)90154-2
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the basis is not clamped, a knot
      *>>   is outside the open domain or non-finite, or a multiplicity
      *>>   would exceed degree + 1.
      *>>
      *>> See also: dyadic_refine()
       01 DM-REFINE-BASIS.
      *>> basis (const BSplineBasis &): B-SPLINE-BASIS handle. Basis to
      *>>   refine; its knot vector must be clamped (first and last
      *>>   knots repeated degree + 1 times, as produced by
      *>>   BSplineBasis::open_uniform()).
          05 DM-REFINE-BASIS-BASIS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> inserted_knots (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE
      *>>   handle. Knots to add, strictly inside the domain, in any
      *>>   order. Repeats are allowed while every knot multiplicity
      *>>   stays <= degree + 1.
          05 DM-REFINE-BASIS-INSERTED-KNOTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE
              UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (BSplineRefinement): B-SPLINE-REFINEMENT handle (new;
      *>>   close it). The refined basis and its refinement operator.
          05 DM-REFINE-BASIS-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fda::dyadic_refine(const BSplineBasis & basis) ->
      *>>   BSplineRefinement
      *>> CALL "dm_dyadic_refine" USING DM-DYADIC-REFINE DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Halves every knot span of a clamped basis (inserts each
      *>>   positive span's midpoint).
      *>>
      *>> This is the nested-space step between consecutive levels of
      *>>   HB/THB splines: the refined
      *>> space contains the coarse one, and the operator maps
      *>>   coefficients exactly.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto r =
      *>>   dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3,
      *>>   5));
      *>> std::print("{} -> {}\n", 5, r.basis.basis_size()); // 5 -> 7
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete
      *>>   B-splines and subdivision
      *>>   techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer
      *>>     graphics.
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: If the basis is not clamped.
      *>>
      *>> See also: refine_basis(), TensorBSpline::dyadic_refine()
       01 DM-DYADIC-REFINE.
      *>> basis (const BSplineBasis &): B-SPLINE-BASIS handle. Clamped
      *>>   basis to refine.
          05 DM-DYADIC-REFINE-BASIS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (BSplineRefinement): B-SPLINE-REFINEMENT handle (new;
      *>>   close it). The refined basis and its refinement operator.
          05 DM-DYADIC-REFINE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_bspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis>
      *>>   bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points,
      *>>   const std::vector<double> & values, const SplineSolveOptions
      *>>   & options = {}) -> TensorBSpline
      *>> CALL "dm_fit_tensor_bspline" USING DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Fits a tensor-product B-spline to scattered data by ordinary
      *>>   least squares.
      *>>
      *>> Builds the sparse design B (each row has at most prod(degree_k
      *>>   + 1) nonzeros), then
      *>> solves (B^T B) c = B^T y. Coordinates outside a basis domain
      *>>   are clamped to it.
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
      *>> auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_bspline({b}, {{0.0}, {0.5},
      *>>   {1.0}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0});
      *>> std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.25)); // 1.500
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - C. de Boor (2001). A Practical Guide to Splines, revised
      *>>   edition. Springer.
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: On empty or mismatched inputs, or
      *>>   non-finite data.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If the normal equations are singular
      *>>   (some basis function has too little data; use a penalized
      *>>   fit or fewer functions).
      *>>
      *>> See also: fit_tensor_pspline()
       01 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 3 to 4 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 3.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              3.
      *>> bases (std::vector<BSplineBasis>): VECTOR-B-SPLINE-BASIS
      *>>   handle. One basis per dimension.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-BASES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> points (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Sample locations, each of
      *>>   length bases.size().
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   One response per point.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> options (const SplineSolveOptions & = {}):
      *>>   SPLINE-SOLVE-OPTIONS handle. Solver settings (default:
      *>>   automatic).
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (TensorBSpline): TENSOR-B-SPLINE handle (new; close
      *>>   it). The fitted spline.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-BSPLINE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_pspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis>
      *>>   bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points,
      *>>   const std::vector<double> & values, const
      *>>   std::vector<double> & lambdas, int order = 2, const
      *>>   SplineSolveOptions & options = {}) -> TensorBSpline
      *>> CALL "dm_fit_tensor_pspline" USING DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE
      *>>   DM-STATUS
      *>>
      *>> Fits a tensor-product P-spline: least squares plus an
      *>>   anisotropic difference
      *>> penalty, solving (B^T B + P) c = B^T y with P from
      *>>   tensor_difference_penalty().
      *>>
      *>> All-zero lambdas reproduce fit_tensor_bspline(). Positive
      *>>   lambdas keep the system
      *>> well-posed even with fewer points than coefficients (for order
      *>>   0, or once the data
      *>> determine the order-q polynomial null space).
      *>>
      *>> **Example**
      *>>
      *>> ```cpp
      *>> auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 8);
      *>> auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_pspline({b}, {{0.0}, {1.0}},
      *>>   {0.0, 1.0}, {1e3});
      *>> std::print("{:.3f}\n", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 0.500
      *>> ```
      *>>
      *>> **References**
      *>>
      *>> - P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing
      *>>   with B-splines and penalties.
      *>>   https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
      *>> - P. H. C. Eilers, I. D. Currie and M. Durban (2006). Fast and
      *>>   compact smoothing on large
      *>>   multidimensional grids.
      *>>     https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csda.2004.07.008
      *>>
      *>> Errors (a non-zero status):
      *>> - std::invalid_argument: On empty/mismatched inputs, invalid
      *>>   lambdas, or an axis with no more functions than `order`.
      *>> - std::runtime_error: If the penalized normal equations are
      *>>   singular.
      *>>
      *>> See also: fit_tensor_bspline(), fit_pspline()
       01 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE.
      *>> Arguments to send: 4 to 6 (C++ defaults fill the rest);
      *>>   initially 4.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-GIVEN USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED VALUE
              4.
      *>> bases (std::vector<BSplineBasis>): VECTOR-B-SPLINE-BASIS
      *>>   handle. One basis per dimension.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-BASES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> points (const std::vector<std::vector<double>> &):
      *>>   VECTOR-VECTOR-DOUBLE handle. Sample locations, each of
      *>>   length bases.size().
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-POINTS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> values (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   One response per point.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-VALUES USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> lambdas (const std::vector<double> &): VECTOR-DOUBLE handle.
      *>>   Nonnegative per-axis smoothing weights, one per basis.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-LAMBDAS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> order (int = 2): int. Difference order (default 2, the
      *>>   conventional curvature penalty).
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-ORDER USAGE BINARY-LONG SIGNED.
      *>> options (const SplineSolveOptions & = {}):
      *>>   SPLINE-SOLVE-OPTIONS handle. Solver settings (default:
      *>>   automatic).
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-OPTIONS USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.
      *>> Result (TensorBSpline): TENSOR-B-SPLINE handle (new; close
      *>>   it). The fitted spline.
          05 DM-FIT-TENSOR-PSPLINE-RESULT USAGE BINARY-DOUBLE UNSIGNED.

      *>> datamunge::nn::nn_gemm_backend() -> std::string
